\h Exodus
\toc1 Exodus
\toc2 Exodus
\toc3 Exo.
\mt1 Exodus
\is1 Introduction
\ip The word \bk Exodus\bk* comes from the Greek word meaning ‘departure’ or ‘leaving’. Hebrew readers call this document ‘Shemōt’ which is the second word in the Hebrew and means ‘Names’ because it begins with a list of names. Some European and other translations call it ‘The second book of Mosheh/Moses’.
\ip It should be noted that ‘Mosheh’ is the best English transliteration of the name of the hero of this account. Sadly, he's commonly known in the English world as ‘Moses’, even though we have all the sounds in our language to be able to pronounce his name more accurately. Unfortunately, because the language of ‘the New Testament’ didn't have the ‘sh’ sound and had no letter for ‘h’, his Hebrew version of his Egyptian name is corrupted as it goes through Greek ‘Mōsaʸs’ and then Latin ‘Moyses’ and then first entered English in the 1300's as ‘Moises’. Now that we live in a modern era where we try to show more respect to the names of peoples from other cultures, the \em OET\em* encourages the same for Biblical characters (especially the major ones), hence ‘Mosheh’. (Maybe a little bit of relearning could demonstrate a lot of respect to the Jewish people whose ancestors are recorded in these accounts.)
\ip After God had used a famine to cause his people to go down into Egypt (Heb. Mitsrayim), this document records how they eventually become slaves, but then God works many miracles to enable them to leave again, and not only that, to be paid at the end for their work.
\ip This story can be divided into three main parts: 1. their slavery in Egypt, and how God called Mosheh (Moses), the child who had been adopted by an Egyptian princess, to lead them out of slavery; 2. how God assisted them when they were chased by the Egyptian army, then when they reached Mt. Sinai, how they were miraculously given food and drink there at the mountain; 3. how Yahweh revealed himself to Mosheh there at Mt. Sinai, and gave him instructions to guide their lives and their nation, and God's agreement with the people; 4. the ending is the construction of the sacred tent, complete with instructions about how they should worship.
\ip An overview would be how God rescued his people from slavery and established them as a nation that was hopeful of a good future.
\ip \em Mosheh\em* is the most important person in this account. He was chosen by God to lead his people out of slavery and right out of Egypt. The \em ten commands\em* are another well-known component of this document and can be found in chapter twenty.
\iot Main components of this account
\io1 Yisrael's descendants are driven out of Egypt \ior 1:1–15:21\ior*
\io2 a. The people are enslaved there in Egypt \ior 1:1-22\ior*
\io2 b. The birth and early life of Mosheh \ior 2:1–4:31\ior*
\io2 c. Far'oh (Pharaoh) king of Egypt stands strongly against Mosheh and Aharon taking the people out of Egypt \ior 5:1–11:10\ior*
\io2 d. God's messenger of death that ‘passed-over’, and the exit of Yisra'el's descendants from Egypt \ior 12:1–15:21\ior*
\io1 Leaving the Red Sea and going to Mt. Sinai \ior 15:22–18:27\ior*
\io1 God's instructions and the agreement \ior 19:1–24:18\ior*
\io1 The sacred tent and warnings concerning worshipping \ior 25:1–40:38\ior*
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\ie
\c 1
\s1 Yisra'el's descendants are driven into slavery
\rem /s1 The Israelites Are Treated Cruelly in Egypt; The Israelites Oppressed; The Israelites in Egypt
\p
\v 1 These¦25401 are¦25402 the names¦25402 of Yisra'el's¦25404 descendants who¦25405 accompanied Yacob¦25408 (Jacob) when he moved his¦25410 household¦25410 to Egypt¦25406:\x + \xo 1:1-4: \xt Gen 46:8-27.\x*
\v 2 Reuben¦25413, Simeon¦25414, Levi¦25415, Yehudah¦25416,
\v 3 Yissakar¦25418 (Issachar), Zebulun¦25419, Benyamin¦25420,
\v 4 Dan¦25423, Naftali¦25424, Gad¦25425, and Asher¦25426.
\v 5 \add At that time,\add* Yacob¦25435 had a total of seventy¦25436 descendants (including¦25438 Yosef¦25438 \add and his sons\add* who were \add already\add* in¦25440 Egypt¦25440.)
\p
\v 6 \add As the years progressed,\add* Yosef¦25443 and¦25442 his brothers¦25446 died¦25442—all of that¦25449 generation¦25448,
\v 7 \add but they'd had many children who went on to have their own children and grandchildren,\add* and so they became¦25456 a large and powerful group \add there in Egypt\add*.\x + \xo 1:7: \xt Acts 7:17.\x*
\rem /s1 The Israelites Are Oppressed
\p
\v 8 \add Eventually\add* a new¦25467 king¦25465 came to power in Egypt¦25470 \add !(Mitsrayim¦25470)\add* who¦25471 had never known¦25474 Yosef¦25477\x + \xo 1:8: \xt Acts 7:18.\x*
\v 9 and he¦25479 told his¦25482 people, “Listen¦25483, those descendants of Yisrael¦25486 \add !(Israel¦25486)\add* are becoming more numerous¦25487 and powerful¦25488 than¦25489 us¦25489.
\v 10 \add So\add*, let's¦25492 deal¦25492 wisely¦25492 with¦25511 them, in¦25511 case they \add continue to\add* multiply. Then if war¦25501 was to break out, they could easily take the other side and fight¦25509 against¦25511 us, and then leave our country.”\x + \xo 1:10: \xt Acts 7:19.\x*
\v 11 Then¦25517 they appointed¦25517 overseers¦25519 to work them in¦25523 slave¦25520 gangs¦25520, and using them as forced¦25523 labour¦25523, they built¦25524 the store¦25526 cities¦25525 at¦25523 Pithon¦25530 and Rameses¦25533 for Far'oh¦25527 (Pharaoh).
\v 12 \add However,\add* the more they oppressed them, the more they multiplied and spread¦25541 out, and \add the more\add* the Egyptians came¦25542 to dread¦25542 the Israelis¦25545.
\v 13 \add So they\add* made the¦25551 Israelis¦25552 work \add long, hard hours\add*,
\v 14 and they made¦25555 their¦25558 lives¦25558 bitter¦25555 with¦25559 the hard¦25560 slavery. \add They were forced to make\add* mortar¦25561 and bricks¦25562, as well as all kinds of slave labour¦25573 in¦25559 the fields¦25566—all the work¦25570 they had to do was \add strenuous and tiring\add*.
\p
\v 15 Then¦25577 the king¦25578 of Egypt¦25579 spoke to¦25580 Shifrah¦25585 and¦25586 Puah¦25588, the two Hebrew¦25581 midwives¦25580,
\v 16 saying¦25590, “When¦25590 you assist¦25591 the Hebrew¦25594 women¦25594 as midwives¦25591, \add as you sit there on your\add* stool, if you see¦25595 that it's a male, do \add something\add* so he'll die, but¦25590 if it's a female, then¦25590 let her live¦25609.”
\v 17 But¦25611 the midwives¦25612 \add were godly women\add* and didn't¦25616 do¦25617 according to what the Egyptian¦25622 king¦25621 \add had instructed\add* them, and they¦25617 let¦25623 the boys live¦25623.
\v 18 \add So\add* the king¦25629 called¦25628 the midwives¦25632 \add back\add*, and he¦25633 \add demanded\add*, “Why¦25631 have¦25636 you done¦25636 that¦25638 and let¦25639 \add their\add* sons live¦25639?”
\p
\v 19 “Because¦25649 the Hebrew¦25653 women aren't¦25650 like¦25651 Egyptian women,” the midwives \add ≈answered\add*. “They're \add more active\add* and¦25644 give¦25662 birth¦25662 before¦25658 the midwife¦25661 \add even\add* gets to¦25660 them¦25660.”
\v 20 \add So\add* God¦25665 rewarded the midwives¦25666, and \add meanwhile,\add* the people¦25668 \add continued to increase in number\add* and become¦25669 more powerful,
\v 21 and¦25672 because¦25673 the midwives¦25676 had \add demonstrated their desire to obey\add* God¦25677, he gave them¦25681 families \add of their own\add*.
\v 22 Then¦25684 Far'oh¦25685 commanded¦25684 all of¦25688 his¦25688 people¦25688, “You¦25694 \add all need to\add* toss¦25695 every¦25690 newborn boy into the river, but¦25684 you can let the girls live¦25699.”\x + \xo 1:22: \xt Acts 7:19.\x*
\c 2
\s1 The birth of Mosheh (Moses)
\rem /s1 Moses; The Birth of Moses; Birth and Youth of Moses
\p
\v 1 \add During this period,\add* a man¦25703 from the tribe of Levi¦25705 married a woman who was also a Levite,
\v 2 and she conceived¦25713 and gave¦25715 birth¦25715 to a son¦25716. When¦25718 she saw¦25717 that¦25719 he was a \add healthy\add* boy, she hid¦25723 him¦25723 away for three¦25724 months¦25725,\x + \xo 2:2: \xt Acts 7:20; Heb 11:23.\x*
\v 3 \add but\add* then¦25727 she wasn't able¦25729 to¦25731 hide¦25731 him¦25731 any longer. \add So\add* she got a basket made from \add woven\add* reeds and plastered it with¦25738 bitumen¦25738 and pitch¦25739. Then¦25727 she put¦25740 the baby in it, and floated \add the basket\add* in the reeds along the riverbank,
\v 4 \add leaving the baby's\add* sister¦25753 to watch from¦25754 a distance¦25754 to see¦25755 what¦25756 would happen to him¦25759.
\p
\v 5 \add After a while,\add* Far'oh's daughter¦25762 came down¦25761 to the river¦25774 to wash \add herself\add*, and \add she and\add* her young \add attendants\add* were¦25770 walking¦25770 along the \add riverbank\add*. She¦25775 saw¦25775 the container among the reeds¦25780 and sent¦25781 \add one of\add* her slave¦25784 women to get it¦25785.
\v 6 She opened¦25787 it and saw¦25788 the baby inside, and wow, he \add started\add* crying¦25795. She felt sorry¦25796 for him¦25788 and said¦25798, “This baby \add must be\add* one¦25799 of the Hebrew¦25800 children¦25799.”
\p
\v 7 Then¦25803 his¦25804 sister¦25804 \add approached and\add* asked \add the princess\add*, “Would¦25809 you like me to go¦25810 and find a Hebrew¦25816 woman¦25813 who'll be able to breastfeed the baby for¦25818 you?”
\p
\v 8 “\add Yes,\add* go¦25829,” answered Far'oh's daughter¦25826, and the girl¦25831 went¦25830 and got the baby's mother¦25835.
\v 9 “Take¦25843 this baby,” said¦25838 the \add princess\add*, “and breastfeed him for me¦25849, and I'll¦25851 pay¦25851 you \add for doing it\add*.” \add So\add* the woman¦25856 took¦25855 the baby and \add looked after\add* him.
\v 10 \add When\add* the boy had grown \add enough\add*, she brought¦25863 him \add back\add* to Far'oh's daughter¦25864 and he¦25867 became her¦25869 son¦25870. She¦25871 named¦25871 him ‘Mosheh¦25873’\f + \fr 2:10 \ft More familiar to most English readers as ‘Moses’ from the Greek ‘Μωσῆς’ (Mōsaʸs) but Greek doesn't have an ‘h’ or a ‘sh’ so by going through Greek we ended up with something quite different from his real name. However, English does have those sounds and letters, so there's no reason why we can't get this name correct.\f* \add (which means ‘pulled out’)\add* because¦25875 she said¦25874 that she'd \add plucked\add* him out of the \add river\add*.\x + \xo 2:10: \xt Acts 7:21.\x*
\s1 Mosheh escapes to Midyan
\rem /s1 Moses Escapes to Midian; Moses Flees to Midian
\p
\v 11 \add Later on\add* when Mosheh¦25886 was¦25881 fully grown¦25885, he went¦25887 out¦25887 to¦25888 visit \add the Hebrews\add* and saw their¦25892 \add forced labour\add*, and he saw an Egyptian man beating a Hebrew man—one of¦25900 his¦25900 own people.\x + \xo 2:11-14: \xt Acts 7:23-28.\x*
\v 12 \add Mosheh\add* looked¦25902 around to check that¦25906 no one was¦25907 watching, then he hit the Egyptian, \add killing him,\add* then he hid¦25913 his body in¦25914 the sand¦25914.
\v 13 The next day¦25917, he went¦25916 out¦25916 again and wow, two¦25920 Hebrew men¦25922 were¦25924 fighting each other, and he said¦25925 to the man in the wrong¦25926, “Why are¦25928 you¦25928 hitting your¦25929 \add fellow Hebrew\add*?”
\p
\v 14 “Who¦25932 made you the ruler and¦25931 judge¦25936 over¦25937 us¦25937?” \add the man replied\add*. “Are you \add planning\add* to kill¦25938 me like you killed¦25942 \add that\add* Egyptian?” Then¦25931 Mosheh¦25947 was¦25946 afraid¦25946 because he realised that what he'd done¦25933 had \add probably become widely\add* known¦25950.
\v 15 \add Indeed,\add* when Far'oh¦25954 heard about it¦25953, he \add ordered\add* Mosheh to be killed¦25960. \add So\add* Mosheh had to flee¦25964 from¦25966 \add the king\add* and he \add took off east\add* to live in¦25969 Midian¦25971 and he stayed near the well.\x + \xo 2:15: \xt Acts 7:29; Heb 11:27.\x*
\p
\v 16 The¦25984 priest¦25977 there in Midian¦25978 had seven¦25979 daughters¦25980, and they would come¦25981 to the well to draw¦25982 water¦25987 out and fill¦25983 up the troughs¦25986 there so their¦25989 father's¦25989 \add sheep and goats\add* could drink¦25986.
\v 17 Now¦25998 some male shepherds¦25992 came¦25991 along and \add started to\add* shoo their flock¦26000 away¦25993, but¦25991 Mosheh¦25995 got involved and helped them¦25996 so that their animals could drink.
\v 18 \add When\add* they¦26002 \add got home\add* to¦26003 their¦26006 father¦26006 Reuel¦26005, he¦26007 \add asked\add*, “How come¦26010 you¦26009 \add got home\add* so early today?”
\p
\v 19 “There was an Egyptian man¦26014,” they¦26013 \add ≈answered\add*, “who stood up for us against those \add other\add* shepherds¦26018. And¦26019 he¦26022 even drew¦26022 \add water\add* for us and¦26013 gave our flock¦26027 water to drink.”
\p
\v 20 “Where¦26033 is¦26033 he \add now\add*?” he \add asked them\add*. “What's¦26034 this—you mean you all just left¦26036 him there? Go and¦26029 get him \add so we can give him a meal\add*.”
\p
\v 21 \add Later it turned out that\add* Mosheh was¦26045 prepared to live with¦26048 the¦26050 man¦26050, and¦26051 \add in due course\add* he¦26051 gave¦26051 his¦26055 daughter¦26055 Zipporah¦26054 to Mosheh \add in marriage\add*.
\v 22 \add When\add* she¦26058 gave¦26058 birth¦26058 to a son¦26059, he named him ‘Gershom¦26064’ \add (which means ‘foreigner’)\add* because¦26065 he said¦26066, “I've¦26068 become¦26068 a foreigner \add living\add* in¦26069 a foreign¦26070 land¦26069.”
\p
\v 23 \add Eventually\add* Egypt's¦26079 king¦26078 died¦26077, \add but\add* the Israelis¦26083 groaned¦26080 from the slavery \add they were still under\add* and they cried¦26087 out¦26087, and their¦26089 cry¦26089 \add for freedom\add* from slavery went¦26088 up¦26088 to¦26090 God¦26092.
\v 24 \add He\add* heard their¦26101 groaning¦26101 and remembered¦26102 his¦26106 agreement¦26106 with Abraham¦26109, with Yitshak¦26112, and with Yacob¦26115,\x + \xo 2:24: \xt Gen 15:13-14.\x*
\v 25 and he \add looked down on\add* the¦26121 Israelis¦26122 and he \add was concerned about them\add*.
\c 3
\s1 God calls Mosheh from a burning bush
\rem /s1 God Calls Moses; Moses at the Burning Bush; Moses and the Burning Bush
\p
\v 1 \add One time\add* Mosheh¦26127 (Moses) was shepherding the flock of his¦26134 father-in-law¦26134 Yetro (or Jethro, the priest¦26135 at Midian¦26136), and he¦26137 led the flock beyond the wilderness¦26142 and came¦26143 to¦26144 a hill¦26146. (\add This was later known as\add* the mountain¦26146 of God¦26147 at Horev¦26148).
\v 2 \add While he was there.\add* Yahweh's¦26152 messenger¦26151 appeared¦26150 to him¦26153 in a flame¦26154 coming from¦26157 the middle¦26157 of¦26157 a bush, and¦26150 as \add Mosheh\add* looked, to his surprise \add he saw that\add* the bush was burning¦26162 in the fire¦26163 yet not¦26165 \add actually\add* being burnt up.\x + \xo 3:2-10: \xt Acts 7:30-34.\x*
\v 3 “I've got to go and¦26173 see¦26173 this¦26178 amazing sight¦26176,” Mosheh¦26169 said¦26168 \add to himself\add*. “How come that¦26178 bush¦26183 isn't¦26180 burning¦26182 up?”
\p
\v 4 When¦26185 Yahweh¦26186 saw¦26185 that¦26187 he'd left his path in order to look, God¦26192 called¦26190 to him¦26191 from¦26193 the middle¦26193 of¦26193 the bush¦26194, “Mosheh, Mosheh.”
\p “I'm here,” he \add ≈replied\add*.
\p
\v 5 “Don't¦26202 come any closer,” \add Yahweh\add* said¦26201. “Take¦26206 off¦26206 your sandals¦26208 because¦26211 the¦26212 place¦26212 where you're¦26214 standing¦26215 is¦26217 HOLY ground¦26217.
\v 6 I'm the god of your father¦26225 and the god of Abraham¦26227, Yitshak¦26229, and Yacob¦26231.” \add So\add* Mosheh¦26233 covered hid¦26232 his¦26234 face¦26234 because¦26235 he¦26236 was¦26236 scared to¦26238 look¦26237 at God¦26224.
\p
\v 7 “I've certainly noticed the suffering¦26262 of my people¦26249 in¦26251 Egypt¦26251,” Yahweh¦26243 \add continued\add*, “I've heard¦26255 their cries¦26254 as the \add slave drivers\add* oppress them—I'm unmistakingly aware of their pain.
\v 8 \add So\add* I've¦26264 come¦26264 down¦26264 to set them free from Egyptian¦26268 control and to bring¦26269 them up¦26269 from \add there\add* to a good¦26277 and wide land—a land flowing¦26282 with milk¦26283 and honey¦26284. \add It's currently\add* the place¦26287 of¦26287 the Canaanites¦26288, the Hittites¦26289, the Amorites¦26290, the Perizzites¦26291, the Hivites¦26292, and the Yebusites¦26293.
\v 9 \add Yes,\add* the cry¦26297 of the Israelis¦26300 has¦26301 now \add reached\add* me¦26302 and¦26303 what's more, I've¦26305 seen¦26305 \add how much\add* the Egyptians are¦26311 oppressing¦26308 them¦26312.
\v 10 \add So\add* go¦26315 now¦26314 and I'll¦26316 send¦26316 you¦26316 to¦26317 Far'oh¦26319 (Pharaoh) and bring¦26320 my¦26323 people—Yisra'el's¦26326 descendants—out from¦26327 Egypt¦26327.”
\p
\v 11 “Who am I, that I should go¦26337 to Far'oh¦26340,” Mosheh¦26330 \add asked\add* God¦26333, “and¦26341 that I should \add be the one to\add* bring¦26342 out the Israelis¦26346 out from¦26347 Egypt¦26347?”
\p
\v 12 “I'll¦26352 certainly be with¦26361 you,” \add God replied,\add* “and¦26349 this will be your sign¦26357 \add so that you'll know\add* that I have¦26361 sent¦26360 you: when¦26361 you bring¦26361 the people¦26364 out from¦26365 Egypt¦26365, you all will serve¦26366 God on¦26361 this \add very\add* hill¦26371.”
\rem /s1 The Divine Name Revealed
\p
\v 13 “\add But listen\add*,” Mosheh¦26375 told God¦26388, “\add If\add* I'm about¦26381 to go¦26381 to the¦26384 Israelis¦26385 and¦26374 if I told them, ‘Your¦26389 ancestors'¦26389 god¦26388 has¦26390 sent¦26390 me to you¦26391.’ then they'd \add ask\add* me, ‘\add Oh yeah,\add* what's¦26395 his¦26397 name¦26397?’ then what would I tell them?”\x + \xo 3:13: \xt Exo 6:2-3.\x*
\p
\v 14 “I AM WHO I AM,” God¦26403 told Mosheh¦26406. “You'll \add tell them this\add*: ‘I AM (Yahweh) sent¦26416 me¦26416 to¦26417 you¦26412.’ ”\x + \xo 3:14: \xt Rev 1:4,8.\x*
\v 15 Then¦26419 he¦26419 \add continued\add*, “This is what you \add need\add* to tell the Israelis¦26431: ‘Yahweh¦26432, the god of your ancestors¦26434, the god of Abraham¦26436, Yitshak¦26438, and Yacob¦26440, sent¦26441 me¦26441 to you. This is my name¦26445 forever, and this¦26447 is my memorial¦26448 throughout the generations¦26450.’
\v 16 Go¦26457 and gather¦26453 the Israeli¦26456 elders¦26456, and tell them¦26459, ‘Yahweh¦26460, the god of your ancestors¦26462, appeared¦26463 to me¦26464, the god of Abraham¦26466, Yitshak¦26467, and Yacob¦26468, to say¦26469, “I've¦26471 certainly noticed you all and \add how you're all being treated\add* in¦26477 Egypt¦26477,
\v 17 and I've¦26480 promised¦26479 to take you¦26481 all from¦26482 your mistreatment in Egypt¦26483, away to the land where the Canaanites¦26487, Hittites¦26488, Amorites¦26489, Perizzites¦26490, Hivites¦26491, and the Yebusites¦26492 \add are currently living\add*—to a land flowing¦26496 with milk¦26497 and honey¦26498.” ’
\p
\v 18 Then¦26500 \add the people\add* will listen¦26500 to what you tell them, and you and the Israeli¦26504 elders¦26504 will go to the Egyptian¦26509 king¦26508 and you'll all tell him¦26511, ‘Yahweh, the god of¦26513 the Hebrews¦26514, has¦26515 met¦26515 with¦26524 us. \add So\add* now¦26517, please¦26520 let¦26518 us go three¦26522 days' journey¦26521 into the wilderness¦26524, so¦26525 we¦26525 can sacrifice¦26525 to Yahweh, our god.’
\v 19 \add But\add* I know¦26530 that¦26531 the Egyptian¦26537 king¦26536 won't¦26532 let you¦26535 go¦26538 \add until he's forced to\add*.
\v 20 \add So I will use force\add* and I'll¦26554 strike¦26547 Egypt¦26550 with¦26551 \add several supernatural acts\add*, and after¦26556 those, he'll let¦26559 you¦26560 all go¦26559.
\p
\v 21 Then¦26562 I'll¦26562 \add make\add* the Egyptian¦26570 \add people favour you all in such a way that\add* when you go, you \add won't leave empty-handed\add*.\x + \xo 3:21-22: \xt Exo 12:35-36.\x*
\v 22 \add For example,\add* an \add Israeli\add* woman¦26579 will¦26578 ask¦26578 her¦26580 \add Egyptian\add* neighbour¦26580 (or from someone staying \add where she lives\add*) for gold¦26587 and silver¦26585 items and for clothing¦26588. Then¦26578 you'll put¦26589 them¦26589 on¦26590 your sons¦26592 and daughters¦26595, and \add in this way\add* you'll all plunder¦26596 the Egyptians.”
\c 4
\s1 God gives Mosheh three miracles
\rem /s1 Signs for Moses; Moses' Miraculous Power; Signs of the Lord's Power; God Gives Moses Miraculous Power
\p
\v 1 “But¦26601 listen,” Mosheh¦26602 responded¦26603, “they won't believe¦26607 me¦26608 and¦26601 won't listen to \add what I say\add*, because¦26612 they'll say, ‘Yahweh¦26618 never showed himself to you¦26617.’ ”
\v 2 “\add Well,\add* what's that in¦26626 your¦26626 hand¦26626?” Yahweh¦26622 \add asked\add*.
\p “\add My shepherd's\add* staff¦26628,” he said.
\v 3 “Throw¦26631 it on the ground,” \add Yahweh\add* said¦26630. \add So Mosheh\add* he threw¦26633 it onto the ground and it became a snake¦26636, and \add he quickly moved back away from it\add*.
\v 4 “Pick it up by its tail.” Yahweh¦26642 told him¦26653. \add So Mosheh\add* picked it up by the tail and¦26641 it became a staff¦26655 in¦26649 his¦26656 hand¦26651 \add again\add*.
\v 5 “That's so that they'll believe¦26659 that \add I\add* appeared¦26662 to¦26663 you¦26663: Yahweh¦26664—the god of their¦26666 ancestors¦26666—the god of Abraham¦26668, Yitshak¦26670, and¦26671 Yacob¦26672.”
\p
\v 6 “\add Now\add* put¦26678 your hand \add on your chest—\add*inside your robe.” Yahweh¦26675 added. \add So Mosheh did that\add*, and¦26674 when he¦26686 pulled it out¦26686 again¦26677, his¦26688 hand had gone \add totally\add* white with¦26682 disease.
\v 7 Then¦26692 Yahweh said¦26692, “\add Now, put\add* your hand¦26694 \add back in again\add*.” \add So Mosheh did it again,\add* and¦26692 when he pulled it out¦26703, wow, it had¦26707 \add returned to normal\add*.
\v 8 “\add So,\add* if they don't¦26713 believe you, and¦26710 \add if the first miracle doesn't convince them\add*, then¦26710 they'll believe \add that second miracle\add*.
\v 9 And then, even if¦26727 they¦26730 don't¦26729 believe those two¦26732 miracles and \add won't believe what you tell them\add*, then you should take¦26738 some¦26739 water from the river and pour¦26741 it onto the dry land. Then¦26726 the water which¦26745 came from the river will¦26750 \add turn into\add* blood¦26751 \add there\add* on¦26752 the dry land.”
\p
\v 10 “Oh my¦26760 master¦26760,” Mosheh¦26755 \add ≈responded¦26754\add*, “I'm not¦26761 \add very good at speaking to others—I never have been and still aren't. I seem to get tongue-tied easily\add*.”
\p
\v 11 “Who \add was it that made people's mouths\add*?” Yahweh said¦26784. “Who makes \add someone\add* mute or deaf¦26797, or seeing¦26799 or blind¦26801? Isn't it me, Yahweh?
\v 12 \add So\add* get going¦26807 now¦26806, and I'll¦26809 \add look after\add* your¦26812 mouth¦26812 and \add show\add* you what you to say¦26815.”
\p
\v 13 \add But Mosheh replied\add*, “Oh master¦26819, please¦26822 send \add someone else\add*.”
\p
\v 14 Then¦26827 Yahweh¦26830 got angry with¦26831 Mosheh¦26831 and told him, “Isn't Aharon¦26834 (Aaron) a fellow Levite¦26836 and your brother¦26835? I know¦26837 that he can speak¦26840 well, and \add what's\add* more, he'll come¦26847 \add from Egypt\add* to meet¦26848 you, and \add when he does, he'll be very happy\add*.
\v 15 \add He'll become your speaker\add* and you'll be able to talk to him and \add tell him what to say\add*. I'll be \add helping both of you speak\add* and I'll teach¦26868 you both what you should do¦26872.
\v 16 \add So in that way,\add* he'll be like a mouth¦26886 for you and speak¦26874 to the¦26880 people¦26880 for you, and you'll be like God¦26891 to him¦26890.
\v 17 \add In addition,\add* you'll have that¦26899 staff¦26895 with¦26898 you in¦26898 order to do¦26900 those miracles.”
\s1 Mosheh returns to Egypt
\rem /s1 Moses Returns to Egypt
\p
\v 18 Then Mosheh¦26909 went¦26908 back to his¦26915 father-in-law¦26915 Yetro (Jethro), and told him¦26917, “Can I please¦26919 leave so¦26920 I can return¦26920 to my \add relatives\add* in Egypt¦26926 and see¦26927 whether they're still¦26928 alive.”
\p “Go¦26933 in¦26926 peace¦26934,” Yetro \add ≈answered\add*.
\p
\v 19 \add Meanwhile\add* Yahweh¦26937 had¦26947 told Mosheh¦26940 in¦26941 Midian¦26941, “Leave here and¦26936 return¦26943 to Egypt¦26944 because¦26945 all¦26948 those who¦26951 \add wanted to kill you\add* are \add now\add* dead.”
\v 20 Then¦26956 Mosheh took his¦26960 wife¦26960 and sons¦26963 and put them on¦26965 a donkey¦26967 and returned¦26968 towards¦26969 Egypt¦26970 \add with them\add*, and Mosheh took the staff¦26975 \add that God had turned into a snake\add*.
\p
\v 21 \add On the way,\add* Yahweh¦26980 said¦26979 to Mosheh¦26983, “When¦26979 you return¦26985 to Egypt¦26986, all¦26988 those miracles that I gave you, you'll be able to \add demonstrate\add* them in¦26984 front¦26996 of Far'oh¦26997 (Pharaoh), but¦26979 as for me, I'm going to \add make him stubborn so that\add* he¦27004 won't¦27003 let¦27004 \add your\add* people¦27007 leave.
\v 22 \add But\add* tell Far'oh¦27012, ‘Yahweh¦27015 has stated that Yisra'el¦27018 is¦27016 \add like\add* his oldest son¦27016
\v 23 and¦27020 he asked you to let that son go so that he can serve¦27026 him¦27028. But¦27020 you refused¦27027 to let \add Yisra'el\add* go, so \add Yahweh\add* will kill¦27031 \em your\em* oldest son.’ ”\x + \xo 4:23: \xt Exo 12:29.\x*
\p
\v 24 \add One night\add* as they were \add overnighting at\add* a lodging¦27039 place¦27039 near the road, Yahweh¦27041 \add confronted Mosheh\add* and¦27037 would have killed¦27043 him,
\v 25 \add but his wife\add* Zipporah¦27046 took¦27045 a flint¦27047 \add knife\add* and cut¦27048 off¦27048 her¦27052 son's¦27052 foreskin¦27051 and \add threw\add* it¦27055 at \add Mosheh's\add* feet¦27054 saying¦27055, “You're definitely a bloody¦27059 \add husband\add* to¦27061 me¦27061.”
\v 26 (That was referring to¦27069 circumcision¦27069.) \add So\add* then¦27063 \add Yahweh left them alone\add*.
\p
\v 27 \add Meanwhile\add* Yahweh¦27073 had told Aharon¦27076, “Go¦27077 \add out\add* into the wilderness¦27080 to meet¦27078 Mosheh¦27079.” \add So\add* he went¦27081 and met¦27082 him at¦27083 the hill¦27083 of¦27083 God¦27084, and he kissed¦27085 him.
\v 28 Then¦27089 Mosheh¦27090 told Aharon¦27091 \add everything\add* that Yahweh¦27096 had told him and how he had sent¦27098 him, and all¦27100 the¦27102 miracles that he commanded¦27104 him \add to demonstrate\add*.
\p
\v 29 Then¦27106 Mosheh¦27107 and Aharon¦27108 went¦27106 \add to Egypt\add* and they¦27109 assembled¦27109 all¦27112 the \add Israeli\add* elders¦27114
\v 30 and¦27131 Aharon¦27119 told them \add everything\add* that¦27124 Yahweh¦27127 had¦27126 spoken¦27126 to Mosheh. Then¦27118 \add Mosheh\add* did the miracles in front of¦27133 \add them\add*,
\v 31 and the people¦27137 believed¦27136. Then¦27136 they heard¦27138 how Yahweh¦27142 had paid attention to the Israelis¦27146 and that he¦27148 had seen¦27148 their¦27151 suffering, and they bowed¦27152 and prostrated themselves \add in thankfulness\add*.
\c 5
\s1 Mosheh challenges the Egyptian king
\rem /s1 Bricks Without Straw; Moses and Aaron Speak to Pharaoh; Moses and Aaron before the King of Egypt; Bricks without Straw
\p
\v 1 After¦27156 that, Mosheh¦27158 and Aharon¦27159 went¦27157 in and said¦27160 to¦27161 Far'oh¦27163, “This is what Yisrael's¦27169 god¦27168 Yahweh¦27167 says¦27166: ‘Let¦27170 my¦27173 people¦27173 go¦27170, \add so\add* they¦27174 can celebrate¦27174 me¦27175 \add out\add* in the wilderness¦27176.’ ”
\p
\v 2 \add But the king\add* said¦27178, “Who's¦27180 \add this\add* ‘Yahweh’ that I should¦27185 listen¦27183 to \add him telling me\add* to let Yisrael¦27188 go? I don't¦27189 know¦27190 Yahweh, and¦27178 what's more, I won't¦27189 let \add the Israelis\add* go \add anywhere\add*.”
\p
\v 3 “The¦27202 god¦27202 of¦27202 \add us\add* Hebrews¦27203 has¦27204 met¦27204 with us,” they¦27201 \add ≈replied\add*, “Please¦27207, let¦27206 us go¦27206 three¦27209 days¦27210 into the wilderness¦27211 so¦27212 we can sacrifice¦27212 to our¦27214 god Yahweh¦27213, \add because we don't want him to strike\add* us with \add ≈deadly diseases\add* or¦27219 with \add war\add*.”
\p
\v 4 “Mosheh¦27227 and¦27228 Aharon¦27228,” the king¦27224 of¦27224 Egypt¦27225 answered, “why are¦27229 you¦27229 causing¦27229 the people¦27232 to \add slacken\add* off from¦27233 their¦27233 tasks? \add Get back\add* to your¦27235 \add work\add*.”
\v 5 And Far'oh¦27238 \add continued\add*, “Listen, there's many¦27241 \add of you\add* and¦27237 you \add two\add* have¦27245 caused¦27245 them to rest from¦27247 their¦27247 \add duties\add*.”
\rem /s1 Making Bricks without Straw
\p
\v 6 Then¦27249 on¦27256 that¦27252 \add very same\add* day¦27251, Far'oh¦27250 ordered¦27249 the¦27255 people's¦27256 slave masters and¦27257 supervisors,
\v 7 “Don't give¦27264 straw to the people¦27266 to make¦27267 bricks¦27268 with as¦27269 \add you've done up till now\add*. They can go¦27272 and¦27273 gather¦27273 straw for themselves.
\v 8 But¦27277 \add keep\add* the¦27279 \add same\add* quota of¦27279 bricks¦27280 \add as before\add*—don't¦27288 decrease it because¦27291 they \add must be\add* idle¦27293 \add if they have time to say\add*, ‘Let¦27301 us go¦27301—let¦27302 us sacrifice¦27302 to our¦27303 god¦27303.’
\v 9 \add Give the men more work so they'll be too busy to worry about deceptive words\add*.”
\p
\v 10 \add So\add* the slave masters and supervisors of¦27321 the people¦27322 went¦27320 out and told \add them all\add*, “\add The king\add* has said¦27324 that he¦27330 won't¦27332 give¦27333 you any more straw¦27335,
\v 11 \add so\add* you'll \add all have to\add* go¦27338 and get your own straw¦27341 from wherever¦27342 you can find¦27343 it, because¦27344 there'll¦27345 be no reduction to your \add quota\add*.”
\v 12 So¦27350 the¦27351 people¦27351 dispersed throughout all¦27352 of¦27354 Egypt¦27355 to¦27356 gather¦27356 stubble¦27357 for¦27358 straw¦27358,
\v 13 and the taskmasters¦27360 were \add hassling\add* them¦27361, saying¦27362, “\add Hurry up and\add* finish your¦27364 day's work¦27364, the same as¦27369 when¦27370 \add you were given\add* straw¦27371.”
\v 14 Then¦27373 the Israeli¦27375 foremen¦27374 that Far'oh's slave masters had¦27379 appointed¦27379 were beaten¦27373 and¦27373 told, “Why haven't you¦27386 met your¦27387 required¦27387 brick¦27388 making¦27388 \add quota\add* yesterday¦27393 or today? \add It's just the same as in the past\add*.”
\p
\v 15 \add So\add* the Israeli¦27400 foremen¦27399 went¦27398 and¦27402 complained to Far'oh¦27405, “Why¦27401 are¦27408 you¦27408 treating your¦27410 servants¦27410 like this?
\v 16 We haven't been given¦27414 any straw and¦27416 yet they're telling us to keep making¦27419 bricks¦27416. And¦27420 see, we your servants¦27421 get beaten, but¦27416 it's your people's¦27424 mistake.”
\p
\v 17 “You're all lazy—plain lazy!” \add the king responded\add*. “That's why you're saying¦27434, ‘Let us go¦27435 sacrifice¦27436 to¦27437 Yahweh¦27437.’
\v 18 \add So\add* go¦27440 now¦27439! \add Back to\add* work¦27441! And¦27442 you won't¦27443 be¦27445 given¦27445 straw¦27442, but¦27439 you \add still have to meet the quota\add* of¦27447 bricks¦27448.”
\p
\v 19 \add When\add* the Israeli¦27453 foremen¦27452 heard that the quota wasn't going to¦27458 be reduced, they¦27451 \add realised how serious their problem was\add*,
\v 20 \add so\add* when they left¦27477 Far'oh¦27479 they \add confronted\add* Mosheh¦27471 and¦27468 Aharon¦27474 who were waiting to¦27476 meet¦27476 them¦27476,
\v 21 “May¦27483 Yahweh¦27484 \add see what you've done and judge you for it\add* because you've \add raised a big stink in Far'oh's mind\add* and with¦27494 his servants¦27495. \add Now they have their excuse\add* to¦27500 kill¦27500 us¦27500!”
\s1 Mosheh complains to God
\rem /s1 Moses Complains to the Lord; God Promises Deliverance
\p
\v 22 Then¦27502 Mosheh¦27503 went back to¦27504 Yahweh¦27506 and¦27502 \add asked\add*, “My¦27508 master¦27508, why have¦27510 you \add taken it out on our\add* people¦27511? Was this why you sent¦27515 me¦27515?
\v 23 \add Ever\add* since¦27517 I went¦27518 to Far'oh¦27521 to \add deliver your message\add*, he's \add made\add* these people¦27533 \add suffer\add* and¦27517 you¦27530 \add clearly\add* haven't rescued \add them\add*.”
\c 6
\rem /s1 Israel's Deliverance Assured; Promises of Deliverance
\p
\v 1 Then¦27535 Yahweh¦27536 said¦27535 to Mosheh¦27539, “Now¦27540 you'll see¦27541 what I'll do¦27543 to Far'oh¦27544. \add I'll use my power to force him to\add* let¦27548 them go¦27548, and¦27535 then¦27535 \add using my power again\add* he'll \add actually\add* drive¦27551 them out¦27551 of¦27552 his¦27552 \add country\add*.”
\s1 God promises to rescue the people
\rem /s1 God Calls Moses
\p
\v 2 Then God¦27556 spoke¦27555 to Mosheh¦27559 \add again\add*, “I am¦27563 Yahweh¦27563\x + \xo 6:2-3: \xt Gen 17:1; 28:3; 35:11; Exo 3:13-15.\x*
\v 3 and I \add revealed myself\add* to Abraham¦27568, to Isaac¦27571, and to Yacob¦27574 as El Shaddai (God¦27575 the provider) but¦27565 they didn't¦27579 know¦27580 me by my¦27577 name¦27577 Yahweh¦27578.
\v 4 \add What's more,\add* I made an agreement¦27587 with¦27601 them: to give¦27589 them the Canaan¦27594 region¦27593—the area which¦27598 they¦27600 had been staying in¦27601.
\v 5 \add Furthermore,\add* I've¦27606 heard¦27606 the complaints from the Israelis¦27611 who have been enslaved by the Egyptians, and¦27616 I have remembered¦27616 my¦27619 agreement¦27619.
\v 6 Therefore, tell the Israelis¦27625, ‘I am¦27628 Yahweh¦27628 and I will¦27629 \add help you escape from\add* the \add control\add* of the Egyptians and rescue¦27634 you from their¦27636 slavery. I'll¦27629 buy you out of slavery using my power and by¦27639 \add punishing the Egyptians for this injustice\add*.
\v 7 I'll take¦27644 you all to be my own people¦27647, and¦27644 I'll be your god. Then¦27644 you'll know¦27651 that¦27652 I am¦27654 Yahweh¦27654 your god—the one rescuing you all from¦27658 the Egyptian¦27660 oppression.
\v 8 Then¦27662 I'll \add take\add* you all to the¦27666 land¦27666 that¦27667 I \add promised\add* to give to Abraham¦27674, to Yitshak¦27675, and to Yacob¦27676, and I'll give it¦27678 to you as \add your own\add*. I am¦27682 Yahweh¦27682.’ ”
\v 9 \add So\add* Mosheh \add passed all that on\add* to the Israelis¦27690, but¦27684 they wouldn't listen¦27692 to Mosheh, because they were \add both discouraged and worn out from the slavery\add*.
\p
\v 10 Then Yahweh¦27703 told Mosheh¦27706,
\v 11 “Go and speak¦27710 to the Egyptian¦27715 king¦27714 Far'oh¦27713 and \add tell him\add* that he¦27716 needs¦27716 to let¦27716 the Israelis¦27721 leave his¦27722 country.”
\v 12 And Mosheh¦27725 said¦27728 in¦27726 front¦27726 of¦27726 Yahweh¦27727, “Look, the¦27730 Israelis¦27732 haven't listened¦27735 to¦27738 me¦27738, so why would Far'oh¦27739 listen¦27738? And I have¦27735 \add difficulty speaking\add*.”
\rem /s1 Family Record of Moses and Aaron
\v 13 Then Yahweh¦27746 spoke¦27745 to Mosheh¦27749 and Aharon¦27752, and he¦27753 gave them¦27753 a command¦27753 to \add pass on to\add* the Israelis and to the Egyptian¦27770 king¦27761 Far'oh¦27760: to \add take\add* the Israelis out of Egypt¦27762.
\s1 The ancestors of Mosheh and Aharon
\rem /s1 The Ancestors of Moses and Aaron; The Family Record of Moses and Aaron; The Genealogy of Moses and Aaron
\p
\v 14 The¦27774 following were¦27782 the leaders of¦27787 the clans¦27787:
\p The sons¦27778 of \bd Reuben\bd* (Yisra'el's¦27781 eldest son) were Hanoch¦27782, Pallu¦27783, Hezron¦27784, and Karmi, and \add they headed\add* the¦27787 clans of Reuben.
\p
\v 15 \bd Simeon\bd*'s sons¦27790 were¦27792 Yemuel, Yamin¦27793, Ohad, Yakin¦27795, Zohar¦27796, and Shaul¦27797—the son¦27798 of a Canaanite¦27800 woman, and \add they headed\add* the clans¦27802 of Simeon.
\p
\v 16 \bd Levi\bd*'s sons¦27807 were Gershon¦27811, Kohath¦27812, and¦27805 Merari¦27813. Levi was 137 years¦27814 old when he died.\x + \xo 6:16-19: \xt Num 3:17-20; 26:57-58; 1Ch 6:16-19.\x*
\v 17 Gershon's¦27823 sons¦27822 were¦27824 Libni¦27824 and Shimei¦27825, and \add they headed\add* their¦27826 clans¦27826.
\v 18 Kohath's¦27829 sons¦27828 were Amram¦27830, Izhar¦27831, Hebron¦27832, and¦27828 Uzziel¦27833. Kohath was 133 years¦27834 old when he died.
\v 19 Merari's¦27843 sons¦27842 were¦27844 Mahli¦27844 and Mushi¦27845, and \add all of those headed\add* the¦27848 clans¦27847 of Levi¦27848.
\v 20 Amram married his¦27856 aunt Yochebed¦27855, and she¦27859 gave¦27859 birth¦27859 to Aharon¦27863 and Mosheh¦27866. Amram lived to be 137 years¦27867 old.
\v 21 Izhar's¦27876 sons¦27875 were¦27877 Korah¦27877, Nefeg¦27878, and¦27875 Zichri¦27879.
\v 22 Uzziel's¦27882 sons¦27881 were¦27883 Mishael¦27883, Elzafan¦27884, and¦27881 Sithri¦27885.
\p
\v 23 Aharon¦27888 \add married\add* Elisheba¦27891, Amminadab's¦27894 daughter¦27892 and the sister¦27895 of Nahshon¦27896, and she¦27899 gave¦27899 birth¦27899 to¦27900 Nadab¦27903, Abihu¦27906, Eleazar¦27909, and Ithamar¦27912.
\v 24 Korah's¦27915 sons¦27914 were¦27916 Assir¦27916, Elkanah¦27917, and Abiasaph¦27918, and \add they headed\add* the¦27920 clans¦27920 of¦27920 Korah.
\v 25 Aharon's¦27926 son¦27924 Eleazar took¦27927 a wife for¦27933 himself from the¦27924 daughters¦27930 of¦27924 Putiel¦27931 and¦27934 she¦27934 gave¦27934 birth¦27934 to¦27935 Finehas.
\p \add All of\add* those \add headed\add* the clans¦27943 of Levi.
\p
\v 26 It was to Aharon¦27946 and Mosheh¦27947 that Yahweh¦27950 had said¦27949, “Bring¦27952 the Israelis¦27956 out of Egypt¦27958 by their¦27961 \add tribes\add*.”
\v 27 They were¦27964 the ones¦27964 who¦27964 spoke¦27964 to the Egyptian¦27977 king¦27968 Far'oh¦27967, to bring¦27971 the Israelis¦27976 out of¦27974 Egypt¦27970.
\s1 God's reassurances to Mosheh and Aharon
\rem /s1 Moses and Aaron Obey God's Commands; Aaron to Speak for Moses; The Lord's Command to Moses and Aaron; The struggle with Pharaoh
\p
\v 28 On¦27983 the day¦27983 that Yahweh¦27985 spoke¦27984 to¦27986 Mosheh¦27988 in¦27989 Egypt¦27990,
\v 29 he¦27993 said¦27998, “I am¦28000 Yahweh¦27994. Tell the¦28005 Egyptian¦28006 king¦28005 Far'oh¦28004 everything that¦28010 I tell¦27998 you¦28013.”
\p
\v 30 “Please listen to me,” Mosheh¦28016 \add ≈responded¦28015\add*. “I'm \add not a good speaker\add*, so why should Far'oh¦28026 listen to me?”
\c 7
\rem /s1 Aaron's Staff Becomes a Snake
\p
\v 1 Yahweh¦28030 \add ≈answered\add*, “Listen¦28033, I've¦28035 made you¦28035 \add like\add* God¦28036 to Far'oh¦28037, and¦28038 your brother¦28039 Aharon¦28038 will¦28040 be¦28040 your prophet¦28041.
\v 2 You'll \add quietly\add* say everything that I tell you¦28049 to and then your brother¦28051 Aharon¦28050 will¦28052 \add relay it\add* to Far'oh¦28055 \add and tell him\add* that he¦28056 must¦28044 let¦28056 the¦28059 Israelis¦28061 leave his¦28062 country.
\v 3 \add But\add* I'll \add make\add* Far'oh¦28069 \add stubborn\add* and \add so\add* I'll increase the signs¦28073 and miracles \add that I'll do\add* in¦28077 Egypt¦28078.\x + \xo 7:3: \xt Acts 7:36.\x*
\v 4 Far'oh¦28084 won't¦28080 listen¦28082 to you so I'll \add pressure\add* Egypt, and¦28080 I'll use severe judgements¦28102 \add to ensure that I\add* bring¦28090 my armies—my people the Israelis¦28099—out Egypt.
\v 5 The Egyptians will¦28105 know¦28105 that¦28107 I am¦28110 Yahweh¦28110 when¦28111 I \add demonstrate my power to\add* Egypt¦28117 \add by taking\add* the Israelis¦28123 out from the middle of them¦28124.”
\v 6 \add So\add* Mosheh¦28127 and¦28128 Aharon¦28128 did¦28134 everything that Yahweh¦28131 told¦28130 them to do.
\v 7 (Mosheh¦28136 was eighty and¦28136 Aharon¦28141 was eighty-three when¦28147 they¦28147 were \add interacting with\add* Far'oh¦28150.)
\s1 Staffs and snakes
\rem /s1 Aaron's Staff Becomes a Snake; Aaron's Walking Stick; Aaron's Miraculous Rod
\p
\v 8 Then Yahweh¦28154 told Mosheh¦28157 and Aharon¦28160,
\v 9 “\add When\add* Far'oh tells¦28164 you¦28171 to perform a miracle \add as a sign\add*, then \add just\add* tell Aharon to take¦28175 his\f + \fr 7:9 \ft It's not clear here in this account, or even in the following events, if Mosheh and Aharon had separate miraculous sticks, or if Mosheh had (temporarily?) entrusted his original staff/rod/stick to his brother and spokesman Aharon.\f* staff and throw¦28179 it down in front¦28180 of¦28180 Far'oh so it turns into¦28184 a snake¦28184.”
\v 10 \add So\add* Mosheh¦28187 and Aharon went¦28186 in to¦28189 Far'oh's \add courtyard\add* and did¦28192 what Yahweh¦28197 had¦28196 told¦28196 them: Aharon threw¦28198 his staff¦28202 down in front¦28203 of¦28203 Far'oh and his servants¦28206 and it¦28207 turned into¦28208 a snake¦28208.
\v 11 Then¦28210 Far'oh¦28213 also¦28211 called¦28210 for \add his\add* wise men¦28214 and sorcerers¦28215, and the Egyptian¦28221 magicians did¦28216 \add the same thing\add* using their¦28222 magic—
\v 12 each¦28226 \add of them\add* threw¦28225 down¦28225 his¦28227 staff and¦28225 they¦28228 became snakes¦28229, \add except that\add* Aharon's¦28234 staff swallowed¦28230 up their¦28236 staffs¦28236.
\v 13 \add But\add* Far'oh's¦28240 \add mind was made up\add* and¦28238 he \add took no notice of them\add* as¦28244 Yahweh¦28246 had¦28245 said¦28245 \add earlier\add*.
\s1 Plague 1: Water to blood
\rem /s1 A Plague of Blood; The Plague of Blood; The First Plague: Water Turned to Blood; Disasters Strike Egypt
\p
\v 14 Then Yahweh¦28250 told Mosheh¦28253, “Far'oh¦28256 \add is very stubborn and\add* he's refused¦28257 to let¦28258 the¦28259 people¦28259 go¦28258.
\v 15 \add So\add* go to Far'oh¦28264. Listen, \add every\add* morning¦28265 he¦28267 goes \add down\add* to the \add river\add*, so station yourself to meet¦28270 him¦28270 on¦28265 the bank¦28273. Take¦28280 the staff¦28275 with¦28265 you that¦28276 turned into a snake¦28279,
\v 16 and¦28283 tell him¦28284, ‘Yahweh¦28285, the god¦28286 of¦28286 the Hebrews¦28287, \add previously\add* sent¦28288 me to you to say¦28290, “Let¦28292 my¦28294 people¦28294 go¦28291 \add so\add* they¦28295 can serve¦28295 me in¦28296 the wilderness¦28296.” But¦28283 listen¦28300, until¦28301 now you haven't \add done it\add*
\v 17 so Yahweh says¦28306, “\add Now\add* you¦28309 will know¦28309 that¦28318 I am Yahweh because¦28310 of this: See, I'm about¦28315 to use my¦28320 staff¦28317 to strike¦28315 the water¦28323 in¦28308 the Nile and¦28326 it will turn to blood¦28327\x + \xo 7:17: \xt Rev 16:4.\x*
\v 18 and the fish¦28329 in¦28332 the river will die¦28333 and the river will stink¦28334. Then¦28329 \add your people\add* will \add gag\add* to¦28338 drink¦28338 \add the\add* water¦28339 from¦28340 the river.” ’
\p
\v 19 “Tell Aharon¦28353 to take¦28354 his staff¦28355 and stretch¦28356 out¦28356 his hand¦28358 over the waters¦28361 of Egypt¦28362: over their streams, their canals¦28369, and their pools¦28372, and over every¦28374 reservoir of their water, and \add the water\add* will become¦28378 blood. Then there'll be blood in \add the entire country\add*—even in wooden or stone¦28388 containers \add of water\add*.”
\p
\v 20 \add So\add* And Mosheh¦28393 and Aharon did¦28390 \add exactly\add* what Yahweh¦28398 had¦28397 commanded¦28397: \add Aharon\add* raised¦28399 the staff¦28400 and struck¦28401 the river, while Far'oh¦28408 and his servants¦28410 \add watched\add*, and the \add entire\add* river turned¦28411 to blood¦28418.
\v 21 Then¦28420 the fish¦28420 in¦28423 the river died¦28424 and the river stank¦28425, so the Egyptians couldn't drink¦28431 water¦28432 from¦28433 the river, and the blood¦28437 was all¦28438 \add over\add* Egypt¦28430.
\v 22 \add But\add* the Egyptian¦28447 sorcerers did¦28443 the same with¦28448 their¦28448 magic, and \add so\add* Far'oh¦28452 \add stayed stubborn\add* and wouldn't listen¦28455 to¦28456 them¦28456, just¦28457 as¦28457 Yahweh¦28459 had¦28458 said¦28458 \add earlier\add*.
\v 23 The Far'oh¦28462 turned¦28461 around and went¦28463 \add back to\add* his house¦28466, and he¦28469 \add put all of that out of his mind\add*.
\v 24 \add Meanwhile,\add* all¦28476 the Egyptians dug¦28475 holes around¦28479 the river to get water to drink¦28482, because¦28483 river water was undrinkable.
\s1 Plague 2: Frogs everywhere
\p
\v 25 \add So\add* a week went by since Yahweh¦28496 had struck¦28494 the¦28499 river
\c 8
\rem /s2 Frogs
\rem /s1 The Second Plague: Frogs; A Plague of Frogs
\nb
\v 1 and \add he\add* said¦28567 to Mosheh¦28571, “Go to Far'oh and tell him, ‘Yahweh¦28568 says that you \add must\add* let my people go \add so\add* they can serve me.\f + \fr 8:1 \ft This is 7:26 in some versifications (and the following three verses are 7:27, 28, & 29).\f*
\v 2 \add But\add* if you refuse to let \add them\add* go¦28608, then look, I'm about to pound your entire territory¦28613 with frogs¦28609.
\v 3 The frogs¦28625 will swarm in the river, then they'll climb out and go into your house and in your bedroom and onto your bed. They'll go into your servants' houses and among your people, and in your \add cooking\add* bowls and into your ovens.
\v 4 They'll climb on you and on your people¦28644 and on all your servants.’ ”
\p
\v 5 Then Yahweh said¦28653 to Mosheh¦28654, “Tell Aharon: Hold your¦28663 staff out over¦28657 the canals, the streams, and the pools, and cause the frogs¦28665 to come up onto the land of¦28667 Egypt.”
\v 6 \add So\add* Aharon reached out his hand over the Egyptian water \add supplies\add* and frogs came up and covered the land of Egypt.
\v 7 \add But\add* the sorcerers did the same with¦28693 their magic, and they¦28694 \add too\add* brought up frogs¦28687 over the land of Egypt¦28693.
\p
\v 8 Then Far'oh¦28701 called for Mosheh¦28698 and Aharon¦28699, and told \add them\add*, “Ask Yahweh to take the frogs¦28710 away from me and from my people. Then I'll let \add your\add* people go¦28697, and they can sacrifice to Yahweh.”
\p
\v 9 Mosheh¦28720 answered, “I'll pray for you and your servants and your people, \add praying that\add* the frogs¦28722 will keep away from you and your homes and only be left in the river. \add And I'll give you the honour of specifying when it'll happen\add*.”
\p
\v 10 “Tomorrow \add morning\add*,” \add the king replied\add*.
\p “Just as you say,” \add Mosheh responded\add*, “so that you'll know that there's no one \add else\add* like our god Yahweh.
\v 11 \add Tomorrow\add* the frogs will leave you and go away from your houses, and from your servants and your people. They'll only be left in the river.”
\v 12 Then Mosheh and Aharon¦28768 left Far'oh and Mosheh called out to Yahweh¦28761 concerning the \add retraction\add* of¦28776 the frogs that he'd sent against \add the king\add*.
\v 13 \add So\add* Yahweh did¦28786 what Mosheh had requested and the frogs died off from the houses and the courtyards, and from the fields.
\v 14 \add The people\add* collected them into many piles and the¦28828 \add whole country\add* stank.
\v 15 \add But when\add* Far'oh¦28837 saw that there was \add relief from the frogs\add*, he \add firmed his resolve\add* and didn't¦28845 listen¦28847 to \add Mosheh and Aharon\add*, just¦28849 as¦28849 Yahweh¦28851 had¦28850 said¦28850 \add previously\add*.
\s1 Plague 3: Lice
\rem /s1 A Plague of Gnats; The Third Plague: Gnats; The Plague of Gnats
\p
\v 16 Then Yahweh¦28856 said¦28855 to Mosheh¦28859, “Tell Aharon: Stretch out your staff and strike the dust on the ground, and it will turn into lice\f + \fr 8:16 \ft The Hebrew word ‘כֵּן/כִּנִּם’ (kēn/kinnim) could refer to either lice, gnats, or fleas. However, unlike lice, gnats don't usually \+em reside\+em* on people and animals (which seems to be implied by the text).\f* all over Egypt.”
\v 17 So Aharon held out his staff and struck the dust \add with it\add*, and \add the dust\add* became lice on the people¦28890 and on the animals. All over Egypt¦28897, the dust turned into lice
\v 18 and the sorcerers tried to do the same with¦28910 their magic but¦28909 they were unable to. \add So\add* there¦28923 were lice on¦28910 people¦28917 and animals
\v 19 and the magicians said to Far'oh, “This is God's finger.” \add But Far'oh was stubborn\add* and he didn't listen to them, just as Yahweh had said \add previously\add*.\x + \xo 8:19: \xt Luk 11:20.\x*
\s1 Plague 4: The swarm
\rem /s1 The Fourth Plague: Flies; A Plague of Flies; The Plague of Flies
\p
\v 20 Then Yahweh said to Mosheh, “Get up early in the morning and \add when you see Far'oh\add* coming¦28949 down to the river, station yourself in front¦28962 of¦28962 \add him\add* and tell him, ‘Yahweh saw that you \add must\add* let my people go \add so\add* they can serve me.
\v 21 Certainly if you don't release my people immediately, then take note: I'm about to send the swarm\f + \fr 8:21 \ft The Hebrew word ‘עָרֹב’ (ˊārov) probably meaning ‘mixture’ is sometimes translated as ‘swarm’. This plague could have been anything from flies to other insects (hornets or mosquitoes???) all the way to a mixture of wild animals.\f* at you and your servants and at your people and into your houses. The Egyptians' houses will be full of the swarm and even the ground that they're on.
\v 22 \add But when it happens,\add* I'll distinguish the Goshen region where my people live so the swarm won't¦28981 be there, \add so that you'll be able to see\add* that I'm Yahweh¦28989 \add and I'm right here\add* in this land.
\v 23 This sign will happen tomorrow and¦29007 \add you'll see how I plan to set my people free\add* from your people.’ ”
\v 24 \add So\add* Yahweh¦29021 did \add what he'd said\add* and a heavy swarm came to¦29021 Far'oh's house and the houses of his servants and all the Egyptians—the country was ruined by the \add arrival\add* of the swarm.
\p
\v 25 Then Far'oh¦29046 called for Mosheh¦29035 and to Aharon and told \add them\add*, “Go¦29038 and sacrifice¦29061 to the¦29045 god of you¦29039 all, \add but here in Egypt\add*.”
\p
\v 26 “It¦29065 wouldn't be right to do that,” Mosheh¦29066 \add ≈replied\add*, “because our sacrifices to Yahweh¦29072 our god \add would be offensive\add* to Egyptians. Listen, if we made sacrifices in front of the Egyptians \add that are repulsive to them\add*, wouldn't they \add throw rocks at us to try to kill us\add*?
\v 27 We need to travel into the¦29077 wilderness for three days, then we'll sacrifice to our god Yahweh¦29076 \add there\add*, just like he's¦29079 telling us to.”
\p
\v 28 \add So\add* Far'oh¦29090 \add relented\add*, “I'll let you all go into the wilderness and¦29097 sacrifice to Yahweh your god—except you definitely can't¦29097 go very far. \add Now,\add* ask God for me \add for the plague to stop\add*.”
\p
\v 29 “Listen, I'm about to \add leave now\add*,” Mosheh \add ≈responded\add*, “and I'll pray to Yahweh and he'll withdraw the swarm from Far'oh tomorrow, and from his servants and his people. \add But\add* may Far'oh not be dishonest \add again and\add* fail to let our people go to sacrifice to Yahweh.”
\p
\v 30 Then Mosheh left Far'oh and prayed to Yahweh
\v 31 and Yahweh did \add what\add* Mosheh \add asked him\add* and he withdrew the swarm from Far'oh, and from his servants and his people. Not one remained.
\v 32 \add But\add* Far'oh \add changed his mind and remained stubborn and he wouldn't let the Israelis leave\add*.
\c 9
\s1 Plague 5: The stock disease
