\rem /s1 The Fifth Plague: Livestock Diseased; The Plague on Livestock; A Plague against Livestock
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh said¦29104 to Mosheh¦29108, “Go¦29109 in to Far'oh¦29112 and¦29113 tell him¦29114: ‘This is what the god¦29119 of¦29119 the Hebrews¦29120. Yahweh, says¦29117: Let¦29121 my people¦29124 go \add so\add* they¦29125 can serve¦29125 me¦29125.
\v 2 Certainly if you're¦29133 unwilling to¦29132 release them and¦29133 if you keep¦29134 holding¦29134 onto them,
\v 3 then listen. Yahweh¦29140 is¦29141 about¦29141 to¦29141 \add inflict\add* a very serious \add ≈deadly disease\add* onto your¦29142 livestock¦29142 which¦29143 are in¦29142 the fields¦29144—on the horses¦29145, the donkeys¦29146, the camels¦29147, the cattle¦29148, and¦29149 on the flock¦29149.
\v 4 \add But\add* Yahweh¦29155 will distinguish¦29154 between¦29156 the livestock¦29157 of the Israelis and¦29154 the Egyptians—none of the Israelis' animals will die¦29163.’ ”
\v 5 Then¦29170 Yahweh \add said when it would happen\add*, “Yahweh will¦29175 do¦29175 this¦29178 \add to Egypt\add* tomorrow¦29174.”
\p
\v 6 \add So\add* the next¦29187 day¦29187 Yahweh¦29182 did \add what he'd said\add* and¦29181 all of the livestock of the Egyptians¦29191 died, yet none at all from the livestock of the Israelis¦29195 died.
\v 7 Far'oh sent¦29201 \add people to investigate\add*, and wow, not¦29204 even one¦29211 of the livestock¦29207 of the Israelis¦29208 had¦29206 died¦29206. \add But\add* Far'oh was strong-willed and wouldn't let¦29216 the people go¦29216.
\s1 Plague 6: Boils
\rem /s1 The Plague of Boils; The Sixth Plague: Boils; A Plague of Boils
\p
\v 8 Then Yahweh¦29223 told Mosheh and Aharon¦29229, “Take¦29230 handfuls of ash out of a furnace, then Mosheh should \add toss it up in the air in front\add* of Far'oh¦29240.
\v 9 It¦29242 will become a powder over all Egypt and then¦29242 it¦29249 will turn into boils¦29256—bursting blisters¦29258—on people and on animals all over Egypt.”
\v 10 \add So\add* they¦29264 took¦29264 some ash from a furnace, and standing¦29269 in¦29280 front¦29270 of Far'oh¦29271, Mosheh¦29274 \add tossed it up into the air\add* and it turned into boils with¦29280 bursting blisters¦29278 on¦29280 the people and the animals.\x + \xo 9:10: \xt Rev 16:2.\x*
\v 11 Because of the boils, the sorcerers weren't \add even\add* able to stand¦29287 \add there with\add* Mosheh¦29289 because¦29292 the sorcerers \add suffered with them\add* along with all¦29297 the Egyptians.
\v 12 \add But\add* Yahweh caused Far'oh¦29306 to remain stubborn and¦29301 he¦29311 didn't¦29307 listen¦29308 to them¦29309, just¦29310 as¦29310 Yahweh had¦29311 told Mosheh¦29315 \add previously\add*.
\s1 Plague 7: Hail
\rem /s1 A Plague of Hail; The Seventh Plague: Thunder and Hail; The Plague of Hail
\p
\v 13 Then Yahweh told Mosheh¦29322, “Get up early¦29323 in the morning¦29324, and stand in front¦29326 of Far'oh¦29327 and say¦29328 to¦29329 him¦29329, ‘This is what Yahweh, the god¦29334 of the Hebrews¦29335, says¦29332: Let¦29336 my¦29339 people¦29339 go¦29336 so¦29340 they¦29340 can serve¦29340 me¦29340
\v 14 because¦29360 this¦29345 time¦29344, I'm about¦29347 to send¦29347 all my¦29352 plagues¦29352 to you and your servants¦29356, and your people¦29357. \add You'll all be deeply distressed by them\add*, in order that you might know¦29359 that there¦29361 is¦29361 no \add one else\add* like¦29362 me¦29362 in all the¦29365 world.
\v 15 \add By this time\add*, I could have reached out¦29369 and struck¦29373 you and your¦29377 people¦29377 with¦29378 such \add ≈deadly disease or destruction¦29379\add* that you would all have been obliterated from¦29380 the¦29382 land¦29382.
\v 16 However, I've¦29387 kept you for this reason: to show¦29389 you my power¦29392 and¦29384 in order to proclaim my name¦29395 all¦29396 over the¦29398 world.\x + \xo 9:16: \xt Rom 9:17.\x*
\v 17 You're still considering yourself¦29401 to be greater than my people¦29402 by failing to release them¦29404.
\v 18 \add Now\add* listen¦29406: I'm about¦29407 to cause extremely heavy¦29411 hail to rain¦29407 \add down\add* at¦29418 this time¦29408 tomorrow¦29409. \add It'll be hail like\add* which¦29413 has¦29416 never been \add seen before\add* in¦29418 Egypt¦29418 from¦29419 the¦29421 day¦29421 it¦29422 was¦29422 founded¦29422 until now¦29425.
\v 19 \add So\add* send¦29428 for your¦29432 livestock¦29432 and everything you own in the country now, and bring¦29429 it¦29445 in under cover because the hail¦29452 will come¦29450 down¦29450 on¦29446 every person and animal¦29442 that¦29443 is found¦29445 in the countryside¦29438 and not \add sheltering under a roof\add*, and they¦29453 will die¦29453.’ ”
\v 20 Some of Far'oh's servants¦29465 who believed what Yahweh¦29459 said, quickly brought their slaves and¦29466 their livestock¦29468 back into¦29469 their houses¦29471,
\v 21 but¦29473 those \add people\add* who didn't¦29474 believe Yahweh¦29481 \add just\add* left¦29482 their slaves and¦29473 their animals out in¦29489 the¦29480 countryside¦29489.
\p
\v 22 “Raise your¦29500 arm up toward the sky,” Yahweh¦29493 told Mosheh¦29496, “and hail¦29505 \add will strike\add* all¦29506 over Egypt¦29509—on the people and animals and on every plant in the Egyptian¦29522 countryside¦29520.”
\v 23 \add So\add* Mosheh¦29525 lifted his¦29528 staff¦29528 to¦29529 the sky, and Yahweh sent thunder¦29534 and hail, and \add lightning caused fires\add* on the land¦29544, and \add so\add* Yahweh rained¦29539 hail \add down\add* onto Egypt¦29545.
\v 24 There was hail, and¦29547 \add also\add* fire¦29549 coming \add down\add* in the middle¦29551 of the hail. It was very heavy¦29553, such as had¦29558 never been¦29558 in all¦29560 of Egypt¦29563 since¦29564 it¦29565 became¦29565 a nation¦29566.\x + \xo 9:24: \xt Rev 8:7; 16:21.\x*
\v 25 The¦29569 hail¦29569 struck everything in Egypt¦29573—everything that¦29577 was in the countryside, from¦29579 people to animals. \add It also\add* struck every plant and¦29568 broke every tree¦29594 \add out\add* in the countryside.
\v 26 Only in¦29599 the Goshen¦29600 region¦29599 where¦29601 the Israelis¦29605 were, was¦29607 there¦29603 no hail¦29608.
\p
\v 27 Then¦29610 Far'oh¦29611 sent¦29610 for Mosheh¦29613 and Aharon¦29614 and told them¦29616, “I've¦29617 sinned¦29617 this¦29618 time¦29618. Yahweh¦29619 is¦29620 righteous¦29620, and me and my¦29622 people¦29622 are¦29623 guilty¦29623.
\v 28 Pray to Yahweh¦29628 \add to stop it,\add* because the¦29631 thunder¦29631 of¦29631 God¦29632 and hail¦29633 are too much. Then I'll¦29634 release you all—you won't¦29636 have to remain \add here\add*.”
\p
\v 29 “As¦29643 I'm leaving the city¦29646,” Mosheh¦29642 \add ≈replied\add*, “I'll¦29647 spread¦29647 my palms¦29650 \add out\add* to Yahweh. The¦29654 thunder¦29654 will stop and¦29640 the hail¦29656 won't¦29657 continue so¦29661 that you¦29662 \add will be able to see\add* that the earth¦29665 belongs to Yahweh.
\v 30 \add But as for\add* you and¦29668 your¦29668 servants¦29668, I¦29669 know¦29669 that¦29670 you still don't¦29671 fear¦29672 Yahweh¦29674 God¦29675.”
\p
\v 31 (\add Now\add* the flax and the barley were \add flattened by the hail\add* because¦29680 the barley was \add full grown and starting to fill out\add* and the flax was in bloom.
\v 32 But¦29690 the wheat¦29686 and¦29686 the spelt¦29687 weren't \add destroyed because they come later in the season\add*.)
\p
\v 33 \add So\add* Mosheh¦29695 left Far'oh¦29697, and as he was leaving the city¦29700, he spread¦29701 his palms¦29702 \add out\add* to¦29703 Yahweh¦29705 and the thunder¦29707 and hail¦29708 ceased¦29706, and the rain¦29709 stopped pouring¦29712 down on the land.
\v 34 \add But when\add* Far'oh¦29716 saw¦29715 that¦29717 the rain¦29720 and hail¦29721 and thunder¦29722 had¦29719 stopped, he added to¦29724 his sins¦29724 and he \add firmed his resolve\add*—he and his servants¦29728.
\v 35 And¦29735 \add so\add* Far'oh¦29732 \add remained stubborn\add* and¦29730 didn't¦29733 let¦29734 the¦29737 Israelis¦29738 leave, just¦29739 as¦29739 Yahweh¦29741 had¦29740 said¦29740 through Mosheh¦29744 \add previously\add*.
\c 10
\s1 Plague 8: Locusts
\rem /s1 A Plague of Locusts; The Plague of Locusts; The Eighth Plague: Locusts
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh¦29748 told Mosheh¦29751, “Go¦29752 in to Far'oh¦29755 because¦29756 I've¦29759 made¦29759 him and his servants¦29766 \add stubborn\add* in order to \add be able to\add* demonstrate these¦29770 miracles of mine among them,
\v 2 and so that \add in the future you'll be able to tell your descendants\add* that I \add made\add* Egypt¦29784 \add look foolish\add*, and my¦29787 miracles which¦29788 I demonstrated among¦29791 them—and \add so that\add* you will¦29792 know¦29792 that I am¦29796 Yahweh¦29796.”
\p
\v 3 \add So\add* Mosheh¦29799 and Aharon¦29800 went¦29798 in to Far'oh¦29803 and told him¦29805, “This is what Yahweh¦29809, God¦29810 of¦29810 the¦29811 Hebrews¦29811 says¦29808: \add how long before you'll submit to me\add*? Let¦29818 my¦29817 people¦29819 go¦29818 \add so\add* they¦29804 can serve¦29820 me,
\v 4 because¦29822 if¦29823 you¦29826 refuse¦29825 to release them, then listen, I'll¦29831 bring¦29832 locusts¦29834 into your¦29835 territory¦29835 tomorrow¦29833.
\v 5 They'll cover¦29837 the ground so that¦29854 no one will¦29843 \add even\add* be¦29843 able¦29843 to¦29855 see¦29844 it¦29859, and¦29837 they'll devour whatever remains¦29852 after the hail¦29858. Plus they'll devour every¦29862 tree¦29864 growing¦29865 in the countryside¦29869.
\v 6 They'll fill your houses and the houses of all your servants¦29876 and the houses of all the Egyptians. It'll be nothing like anything which¦29881 your \add parents generation\add* ever saw¦29884, nor anything seen by the \add previous generations\add* from the day¦29888 they¦29889 came to be on¦29890 the earth until¦29893 today.” Then¦29871 \add Mosheh\add* turned¦29896 around and went¦29889 out¦29897 from Far'oh¦29899 \add with Aharon\add*.
\p
\v 7 Then¦29901 Far'oh's servants¦29902 \add asked\add* him¦29904, “How much longer is this¦29909 \add going to cause trouble\add* for¦29910 us¦29910? Let¦29912 the¦29915 men¦29915 go¦29912 and¦29916 they'll worship¦29916 their¦29920 god¦29920 Yahweh¦29919. Don't¦29921 you¦29922 understand yet¦29921 that¦29909 Egypt¦29925 \add is being destroyed\add*?”
\p
\v 8 \add So\add* Mosheh¦29930 and Aharon¦29933 were brought¦29927 back to¦29934 Far'oh¦29936, and he¦29937 told them¦29938, “Go¦29939 and worship your¦29944 god¦29944 Yahweh¦29943. Who¦29946 \add are the ones\add* who'll be going¦29947?”
\p
\v 9 “We'll take our children and our old¦29952 folks,” Mosheh¦29950 \add ≈answered\add*. “We'll go¦29953 with our sons¦29954 and daughters¦29955, and with our flocks¦29956 and herds¦29957, because¦29959 the¦29960 festival¦29960 of Yahweh¦29962 is for¦29963 \add all of\add* us¦29963.”
\p
\v 10 “\add You'd need Yahweh with you if I ever\add* let you and¦29974 your¦29981 children go¦29972 \add together\add*,” Far'oh \add ≈responded¦29965\add*. “You're clearly \add plotting an evil trick\add*!
\v 11 \add Well that won't happen!\add* The men¦29988 may go¦29985 and worship¦29989 Yahweh¦29992 since that's what you've been wanting.” Then he¦29997 drove¦29997 them \add out of the room\add*.
\p
\v 12 Then Yahweh¦30005 told Mosheh¦30008, “Extend your¦30010 hand¦30010 over Egypt¦30014 and let \add the locusts\add* rise up¦30016 over the land¦30019, and let \add them\add* devour all¦30024 the plants—everything which the hail¦30033 had spared.”
\v 13 \add So\add* Mosheh¦30036 stretched¦30035 his staff¦30039 out over Egypt¦30043, and Yahweh¦30044 sent an east wind over the land¦30042 all that¦30052 day¦30051 and all night¦30055. Then¦30035 morning¦30056 came, and the east wind had brought the locusts¦30063.
\v 14 The¦30066 locusts covered across all¦30068 the land¦30070 of¦30070 Egypt¦30071 and came to rest everywhere within the \add country's\add* borders. They¦30075 were extremely numerous—never before¦30078 were there so many locusts, and it'll never be¦30087 \add repeated again\add*.\x + \xo 10:14-15: \xt Rev 9:2-3.\x*
\v 15 \add The cloud of locusts blocked the sun\add* and \add prevented people from being able to see very far\add*. They devoured all the plants on the land¦30106 and all of the fruit¦30110 on the trees that¦30112 the hail¦30114 had¦30113 left¦30117, and \add no piece\add* of greenery¦30120 remained on any tree¦30111 or plant in¦30121 the entire¦30095 Egyptian¦30127 countryside¦30123.
\p
\v 16 Far'oh¦30130 quickly summoned Mosheh¦30132 and Aharon¦30133 and said¦30134, “I've¦30135 sinned¦30135 against your¦30137 god¦30137 Yahweh¦30136 and against you¦30138.
\v 17 So¦30140 now¦30140 just this once, please¦30142 \add overlook\add* my sin¦30143 and pray¦30146 to¦30147 your¦30148 god¦30148 Yahweh¦30147 and just let him take this \add deathly plague\add* away from¦30150 me¦30150.”
\v 18 \add So Mosheh\add* left Far'oh¦30159 and went¦30157 out¦30157 and prayed¦30160 to¦30161 Yahweh¦30163,
\v 19 and Yahweh¦30166 \add caused\add* a very strong¦30170 sea¦30177 wind. \add The wind\add* carried¦30172 away¦30172 the locusts¦30175 and drove¦30176 them into the Red Sea—not a single locust¦30181 remained¦30180 anywhere in¦30183 Egyptian¦30185 territory¦30184.
\v 20 \add But\add* Yahweh¦30188 caused Far'oh¦30192 \add to remain stubborn\add* and¦30187 he wouldn't release the Israelis¦30198.
\s1 Plague 9: Darkness
\rem /s1 The Plague of Darkness; The Ninth Plague: Darkness; A Plague of Darkness
\p
\v 21 Then Yahweh¦30202 told Mosheh¦30205, “Raise your¦30207 hand¦30207 up toward the¦30210 sky and¦30211 let¦30211 it¦30211 become dark over¦30213 all Egypt¦30216—darkness \add that can be felt\add*.”
\v 22 \add So\add* Mosheh¦30221 stretched¦30220 his hand¦30224 out \add towards\add* the¦30227 sky, and¦30220 a \add thick\add* darkness¦30229 was¦30228 in¦30232 all¦30232 Egypt¦30235 for three¦30236 days¦30237.\x + \xo 10:22: \xt Psa 105:28; Rev 16:10.\x*
\v 23 No¦30256 one could see¦30241 \add anyone else\add*, and¦30253 for three¦30251 days¦30252 no one was able to go anywhere, yet there was light¦30258 in¦30259 \add the region where\add* the Israelis¦30255 lived¦30259.
\p
\v 24 Then¦30261 Far'oh¦30262 summoned Mosheh¦30265 and told him, “Go¦30267 serve¦30268 Yahweh¦30271. Your children can go¦30279 with¦30280 you all, \add but\add* your flocks¦30273 and your herds¦30274 must¦30275 remain behind.”
\p
\v 25 “On top of that,” Mosheh¦30283 \add ≈responded¦30282\add*, “you must¦30287 also give¦30287 us the sacrifices¦30289 and¦30282 offerings¦30290 to be burnt¦30290 when we¦30291 will¦30287 \add sacrifice to\add* Yahweh¦30292 our¦30288 god¦30293.
\v 26 And even our livestock¦30297 needs to go¦30298 with¦30299 us¦30299. Not¦30300 a hoof¦30302 will¦30301 be left behind because¦30303 we'll \add choose\add* from¦30304 them to serve¦30306 Yahweh our god¦30310. \add You see\add*, we won't¦30300 know¦30314 what¦30315 we must¦30298 \add sacrifice to\add* Yahweh until¦30321 we arrive¦30323 there¦30324.”
\p
\v 27 \add But\add* Yahweh¦30327 caused Far'oh¦30331 to \add strengthen his resolve\add* and¦30326 he wouldn't agree to let¦30334 them¦30334 go¦30334.
\v 28 “Get \add out of here\add*!” Far'oh¦30339 \add shouted\add*. “And watch yourself¦30343! You'll never see me again, because¦30349 if¦30349 I ever see you again, I'll \add have you executed\add*!”
\p
\v 29 “\add Yes,\add*” Mosheh¦30356 responded¦30355. “It's¦30355 just as you say¦30355: I'll¦30361 never see¦30363 you again¦30362!”
\c 11
\s1 Plague 10: Death of oldest sons
\rem /s1 Moses Announces the Death of the First-Born; The Plague on the Firstborn; Warning of the Final Plague; Death for Egypt's Firstborn
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh¦30368 told Mosheh¦30371, “I'll¦30375 send one more plague¦30373 to Far'oh¦30378 and¦30379 Egypt¦30381. After¦30382 that he'll let¦30385 you all go¦30385 from¦30387 here. \add What's more,\add* when he does he'll actually chase you all right out of here.
\v 2 \add Go and\add* quietly tell the people¦30399 that¦30400 both men and women¦30405 should ask¦30400 their \add Egyptian\add* neighbours¦30404 for items of gold¦30412 and silver¦30410.”
\v 3 Then¦30414 Yahweh¦30415 gave¦30414 the \add @Israelis¦30419\add* favour¦30418 in the eyes of the Egyptians. What's more, Mosheh¦30425 was¦30426 very¦30427 prestigious in the eyes of Far'oh's servants¦30431 and¦30414 in the eyes of the Egyptian¦30421 people¦30435.
\p
\v 4 Then Mosheh¦30439 told \add Far'oh\add*, “This is what Yahweh¦30442 says¦30441: Sometime around the middle¦30443 of¦30443 the night¦30444 I'll go¦30446 out¦30446 \add around\add* Egypt¦30448,
\v 5 and¦30450 every oldest son in Egypt¦30455 will die¦30450, from¦30456 the oldest son of Far'oh¦30457 who sits¦30458 on¦30459 his¦30461 throne¦30461, to the oldest son of the slave¦30464 girl¦30464 who \add lives\add* behind¦30466 the mill, as well as every oldest male offspring of \add ≈your livestock¦30470\add*.
\v 6 Then¦30472 there'll¦30472 be loud wailing right across Egypt¦30478 which¦30479 will be nothing anything that's ever happened before, and¦30472 nothing like it will \add ever\add* happen again.
\v 7 But¦30487 \add ≈amongst the Israelis¦30490\add*, \add ≈the dogs won't¦30491 growl at any person or animals\add* so¦30500 that you'll know¦30501 that Yahweh¦30504 distinguishes¦30503 between¦30505 Egypt¦30506 and¦30487 Yisrael¦30490.
\v 8 Then all your¦30528 servants¦30513 will¦30510 come¦30510 down¦30510 to me, and they'll bow¦30516 to me, \add ≈pleading\add*, ‘Go¦30521, you and all the¦30525 people¦30525 who \add ≈follow you\add*.’ And after¦30529 that¦30526 I'll¦30532 \add ≈leave Egypt\add*.” Then \add @Mosheh\add* \add ≈left\add* Far'oh¦30536 \add ≈in¦30528 a fiery rage\add*.
\p
\v 9 Then Yahweh¦30543 told Mosheh¦30546, “Far'oh¦30551 \add refused to\add* listen¦30549 to you¦30550, so¦30552 that I'm able to multiply my¦30554 miracles in¦30555 Egypt¦30556.”
\v 10 Mosheh¦30558 and¦30558 Aharon¦30559 had done¦30560 all¦30563 those miracles in¦30567 front¦30567 of¦30567 Far'oh \add but\add* Yahweh¦30570 \add had made Far'oh stubborn so\add* he¦30577 hadn't let¦30577 the¦30580 \add ≈Israelis¦30582 leave his¦30583 country\add*.
\c 12
\s1 Meal preparations to avoid death
\rem /s1 The Passover; The First Passover Instituted; The First Passover; The institution of the Passover
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh¦30587 told Mosheh¦30590 and Aharon¦30593 in¦30594 Egypt¦30595,\x + \xo 12:1-13: \xt Lev 23:5; Num 9:1-5; 28:16; Deu 16:1-2.\x*
\v 2 “This month is to¦30600 become the beginning¦30601 of the months¦30602 for¦30605 you all—\add from now on\add* it¦30604 will¦30603 be¦30603 the first¦30603 month of the \add new\add* year¦30607.
\v 3 Assemble¦30615 the people and¦30620 tell them that¦30619 on¦30617 the tenth of this month, every man¦30622 who's the head of the household¦30624 must select a lamb or young goat \add for his family\add*.
\v 4 \add But\add* if¦30630 the household's¦30633 too small¦30632 \add to cook\add* a whole animal, then¦30630 \add neighbours can combine and share—budgeting\add* on¦30643 how much each¦30645 person eats.
\v 5 You all can choose either a sheep¦30663 or a goat, but¦30664 it¦30659 must¦30659 be¦30659 a perfect, one-year-old male¦30655—without any defects.
\v 6 You¦30684 all must¦30678 \add ≈take special care of those animals\add* until¦30672 the fourteenth day¦30675 of this month¦30676, then¦30669 \add ≈every Israeli¦30682 household\add* will slaughter¦30678 \add @their animal early\add* that¦30677 evening¦30686.
\v 7 Then¦30705 they must¦30688 take¦30688 some¦30689 of the blood¦30691 and put¦30692 it on both¦30695 doorposts¦30696 and on the lintel¦30699 of the houses¦30701 that¦30702 they'll be in¦30706 when they eat¦30704 it.
\v 8 They¦30709 must¦30708 roast¦30714 them on¦30712 the fire¦30716 and eat¦30708 the meat¦30711 that¦30713 night¦30712, along with¦30712 flat bread¦30717 and bitter¦30720 herbs¦30720.
\v 9 Don't¦30731 eat¦30725 any of the meat raw¦30727 and don't¦30723 boil it in¦30730 water¦30730—it must be roasted¦30734 over the fire¦30736 with¦30730 its head¦30737, legs¦30740, and internal organs.
\v 10 Nothing must be left¦30747 by morning—anything that's¦30752 \add ≈not¦30745 eaten\add* must be burnt¦30758 in¦30757 the¦30752 fire¦30757.
\v 11 And this is how you all must eat it: with¦30766 your belts fastened on your waists, your sandals¦30765 on your feet¦30766, and¦30760 your staffs¦30767 in your hands. You all must eat it in a hurry. It is the Passover¦30772 to¦30774 Yahweh¦30774.
\p
\v 12 Then¦30776 during that¦30781 night¦30780, I will move throughout Egypt and I will \add ≈kill\add* all the \add ≈oldest males\add*—\add ≈both people and animals\add*. I will \add ≈execute\add* judgement¦30797 on¦30777 all the gods¦30794 of Egypt. I am¦30799 Yahweh¦30799.
\v 13 That blood \add on the doorposts and lintel\add* will be your sign¦30804 on the houses¦30806 which you are all in¦30819. I will see¦30810 the blood and will pass¦30814 over above you all, and the plague¦30820 won't¦30816 \add affect\add* you all with¦30819 destruction¦30821 as I strike¦30822 against Egypt¦30824.
\v 14 Then¦30826 this¦30828 day¦30827 will become a memorial¦30830 for you all, and¦30826 you'll celebrate it¦30832 as a festival¦30833 to¦30834 Yahweh¦30834 for \add future\add* generations¦30835—a statute¦30836 that¦30828 you'll all celebrate forever¦30837.\x + \xo 12:14-20: \xt Exo 23:15; 34:18; Lev 23:6-8; Num 28:17-25; Deu 16:3-8.\x*
\s1 The ‘Flat Bread Celebration’
\rem /s1 The Festival of Unleavened Bread
\p
\v 15 For seven days you all must only eat¦30843 flat bread \add made without yeast\add*. Yes, on the first day you all must remove¦30847 any yeast from your houses¦30849, because¦30850 anyone¦30854 who¦30854 eats¦30854 bread with yeast during those seven days, that person must be permanently driven out of Yisrael¦30859.
\v 16 On¦30867 the first¦30868 day, you all must¦30867 gather for a sacred¦30871 assembly¦30869, and¦30867 \add ≈similarly\add* on the seventh¦30873 day. No work¦30881 can be done on those \add days\add* \add ≈other than meal preparation\add*.
\v 17 You all must \add regularly\add* observe the \it Flat Bread \+add Celebration\+add*\it*, because¦30901 on¦30902 this very day I will have brought¦30905 your divisions¦30908 out¦30905 from¦30909 Egypt¦30910. You all must observe this day throughout¦30916 \add future\add* generations¦30916—a statute¦30917 that¦30904 you'll all celebrate forever¦30918.
\v 18 Starting on¦30921 14th of the first¦30920 month in the evening, you all must¦30926 eat¦30926 \add only\add* flat bread¦30927 until¦30928 the 21st day¦30929 of the month in the evening.
\v 19 For¦30951 seven days¦30936 you all must have no yeast in your¦30940 homes, because¦30941 anyone¦30945 who¦30945 eats¦30945 bread with¦30940 yeast in it¦30939, that person \add must be driven out\add* from¦30950 the Israeli¦30950 people whether they're a foreigner or a native¦30953.
\rem /s1 The First Passover
\v 20 You all mustn't eat \add anything made with\add* yeast. Wherever you live, you all must¦30960 only eat flat bread¦30964.
\s1 The first ever ‘pass-over’ celebration
\p
\v 21 Then Mosheh¦30968 summoned the¦30971 Israeli¦30971 elders¦30971 and told them¦30974, “Go ahead and select¦30976 a lamb or young goat for¦30977 each¦30969 family¦30979 and slaughter¦30980 it¦30973.
\v 22 Then¦30983 you all must¦30983 take¦30983 a bunch¦30984 of \add leafy\add* hyssop¦30985 \add stalks\add*, and dip¦30986 it in¦30987 the basin \add with the blood from the sacrifice\add* and paint the blood onto the lintel¦30994 and both¦30997 doorposts¦30998. \add After that\add*, \add ≈absolutely no one\add* should go¦31006 out¦31006 the door¦31008 of your houses¦31010 until¦31011 morning¦31013.
\v 23 Then¦31015 Yahweh¦31016 will¦31015 pass through¦31015 \add out Egypt\add* to strike the Egyptians¦31032 and \add when\add* he sees¦31021 the blood¦31024 on the lintel¦31027 and on the two¦31029 doorposts¦31030, \add *he'll\add* ‘pass over’ that doorway¦31035 and won't¦31036 permit¦31037 ‘the destroyer¦31038’ to enter into¦31040 your houses¦31042 to strike.\x + \xo 12:23: \xt Heb 11:28.\x*
\v 24 All¦31046 of you and your¦31053 descendants must¦31045 maintain this¦31049 as a law and celebrate it forever¦31056,
\v 25 \add so\add* when you all enter into¦31062 the¦31064 land¦31064 that¦31065 Yahweh¦31067 will¦31066 give¦31066 to¦31068 you just¦31069 as¦31069 he¦31070 \add ≈promised\add*, then¦31058 you must¦31061 \add ≈observe¦31071\add* this¦31075 ceremony¦31074.
\v 26 Then¦31077 when it¦31077 occurs, your children¦31082 will¦31080 ask, ‘What¦31083 does this¦31085 ceremony¦31084 \add mean\add* to¦31086 you?’
\v 27 you all must¦31088 tell \add <them\add*, ‘It's the sacrifice¦31089 of ‘pass-over¦31091’ to¦31093 Yahweh¦31093, who¦31094 passed¦31095 over the houses¦31098 of the Israelis¦31101 in¦31102 Egypt¦31102 \add when he\add* struck¦31103 the Egyptians and¦31107 rescued our¦31109 families.’ ”
\p And¦31111 the¦31098 people¦31112 bowed¦31111 their heads and prostrated themselves,
\v 28 and the¦31117 Israelis¦31118 went¦31115 and did¦31127 \add just\add* as¦31119 Yahweh¦31121 had¦31120 commanded¦31120 \add through\add* Mosheh¦31124 and Aharon¦31125.
\s1 The death of all the oldest male offspring
\rem /s1 The Tenth Plague: Death of the Firstborn; The Death of the First-Born
\p
\v 29 Then, in the middle¦31132 of the night¦31133, Yahweh¦31134 struck¦31135 all the oldest males in Egypt¦31140, from¦31141 the oldest son of Far'oh¦31142 who¦31143 sat¦31143 on¦31132 his¦31146 throne¦31146, to the firstborn¦31138 of the \add ≈prisoner¦31149 in the dungeon\add* and¦31153 all \add oldest male offspring\add* of the animals.\x + \xo 12:29: \xt Exo 4:22-23.\x*
\v 30 During the night¦31159, all the Egyptians got up¦31157, including¦31161 Far'oh¦31158 and¦31157 all his¦31163 servants¦31163. There was \add ≈tremendous wailing throughout\add* Egypt¦31166 because¦31171 \add it was a very rare house\add* where¦31175 there wasn't someone dead¦31179.
\rem /s1 The Exodus; Israel's Exodus from Egypt
\v 31 \add @Far'oh\add* called¦31181 for Mosheh¦31182 and Aharon¦31183 in the night¦31184 and told \add <them\add*, “Pack up and get out¦31187 from¦31188 among¦31188 my¦31189 people—you \add two\add* and all the other Israelis¦31196. Go and serve¦31198 Yahweh¦31201 as¦31202 you've said¦31202.
\v 32 Also take¦31210 your flocks¦31206 and your herds¦31209 as you've said¦31212, and go¦31213. And¦31214 bless¦31214 me¦31217 as well.
\rem /s1 The Exodus: From Rameses to Succoth
\p
\v 33 The Egyptians urged¦31219 the \add @Israelis¦31223\add*, hurrying¦31224 to let them¦31225 go from¦31226 the land¦31228 because¦31229 they¦31230 said¦31230, “We're all¦31231 dying¦31232.”
\p
\v 34 \add So\add* the¦31235 people¦31235 carried¦31234 their \add bread\add* dough¦31238 before¦31239 it¦31240 \add ≈could rise\add*. Their¦31236 bread-making bowls¦31241 were¦31242 wrapped in¦31243 their clothes¦31243 on¦31243 their shoulders¦31246.
\p
\v 35 The Israelis¦31250 did¦31251 everything that Mosheh¦31253 had instructed them, \add including\add* asking¦31254 for items of gold¦31260 and silver¦31258, and clothing¦31261 from the Egyptians.\x + \xo 12:35-36: \xt Exo 3:21-22.\x*
\v 36 Yahweh¦31263 gave¦31264 the people¦31268 favour¦31267 in the eyes¦31269 of the Egyptians, \add so by\add* their requests¦31271, they were able to strip the Egyptians of their wealth.
\rem /s1 The Israelites Leave Egypt; The exodus from Egypt
\p
\v 37 Then the Israelis¦31281 walked from Rameses¦31282 toward Succoth¦31283—about¦31284 600,000 strong men¦31289 on foot¦31288, not counting \add women and\add* children¦31291.
\v 38 A large assortment of other people also¦31293 went¦31297 with them¦31298, along with large numbers of sheep and goats, and cattle.
\p
\v 39 They¦31305 baked¦31305 the dough¦31308 that they brought¦31310 from Egypt into flat bread¦31312 since it¦31316 \add ≈hadn't had time to rise\add*,\f + \fr 12:39 \ft Most modern readers probably visualise yeast bought from a supermarket when ‘leaven’ (or even ‘yeast’) is mentioned in a Bible translation. However, it's much more likely that each day, a portion of the bread dough was set aside for the natural yeasts (think ‘sour dough’ bread) to accumulate, and then mixed into the new batch of dough the next day and left for the natural yeasts to work and rise the dough. The text here implies that it's possible that the Hebrew word translated ‘unleavened’ really meant ‘unrisen’ on that first ‘pass-over’ night.\f* because¦31314 they were driven¦31319 out¦31319 of Egypt \add ≈in a hurry\add* and hadn't \add ≈had time to prepare food¦31312 to take\add*.
\p
\v 40 The descendants of Yisra'el¦31334 \add !(Yacob¦31334)\add* had lived in¦31337 Egypt¦31337 for 430 years\x + \xo 12:40: \xt Gen 15:13; Gal 3:17.\x*
\v 41 to the day, because that¦31354 very day was when all¦31356 the \add various\add* divisions of Yahweh's¦31359 \add people\add* left Egypt¦31361.
\v 42 It was¦31363 a \add time\add* when they stayed awake all night as Yahweh¦31366 took them¦31367 out¦31367 of¦31368 Egypt¦31369—a night to¦31374 be¦31375 observed in that¦31373 same way by every¦31376 future generation¦31380.
\s1 Instructions for observing the ‘pass-over’
