\v 7 \add So\add* Mosheh¦33967 went out¦33966 to¦33968 meet¦33968 his¦33977 father-in-law¦33969, and he¦33970 bowed¦33970 down¦33970 and kissed¦33971 him¦33973. They¦33974 asked¦33974 each¦33975 other¦33977 about their welfare¦33978, then¦33966 they¦33979 went into the¦33980 tent¦33980,
\v 8 and Mosheh¦33983 related to his¦33984 father-in-law¦33984 all that Yahweh had done¦33989 to Far'oh¦33991 and to Egypt¦33992 on¦34002 account of¦33994 Yisrael¦33995, all the hardship¦33999 that found them along the way¦34002, and about how Yahweh had rescued them.
\v 9 Yetro \add ≈was very happy to hear about\add* all¦34009 the¦34011 good¦34011 \add things\add* that¦34012 Yahweh¦34015 had done¦34014 for Yisrael¦34016 when he¦34018 had rescued them out from¦34019 Egyptian control,
\v 10 and \add *he¦34022\add* said¦34022, “Bless Yahweh¦34025, who¦34026 rescued you¦34028 from \add slavery\add* in Egypt¦34030, and from Far'oh's power.
\v 11 Now I \add ≈can see\add* that¦34054 Yahweh¦34048 is¦34047 greater¦34047 than all the \add other\add* gods because¦34045 of the \add this result after\add* \add @the Egyptians\add* had¦34055 acted¦34055 proudly against¦34056 \add @you all\add*.”
\v 12 Then¦34058 Mosheh's father-in-law Yetro made a burnt¦34062 offering¦34062 as well as sacrifices¦34063 to God, and Aharon¦34066 and all¦34067 of the Israeli¦34069 elders¦34069 came¦34065 to eat¦34071 bread with¦34074 Mosheh's father-in-law \add ≈in God's presence\add*.
\s1 Yetro's helpful administrative advice
\rem /s1 Jethro's Wise Advice; The Appointment of Judges
\r (Deu. 1:9-18)
\p
\v 13 The¦34093 following day¦34082, Mosheh¦34084 sat¦34083 down¦34083 to¦34085 judge¦34085 the people, and¦34081 the people stood¦34089 around \add *him¦34093\add* from¦34094 morning¦34096 until¦34097 evening¦34099.
\v 14 When¦34101 \add *his\add* father-in-law¦34102 noticed¦34101 everything that he \add personally\add* did for¦34111 the people, he \add asked\add*, “What's¦34113 this¦34116 that you're¦34118 doing¦34119 with the people? Why¦34121 do you sit¦34123 \add there\add* alone¦34124, and¦34101 all¦34125 the people position themselves around¦34129 you from¦34130 morning¦34132 until¦34133 evening¦34135?”
\p
\v 15 “Because¦34140 the people¦34144 come¦34142 to me to \add ≈find out what God¦34146 wants for them\add*,” Mosheh¦34138 replied to \add @Yetro\add*.
\v 16 “When a \add difficult\add* situation comes up among them, someone comes to¦34154 me¦34154 and I make¦34160 the judgement¦34155 between¦34156 a man and his¦34159 neighbour¦34159. \add In addition,\add* I help them understand the god's statutes¦34163 and laws¦34167.”
\p
\v 17 “It's not¦34173 a good¦34175 \add idea\add* what you're¦34178 doing¦34179,” said¦34169 Yetro.
\v 18 “You're¦34192 definitely \add on the road to\add* wear yourself¦34182 out, plus you'll also wear out these people¦34188 who are with you, because¦34192 the \add responsibility\add* is too¦34194 heavy¦34194 for¦34195 you—you can't¦34197 \add just\add* do¦34200 it¦34200 alone¦34201.
\v 19 Now, listen¦34204 to \add this\add*: I'll advise¦34206 you and God will be with¦34205 you. You be before God for¦34212 the¦34214 people¦34212, and you should¦34207 bring¦34215 their disputes to God yourself.
\v 20 You¦34224 should \add also\add* teach them the statutes¦34228 and the laws¦34231, and you¦34232 should \add teach\add* them the way¦34236 they should \add live\add* and the work¦34241 that¦34237 they should do¦34243.
\v 21 \add But\add* you¦34246 yourself should search through all¦34247 the people¦34249 to find capable men who¦34257 are godly and faithful¦34256, and not \add greedy for\add* dishonest profit. Then¦34245 appoint¦34259 \add the best of them\add* to be over a thousand¦34262 \add >households\add*, and then¦34245 others to be over groups of one hundred¦34264, fifty, and ten \add >households\add*.
\v 22 They'll \add be the ones to\add* judge the people¦34273, except that every serious situation they'll bring¦34282 to you—they'll judge every minor situation themselves. \add Yes,\add* you must¦34270 lighten¦34291 this load off yourself¦34292, and¦34270 they'll share the load with¦34294 you.
\v 23 If¦34297 you do¦34301 that¦34300, and if God¦34303 tells you \add to proceed\add*, then you'll be able to endure¦34305 \add in the long term\add*, plus¦34302 all of these people¦34309 \add standing around\add* will be able to return to their¦34313 tents peacefully¦34315.”
\p
\v 24 \add So\add* Mosheh¦34318 \add ≈took his father-in-law's¦34320 advice\add* and¦34321 \add ≈implemented all of his suggestions\add*.
\v 25 \add *He¦34326\add* chose¦34326 capable men¦34328 from¦34331 among all¦34331 the Israelis¦34339, and he¦34334 appointed them¦34335 as heads over¦34337 the people¦34339: leaders of thousands¦34341, leaders of hundreds¦34343, leaders of fifties¦34345, and leaders of tens¦34347 \add of households\add*.
\v 26 They became the ones who settled most disputes for the people¦34352. They brought¦34360 the difficult¦34359 cases¦34358 to Mosheh¦34363, \add but\add* they¦34368 judged¦34368 each minor situation themselves.
\p
\v 27 Then¦34373 Mosheh¦34372 bid farewell to \add @Yetro\add* and¦34371 he¦34371 returned to his¦34380 own home.
