\p
\v 2 If you buy¦35330 a Hebrew¦35332 slave¦35331, he should serve¦35335 for six¦35333 years¦35334, then in¦35336 the seventh¦35336 year¦35336 he must¦35330 be set free without¦35339 having to¦35338 pay¦35339 you anything.\x + \xo 21:2-6: \xt Lev 25:39-46.\x*
\v 3 If he came¦35344 by himself, he can go¦35346 free by himself. If he's married with a wife, then his¦35353 wife must¦35344 be set free with him¦35354.
\v 4 If his¦35358 master gave¦35359 him a wife and she¦35363 bore¦35363 him sons¦35366 or¦35367 daughters¦35368, then the wife and her children¦35370 are her master's, and only the husband must¦35359 be set free.
\v 5 \add But\add* if¦35377 \add *he¦35380\add* plainly says, “I love¦35382 my master¦35385, my wife¦35388, and¦35389 my children¦35391 so I \add voluntarily\add* choose not¦35392 to be set free¦35394,”
\v 6 then¦35396 his master must take him to \add ?≈the place of worship\add*. He¦35411 must bring¦35401 him to a door¦35404 or¦35405 to a doorpost¦35408, and¦35396 his master must bore a hole his ear¦35413 with¦35414 an awl¦35414. Then¦35396 he¦35415 must serve¦35415 him \add for life\add*.
\p
\v 7 And¦35423 if a man¦35422 sells¦35421 his¦35425 daughter¦35425 as¦35429 a female¦35426 slave¦35426, she¦35428 \add ≈won't¦35427 be sent away\add* \add after six years\add* like the¦35430 male¦35430 slaves¦35430.
\v 8 If the man who¦35437 bought her \add ≈wanted her to become his slave-wife\add*, but¦35442 \add later\add* \add ≈he isn't¦35445 pleased with¦35449 her behaviour\add*, he must¦35442 allow her to be bought back by her \add ≈relatives\add*. He¦35447 has¦35449 no authority to sell¦35448 her on¦35449 to outsiders, because¦35449 that would \add ≈break the understanding of proper treatment\add* of her.
\v 9 Or if¦35453 he selected her \add ≈to become his son's¦35455 slave-wife\add*, then¦35453 he must¦35456 treat her \add ≈like a daughter¦35458 would expect to be treated\add*.
\v 10 If he \add ≈go on to take¦35466 another¦35465 slave-wife\add*, he mustn't reduce¦35473 \add @the first one's\add* food¦35469 or clothing¦35470, or her¦35469 marital¦35471 rights¦35471,
\v 11 \add but\add* if¦35475 he¦35481 doesn't provide these¦35479 three¦35477 things to her¦35482, then¦35475 she¦35483 can go¦35483 free without¦35484 \add ≈having to pay¦35484 anything\add*.
\s1 Rules about violence to others
\rem /s1 The Law concerning Violence; Laws about Violent Acts; Cases of Personal Injury; Personal Injuries
\p
\v 12 Anyone¦35489 who¦35489 strikes¦35489 \add #someone¦35490\add* and¦35491 \add #that person\add* dies¦35491, must¦35489 definitely be¦35493 put¦35493 to¦35493 death¦35493.\x + \xo 21:12: \xt Lev 24:17.\x*
\v 13 But¦35495 if \add ≈it wasn't premeditated\add*—if God¦35498 had let it happen¦35499 as an accident—then¦35495 I'll¦35501 assign a place¦35503 where¦35504 \add @the¦35498 one who hit them\add* can flee¦35505 to.\x + \xo 21:13: \xt Num 35:10-34; Deu 19:1-13; Josh 20:1-9.\x*
\v 14 \add However,\add* if \add #someone¦35512\add* \add ≈had schemed to trap and¦35509\add* kill¦35516 \add #their\add* neighbour¦35515, then \add even if the killer clings to\add* my altar¦35519, you¦35520 must¦35511 take¦35520 \add #them\add* for execution.
\p
\v 15 Anyone¦35524 who¦35524 strikes¦35524 \add #their\add* father¦35525 or mother¦35526 must¦35524 certainly be¦35528 put¦35528 to¦35528 death¦35528.
\p
\v 16 Anyone¦35530 who¦35530 abducts another person—either to sell¦35532 or to keep \add as a slave\add*—\add @that person\add* must¦35530 certainly be put¦35536 to death¦35536.\x + \xo 21:16: \xt Deu 24:7.\x*
\p
\v 17 Anyone¦35539 who¦35539 curses¦35539 \add #their\add* father¦35540 or mother¦35541 must¦35539 certainly be¦35543 put¦35543 to¦35543 death¦35543.\x + \xo 21:17: \xt Lev 20:9; Mat 15:4; Mrk 7:10.\x*
\p
\v 18 If¦35553 \add #two people\add* fight and \add #one\add* hits \add #their\add* neighbour¦35555 with a stone¦35556 or¦35557 with \add #their\add* fist¦35558, and \add #the injured one\add* doesn't die¦35560 but¦35546 is bedridden,
\v 19 if \add #that person\add* \add eventually\add* rises and can walk¦35567 about¦35567 outside¦35568 using a staff¦35571, then the \add #person who¦35573 struck¦35573 them\add* is free¦35572, other than \add ≈reimbursing the costs of¦35575 their lost work and their medical expenses\add*.
\p
\v 20 If a \add #master\add* hits \add #their\add* male¦35587 or¦35588 female¦35591 slave with¦35592 a staff and¦35593 \add @the slave\add* dies¦35593, then the death must¦35583 certainly¦35596 be¦35597 avenged¦35596.
\v 21 However¦35608, if \add @the slave\add* is able to stand within a couple of days¦35604, then the \add owner\add* won't¦35606 be \add ≈punished\add* because¦35608 \add @the slave\add* is \add #their\add* property¦35609.
\p
\v 22 If \add #two people\add* fight together¦35615 and strike¦35617 a pregnant¦35619 woman and \add ≈cause her¦35621 to deliver the baby\add*, if there's no serious injury, then he¦35626 must¦35615 certainly¦35625 be¦35626 fined¦35626 whatever the husband¦35630 of¦35630 the woman \add ≈demands\add* and the judges¦35633 allow.
\v 23 But¦35635 if¦35635 there's serious injury, then¦35635 you must¦35638 give¦35639 a life for a life,
\v 24 an eye for an eye, a tooth for a tooth, a hand for a hand, a foot for a foot,\x + \xo 21:24: \xt Lev 24:19-20; Deu 19:21; Mat 5:38.\x*
\v 25 a burn for a burn, a wound for a wound, and a bruise for a bruise.
\p
\v 26 If a \add #master\add* hits \add #their\add* male¦35675 or¦35676 female¦35681 slave's¦35675 eye and¦35682 damages it¦35682, \add #they\add* must¦35670 let \add @the slave\add* go free¦35684 in¦35685 compensation for the eye.
\v 27 \add Or\add* if¦35689 the \add #master\add* knocks¦35697 out a tooth of a male¦35692 or female¦35696 slave, \add #they\add* must¦35697 let \add @the slave\add* go free¦35699 in compensation for the tooth.
\s1 Rules about property
\rem /s1 Laws concerning Property; The Responsibility of Owners
\p
\v 28 If a bull gores a man¦35710 or¦35711 a woman¦35714 and¦35715 \add #they\add* die¦35715, the bull must¦35706 certainly¦35716 be \add ≈killed by throwing rocks¦35716 at it\add*. The¦35724 owner¦35724 of¦35724 the animal is \add to be considered\add* innocent, \add but\add* its¦35723 meat¦35723 mustn't be eaten¦35720.
\v 29 But¦35728 if¦35728 the bull had¦35734 a habit of goring in the past and its owner was warned¦35734 but¦35728 didn't¦35736 keep it \add fenced\add* in, and it kills¦35738 a man¦35739 or¦35740 a woman¦35741, then¦35728 the bull must be \add ≈killed by throwing rocks at¦35735 it\add* and its owner must also be put¦35747 to¦35747 death¦35747.
\v 30 If a ransom¦35751 payment is demanded instead, \add @the owner\add* may buy back \add #their\add* life¦35756 by paying the full amount demanded.
\v 31 Those rules also apply if the bull gores a \add person's\add* son¦35765 or daughter¦35769.
\v 32 If the bull gores a male¦35778 or¦35781 female¦35782 slave, \add @its owner\add* must give¦35787 thirty¦35785 silver¦35783 shekels¦35786 to¦35788 the \add slave owner\add* and¦35789 the bull must be \add ≈killed by throwing rocks at it\add*.
\p
\v 33 If¦35799 \add #someone¦35796\add* uncovers a \add pit for storage\add*, or digs¦35801 a \add new one\add* and leaves it uncovered, and a cow¦35809 or a donkey¦35811 falls¦35806 into it,
\v 34 the owner of¦35813 the pit must pay compensation¦35815. \add @The pit owner\add* must pay in cash¦35816, \add but\add* the dead¦35819 animal will then belong¦35820 to them.
\v 35 If \add #one person's bull\add* attacks \add someone else's\add* bull and it¦35827 dies¦35835, then they¦35836 should sell¦35836 the live¦35840 animal and divide¦35841 the money as well as dividing up the dead¦35848 animal.
\v 36 \add But\add* if it was known¦35852 that the bull had a habit of goring in the past and its¦35861 owner hadn't kept it \add fenced\add* in, then \add @that owner\add* must¦35863 certainly¦35862 give a live bull for the dead one, \add but\add* the dead animal will¦35868 become \add #theirs\add*.
\c 22
\s1 Rules about loss of property or farm animals
\rem /s1 Laws of Restitution; Protection of Property; Laws about Repayment
\p
\v 1 If¦35897 \add #someone\add* steals a cow or a sheep and slaughters it or sells it, he must¦35900 compensate five cattle for the cow or four sheep for the sheep.
\p
\v 2 (If a thief is discovered \add at night\add* while breaking in¦35923, and is struck and dies, \add ≈no one should be¦35922 charged for¦35923 murder\add*.
\v 3 But if it's¦35929 daylight already, then that person is guilty of murder.)
\p \add @A thief\add* who's caught must make¦35941 restitution¦35941, but if \add #they're\add* unable to pay, then \add @the thief\add* must be sold \add to cover it\add*.
\v 4 If¦35954 a thief is found in¦35959 the possession of a stolen animal, be it¦35958 a cow or a donkey or a sheep, then \add #they\add* must pay back another¦35960 matching animal as well.\f + \fr 22:4 \ft Or possibly, must pay back \+bd two\+bd* additional animals.\f*
\p
\v 5 If¦35984 a person releases their livestock to graze \add their own\add* field or vineyard, and it¦35973 \add ends up\add* grazing in the someone else's field, \add @the owner of the animal\add* must¦35971 compensate from the best of their field and from the best of their vineyard.
\p
\v 6 If¦35990 a fire breaks out and spreads in \add ≈the bush\add*, and grain that's already stacked or is still in the field gets burnt up, the person who started the fire must¦35992 certainly pay compensation.
\p
\v 7 If¦36024 \add #anyone\add* gives their neighbour money or goods for safekeeping, and if it is stolen from the neighbour's house¦36021, if the thief¦36017 is found¦36016, \add #they\add* must¦36016 pay double in¦36030 compensation.
\v 8 If¦36060 the thief can't be found, then the owner of the house must¦36059 be brought to \add ?≈the judges¦36072 for God's¦36065 decision\add* as to whether or not \add #they¦36071\add* have \add ≈improperly handled\add* \add #their¦36069\add* neighbour's¦36075 property.
\p
\v 9 Concerning every other squabble about any cattle or a donkey¦36086 or sheep¦36092, or about clothing or any lost property that's under dispute, both parties must go to \add ?≈the judges for God's decision\add*—anyone¦36082 who¦36105 God declares to be guilty must pay his neighbour¦36085 double as compensation.
\p
\v 10 If¦36109 anyone gives \add #their¦36112\add* neighbour¦36119 a donkey or a cow or a sheep or any animal to look after, and¦36120 it¦36120 dies or is injured or disappears without anyone seeing,
\v 11 both of them must declare in front of Yahweh as to whether or not they \add ≈improperly handled\add* \add #their\add* neighbour's property. The owner¦36132 must accept \add <the sworn statement\add* and¦36126 no compensation is required,
\v 12 \add but\add* if¦36135 it was truly stolen from \add @the neighbour\add*, \add #they\add* must¦36138 pay compensation to the owner.
\v 13 If it was truly \add ≈killed by a some other animal\add*, \add @the neighbour¦36152\add* must¦36149 bring \add @the remains\add* as evidence, and no compensation is required.
\p
\v 14 If¦36165 anyone borrows \add <an animal¦36173\add* from \add #their\add* neighbour and it gets injured or dies without the owner¦36167 being with¦36176 it, \add @the person who borrowed it\add* must¦36170 pay compensation,
\v 15 \add but\add* if its owner was \add there\add* with¦36190 it, no compensation is required—if it was for¦36194 hire, then that was included in its hiring fee.
\s1 More rules about everyday life
\rem /s1 Social and Religious Laws; Moral and Religious Laws; Social Responsibility
\p
\v 16 If¦36197 a man entices a young woman who's not engaged, and sleeps with her, he must¦36200 certainly pay the bride¦36206 price \add to her father\add* for her to become his wife.\x + \xo 22:16-17: \xt Deu 22:28-29.\x*
\v 17 If her father absolutely refuses to give her to him, \add @the man\add* must¦36213 \add still\add* pay the normal bride price in cash.
\p
\v 18 You must¦36224 execute any¦36217 woman who¦36219 practices sorcery.\x + \xo 22:18: \xt Deu 18:10-11.\x*
\p
\v 19 Anyone¦36232 who¦36228 has sex with an animal must¦36230 be¦36230 executed.\x + \xo 22:19: \xt Lev 18:23; 20:15-16; Deu 27:21.\x*
\p
\v 20 You must execute anyone who offers a sacrifice to any god other than Yahweh.\x + \xo 22:20: \xt Deu 17:2-7.\x*
\p
\v 21 You mustn't disadvantage or oppress any¦36250 \add ≈foreigner staying in your country\add*, because you were \add ≈foreigners staying\add* in the land of¦36250 Egypt.\x + \xo 22:21-22: \xt Exo 23:9; Lev 19:33-34; Deu 24:17-18; 27:19.\x*
\v 22 You¦36270 mustn't mistreat any widow or any fatherless child,
\v 23 \add because\add* if you¦36277 \add *do\add* and they¦36279 ask me to help them, I'll¦36277 help them
\v 24 and get angry. I'll cause you all to die in battle, and your wives will become widows and your \add #children\add* will become fatherless.
\p
\v 25 If¦36311 you loan money to any my people who are poor, you mustn't act like an \add impartial\add* creditor to \add #them\add*—you mustn't charge \add #them\add* interest.\x + \xo 22:25: \xt Lev 25:35-38; Deu 15:7-11; 23:19-20.\x*
\v 26 If¦36344 you actually take the your neighbour's \add ≈cloak¦36331\add* as a security, you must¦36334 return it to¦36339 \add #them\add* by sunset,\x + \xo 22:26-27: \xt Deu 24:10-13.\x*
\v 27 because it's \add #their\add* only covering. What else could \add #they\add* cover themselves with¦36352 when \add #they\add* try to sleep? If \add #they\add* cry out to me, I'll \add ≈act on¦36352 it\add*, because I'm compassionate.
\p
\v 28 You mustn't insult God, and¦36358 don't¦36359 curse your¦36362 leaders.\x + \xo 22:28: \xt Acts 23:5.\x*
\p
\v 29 You¦36370 mustn't \add fail to give\add* me¦36383 the best parts of the grain that you harvest, or \add the best\add* olive oil or wine that you produce.
\p You must¦36370 give your firstborn sons to me,
\v 30 plus¦36386 you must do the same with your cattle and your sheep and goats. The¦36398 young must stay seven days with \add #their\add* mothers, then you must give \add #them\add* to me on the eighth day.
\p
\v 31 You must be \add ≈different from others because you're dedicated to me\add*. \add So\add* you mustn't eat the meat of animals that are mauled in the countryside—throw it to the dogs \add instead\add*.\x + \xo 22:31: \xt Lev 17:15.\x*
\c 23
\s1 Rules about honesty and kindness
\rem /s1 Justice for All; Justice and Fairness; Laws of Justice and Mercy; A Call for Justice
\p
\v 1 You¦36402 mustn't present a false statement. Don't¦36401 conspire with¦36409 a wicked¦36411 \add >person\add* to¦36412 give false evidence.\x + \xo 23:1: \xt Exo 20:16; Lev 19:11-12; Deu 5:20.\x*
\v 2 Don't¦36417 \add just\add* follow the crowd into doing what's wrong¦36423, and¦36424 don't¦36417 \add just\add* say what others say in court \add if it's untrue\add*,
\v 3 and¦36435 don't¦36436 \add automatically\add* favour the poor¦36435 in¦36438 court either.\x + \xo 23:3: \xt Lev 19:15.\x*
\p
\v 4 If¦36441 you come across your enemy's¦36444 cow¦36443 or¦36445 donkey¦36446 going astray, you must¦36442 certainly¦36448 take¦36449 it¦36449 back to¦36450 \add #them\add*.\x + \xo 23:4-5: \xt Deu 22:1-4.\x*
\v 5 If¦36453 you see¦36455 that the donkey¦36456 of¦36456 someone who¦36457 hates¦36457 you has fallen to the ground under¦36459 its¦36460 load¦36460, you mustn't \add ≈just walk way\add*—you must¦36455 certainly¦36464 help \add #them\add* \add ≈with¦36466 it¦36466\add*.
\p
\v 6 You¦36470 mustn't deny justice¦36471 to the¦36471 poor in¦36473 \add #their\add* lawsuits¦36473.\x + \xo 23:6-8: \xt Lev 19:15; Deu 16:19.\x*
\v 7 Stay far way from deceptive words. You¦36478 mustn't kill¦36483 innocent or honest \add >people\add* because¦36484 I won't¦36481 \add allow\add* wicked¦36488 \add >people\add* to be¦36478 declared¦36487 innocent.
\v 8 You¦36493 mustn't accept \add #bribes\add*, because¦36493 \add #bribes\add* \add ≈stop people from being unbiased\add* and¦36497 \add #they\add* undermine the words¦36498 of¦36498 \add ≈honest\add* \add >people\add*.
\p
\v 9 You¦36506 mustn't oppress¦36503 \add #foreigners\add* since you \add #yourselves\add* know¦36505 \add ≈how that feels\add* because¦36510 you all were¦36513 foreigners in¦36514 Egypt¦36515.\x + \xo 23:9: \xt Exo 22:21; Lev 19:33-34; Deu 24:17-18; 27:19.\x*
\s1 The seventh year and the seventh day
\rem /s1 Sabbath Laws; Sabbatical Year and Sabbath; The Seventh Year and the Seventh Day
\p
\v 10 You should plant your¦36522 land¦36522 and¦36517 gather¦36523 its¦36526 produce¦36526 for six¦36517 years¦36518,\x + \xo 23:10-11: \xt Lev 25:1-7.\x*
\v 11 \add but\add* in the seventh¦36528 \add >year¦36528\add* you¦36529 must let¦36529 \add @the harvest\add* drop and \add just\add* leave¦36534 it there and \add allow\add* the poor people¦36533 to eat it. Then¦36528 the animals¦36536 in the countryside¦36537 can eat the reminder. You¦36540 must \add also\add* do¦36540 that to your vineyards¦36541 and your olive¦36542 orchards.
\p
\v 12 Six days You¦36546 should work¦36547 for six days, then on¦36548 the seventh¦36549 \add >day¦36548\add* you¦36550 must¦36546 rest so¦36551 that¦36551 your cow¦36553 and your donkey¦36554 can rest, and the \add #children¦36556\add* of your \add #slaves¦36558\add* and the foreigner \add staying in your land\add* can be refreshed¦36555.\x + \xo 23:12: \xt Exo 20:9-11; 31:15; 34:21; 35:2; Lev 23:3; Deu 5:13-14.\x*
\p
\v 13 \add ≈Take¦36566 careful notice of\add* everything that I've¦36564 told you all. You all mustn't \add invoke\add* other¦36569 gods¦36568—\add in fact\add* \add ≈don't¦36570 even mention their names\add*.
\s1 Three compulsory celebrations each year
\rem /s1 Three Annual Festivals; The Annual Festivals; The Three Great Festivals; The Three Annual Festivals
\r (Exo. 34:18-26; Deu. 16:1-17)
\p
\v 14 You¦36580 must¦36580 celebrate¦36580 me¦36581 three times every year¦36582.
\v 15 \add 1/\add* You must observe the \it Flat Bread Celebration\it*. You must eat¦36591 unleavened¦36592 bread¦36587 for seven¦36589 days¦36590 as¦36593 I commanded¦36594 you, in¦36600 late March or early April because¦36598 that's when you came¦36601 out¦36601 from¦36602 Egypt¦36602. You mustn't come without an offering.\x + \xo 23:15: \xt Exo 12:14-20; Lev 23:6-8; Num 28:17-25.\x*
\p
\v 16 \add 2/\add* You¦36614 must observe the \it \+add Spring\+add* Harvest¦36610 Celebration\it*, bringing the first¦36611 portion of¦36616 your¦36612 harvests¦36610 from what you¦36620 planted.
\p \add 3/\add* You must observe the \it \+add Finished\+add* Harvest Celebration\it* at¦36618 the end¦36618 of the year¦36619, after you've¦36620 finished harvesting \add ≈everything you planted\add*.
\v 17 \add So\add* all your¦36634 males¦36634 must¦36631 gather in¦36630 front of the master¦36638 Yahweh¦36640 three times¦36629 each year¦36630.
\p
\v 18 You¦36644 mustn't sacrifice¦36644 the blood¦36648 of my sacrifices¦36650 together with bread \add ≈that's been risen\add*, and¦36651 all the fat¦36654 \add must be burnt during the night\add* so that none remains¦36653 in the morning¦36659.
\p
\v 19 You must¦36664 bring¦36664 the \add ≈best\add* of the firstfruits¦36661 of your \add ≈harvests\add* to the house¦36665 of Yahweh¦36666 your god¦36667.
\rem /s1 The Conquest of Canaan Promised; A Promise of the Lord's Presence; Promises and Instructions; God's Angel to Prepare the Way
\p You mustn't boil¦36670 a young goat in¦36672 its¦36673 mother's¦36673 milk¦36672.\x + \xo 23:19: \xt Deu 26:2; Exo 34:26; Deu 14:21.\x*
\s1 Promises and teachings
\p
\v 20 Listen, I'm about¦36678 to send¦36678 a messenger¦36679 \add ≈in¦36682 front¦36680 of you\add* to guard¦36681 you on¦36682 the way¦36682, and¦36683 to take you to the place¦36686 that¦36687 I have¦36688 prepared¦36688.
\v 21 \add ≈Stay behind him\add* and¦36692 listen¦36692 to¦36693 \add ≈what he says\add*. Don't¦36694 \add ≈annoy\add* him because he won't¦36694 \add ≈put up with¦36693 disobedience\add* because \add ≈he represents my¦36703 authority\add*.
\v 22 \add But\add* if¦36707 you \add ≈take careful¦36709 notice of¦36711 his¦36711 words\add* and do¦36712 everything that¦36714 I say¦36715, then I'll be an¦36716 enemy¦36716 to¦36716 your¦36719 enemies¦36719 and I'll be against those who are against you.
\v 23 My¦36728 messenger¦36728 will¦36727 go¦36727 \add ≈ahead of¦36728 you\add* and take you to¦36731 \add face\add* the Amorites¦36733, the Hittites¦36734, the Perizzites¦36735, the Canaanites¦36736, the Hivites¦36737, and the Yebusites¦36738, and then I'll¦36739 annihilate¦36739 them.
\v 24 You¦36750 mustn't serve¦36746 or bow¦36743 down¦36743 to¦36744 their gods¦36744, and you mustn't do \add @what those people\add* do, because¦36750 you must¦36752 completely overthrow them¦36752 and completely smash their \add sacred\add* stone pillars¦36755.
\v 25 You all must¦36757 worship your god¦36760 Yahweh¦36759, and he'll bless¦36761 your food¦36764 and your water¦36767. I'll¦36768 keep sickness¦36769 away from you¦36770
\v 26 \add and\add* there won't be \add #any miscarriages¦36774\add* or \add #women\add* in¦36776 your land¦36776 that can't conceive. I'll¦36781 ensure \add ≈that you live your full\add* number¦36779 of days¦36780.
\p
\v 27 I'll \add ≈go¦36795 ahead of you and will terrify\add* and confuse¦36788 all of the people¦36793 \add groups\add* \add ≈whose lands you'll enter\add*, and I'll \add ≈cause them to¦36803 flee from you in battle\add*.
\v 28 I'll¦36806 send¦36806 hornets¦36809 \add ≈ahead of you\add* and \add #they'll\add* drive¦36811 out¦36811 the Hivites¦36814, the Canaanites¦36817, and the Hittites¦36820 \add ≈before¦36821 you get there\add*.
\v 29 I¦36824 won't drive¦36824 them out¦36824 \add ≈more than a year¦36826 ahead of¦36825 you¦36825\add* in case the area becomes¦36830 desolate and¦36833 the \add ≈wild animals¦36835 proliferate too much\add* in the countryside¦36836.
\v 30 I'll¦36846 drive¦36840 them out little by little \add ≈ahead of¦36841 you\add* until¦36842 \add ≈your¦36841 numbers increase\add* and¦36845 you take¦36845 over the¦36848 land¦36848.
\v 31 Then¦36850 I'll \add ≈help you establish\add* your¦36873 borders¦36853 from the Red Sea to the Mediterranean, and from the wilderness¦36861 to the \add Euphrates\add* river¦36864, because¦36865 I'll \add ≈help you defeat the existing\add* inhabitants¦36870 of¦36870 the land¦36871 and so you'll drive¦36872 them¦36872 right out¦36872.
\v 32 You¦36877 mustn't make¦36877 any agreement¦36880 with \add @those people\add* or with their¦36879 gods¦36879.
\v 33 They¦36883 mustn't live in your¦36884 land¦36884, in case they¦36887 cause¦36887 you to¦36889 sin¦36887 against me¦36889. Their¦36894 gods¦36894 would certainly become¦36897 a trap for¦36898 you, and then you'd \add end up\add* worshipping them.
\c 24
\s1 The people confirm their agreement
\rem /s1 Israel Accepts the Lord's Covenant; The Covenant Is Sealed; The Blood of the Covenant; The Covenant Confirmed
\p
\v 1 Then \add @Yahweh¦36909\add* told Mosheh¦36904, “Come¦36906 up¦36906 \add the mountain\add* to \add *me\add*—you, Aharon¦36911, Nadab¦36912, Abihu¦36913 and seventy¦36914 of the Israeli¦36916 elders¦36915, and you must¦36917 \add stay\add* at a distance¦36918 to worship¦36917.
\v 2 Then¦36925 Mosheh¦36921 alone¦36922 must¦36920 approach \add *me\add*, \add but\add* \add @the others\add* mustn't come near, and¦36920 \add also\add* the people¦36929 mustn't come up¦36931 with¦36932 him¦36932.”
\p
\v 3 \add So\add* Mosheh¦36935 went¦36934 and¦36934 explained to the people \add ≈everything that Yahweh had said¦36953, including¦36936\add* all the regulations. Then¦36934 all of the people answered¦36947 \add ≈in unison\add*, “We'll do everything that Yahweh has¦36959 \add ≈told us to do\add*.”
\v 4 \add So\add* Mosheh¦36964 wrote¦36963 \add ≈down everything that Yahweh¦36969 had instructed\add*. Then¦36963 he¦36963 got up early¦36970 in¦36971 the morning¦36971 and built¦36972 an¦36973 altar¦36973 at¦36971 the bottom of the mountain¦36975 and \add set up\add* twelve stone pillars¦36978 for¦36979 the twelve tribes¦36981 of Yisrael¦36982,
\v 5 and he sent¦36984 some young¦36987 men¦36987 to offer¦36990 burnt¦36991 offerings and to sacrifice¦36992 some bulls¦36996 as peace¦36994 offerings to Yahweh¦36995.
\v 6 Mosheh¦36999 took¦36998 half of the blood \add from the sacrifices\add* and¦36998 put it into basins¦37003. He sprinkled¦37006 \add the other\add* half of the blood onto the altar¦37009.
\v 7 Then¦37011 he¦37011 took¦37011 the scroll with¦37015 the agreement¦37013 \add written on it\add* and read it out loud to the people¦37016, and they¦37017 \add ≈responded¦37017\add*, “We'll obey¦37024 everything that¦37019 Yahweh¦37022 has¦37021 instructed.”
\p
\v 8 Then¦37026 Mosheh¦37027 took¦37026 the blood \add in the bowls\add* and¦37026 sprinkled¦37031 it¦37035 onto the people¦37034, telling \add <them\add*, “Look. \add This is\add* the blood \add confirming\add* the agreement¦37039 that¦37040 Yahweh¦37042 has¦37041 made¦37041 with¦37043 you according to \add ≈what you just heard\add*.”\x + \xo 24:8: a \xt Mat 26:28; Mrk 14:24; Luk 22:20; 1Cor 11:25; Heb 10:29; \xo b \xt Heb 9:19-20.\x*
\rem /s1 On the Mountain with God
\p
\v 9 Then¦37050 Mosheh¦37051, Aharon¦37052, Nadab¦37053, Abihu¦37054, and¦37050 seventy¦37055 Israeli¦37057 elders¦37056 climbed up \add the mountain\add*
\v 10 and¦37059 they¦37059 saw¦37059 the god¦37061 of Yisrael¦37062. Under¦37060 his feet¦37064 was something like a tile made of sapphire¦37067 that \add ≈looked so pure that it was as clear¦37070\add* as the sky.
\v 11 Those¦37076 Israeli¦37075 leaders saw¦37080 God¦37083 and \add ≈yet they weren't harmed by him.\add*. Then¦37072 they ate¦37084 and drank¦37085 \add together\add*.
\s1 Mosheh's long stay on Mt. Sinai
\rem /s1 Moses on Mount Sinai
\p
\v 12 Then Yahweh¦37089 told Mosheh¦37092, “Come¦37093 up¦37093 to me¦37094 on the mountain¦37095 and \add wait\add* there¦37098, and I will give¦37099 you some stone¦37104 slabs with the rules and regulations that¦37107 I have¦37108 written¦37108 \add on them so you can\add* to teach¦37109 them \add <to the people\add*.”
\v 13 \add So\add* Mosheh and his¦37114 assistant¦37114 Yehoshua set off, and Mosheh climbed up God's mountain¦37119.
\v 14 He had \add already\add* told the elders¦37124, “Wait for us¦37128 \add down here\add* in¦37129 this¦37129 place¦37129 until¦37130 we¦37133 return¦37133 \add here\add* to¦37134 you all. And see, Aharon¦37136 and¦37122 Hur¦37137 are¦37138 with¦37129 you—\add ≈any leader judging matters¦37142 requiring more wisdom can approach\add* them¦37144 \add for help\add*.”
\p
\v 15 Then¦37153 Mosheh¦37147 went to¦37148 \add the base of\add* the mountain and¦37151 a cloud¦37152 covered¦37151 \add over\add* \add *it\add*.
\v 16 Yahweh's¦37160 \add ≈radiance\add* settled¦37157 on Mt¦37163. Sinai¦37164, and¦37157 the cloud covered¦37165 it¦37157 for six¦37167 days¦37168, then¦37157 on the seventh¦37174 day¦37173 he¦37169 called¦37169 to¦37170 Mosheh¦37172 from¦37175 within the cloud.
\v 17 The¦37179 appearance¦37178 of Yahweh's¦37180 \add ≈radiance\add* was¦37181 like¦37181 a \add ≈roaring\add* fire¦37181 on¦37183 the top¦37183 of the mountain¦37184 \add ≈from what the Israelis¦37187 below could see\add*.
\v 18 Then¦37189 Mosheh¦37190 entered \add into\add* the cloud¦37192 and continued up the mountain¦37196, and \add *he¦37197\add* remained up there for forty full days.\x + \xo 24:18: \xt Deu 9:9.\x*
\c 25
\s1 Contributions for God's tent
\rem /s1 Offerings for the Sacred Tent; Offerings for the Tabernacle
\r (Exo. 35:4-9)
\p
\v 1 Yahweh¦37207 told Mosheh¦37210,
\v 2 “Tell the¦37216 Israelis¦37217 that they¦37218 should \add ≈raise funds\add* for me¦37220. You should collect a contribution¦37221 from¦37222 every¦37223 \add #family\add* that's willing to¦37220 donate.
\v 3 You should accept contributions¦37235 of gold¦37239, silver¦37240, or bronze¦37241,
\v 4 as well as yarn \add dyed\add* blue, purple¦37244, or crimson¦37245, plus¦37244 finely-spun linen¦37247 and¦37243 \add spun\add* goat¦37248 hair¦37248,
\v 5 and red-dyed ram skins¦37250, fine leather¦37253, and acacia¦37256 timber.
\v 6 \add Also\add* lamp oil, spices¦37260 for \add making\add* anointing¦37262 oil and¦37263 fragrant¦37264 incense¦37263,
\v 7 onyx¦37268 and \add other\add* gemstones for¦37271 setting¦37270 into the apron and the breastplate¦37272.
\v 8 Then¦37274 they¦37274 should make¦37274 me¦37275 a residence \add so\add* I¦37277 can live \add in it\add* among¦37278 them¦37278,
\v 9 according to the \add #patterns¦37286\add* that I'll show¦37283 you¦37284 for the residence and¦37288 for all of its¦37292 utensils. You all must¦37294 make¦37294 \add ≈all of it exactly to plan\add*.
\s1 The box to hold the stone slabs
\rem /s1 Plans for the Ark; The Ark of the Covenant; The Covenant Box; The Ark
\r (Exo. 37:1-9)
\p
\v 10 They¦37297 must¦37297 make¦37297 a wooden chest¦37298 from acacia¦37300 wood. It should be 125cm long¦37303, with the width and the height¦37309 being 75cm.
\v 11 It¦37312 must¦37311 be overlaid with pure gold \add sheeting\add* on the outside¦37316 and the inside¦37315, and have a gold moulding¦37320 \add fitted\add* all¦37322 around¦37322 it.
\v 12 Four¦37326 gold¦37328 rings must¦37324 be¦37335 cast and¦37324 attached to the¦37338 four¦37331 feet¦37332—two rings on¦37341 each side¦37343.
\v 13 Make¦37351 two \add carrying\add* poles¦37347 from acacia¦37349 wood and¦37350 overlay¦37350 them¦37351 with gold¦37352 \add sheeting\add*.
\v 14 Those poles¦37357 will¦37354 be inserted through the rings¦37358 on the sides¦37360 of¦37360 the chest¦37361 so it can be \add picked up and\add* carried¦37362 on them¦37366.
\v 15 The poles¦37371 must¦37370 be kept in the rings¦37368 on the chest¦37369—they mustn't be removed¦37373.
\v 16 The¦37379 \add ≈two stone slabs that¦37382 I'll¦37383 give¦37383 you¦37384 with our agreement inscribed on them\add* must¦37376 be placed inside the¦37381 chest¦37379.
\p
\v 17 You¦37386 must make¦37386 a lid the same length¦37392 and width as the chest. It must be made of¦37392 pure¦37389 gold¦37388 \add and will be known as\add* ‘the atonement¦37387 platform’.\x + \xo 25:17: \xt Heb 9:5.\x*
\v 18 Then¦37403 make a pair of winged-creatures from gold¦37400 that's hammered¦37401 from the two¦37404 ends¦37405 of the atonement¦37406 platform.
\v 19 \add Yes,\add* make¦37408 one winged-creature from each end of the gold lid material.
\v 20 \add *Their¦37436\add* up-lifted wings¦37433 should spread¦37432 out¦37432 covering¦37435 the atonement¦37439 platform and¦37430 they¦37448 should be¦37448 facing each¦37441 other across the top of¦37449 the platform.
\v 21 When¦37452 I¦37468 give¦37468 you¦37452 the stone slabs, put¦37452 them inside the box¦37458 and¦37452 then¦37452 fasten the atonement¦37455 platform on top to form the lid.
\v 22 Then¦37471 I'll meet¦37471 with¦37475 you there¦37473, and from¦37476 the atonement¦37477 platform between¦37478 the two¦37479 winged-creatures and above¦37482 the stone slabs, I'll be¦37482 able to tell you my commands¦37490 to pass on to the Israelis¦37495.
\s1 The bread offering table
\rem /s1 The Table for the Bread of the Presence; Plans for the Table; The Table for the Bread Offered to God; The Table
\r (Exo. 37:10-16)
\p
\v 23 You¦37498 must¦37498 make¦37498 a table¦37499 from acacia¦37501 wood: one metre long¦37503, 500cm wide¦37505, and 750cm high¦37508.
\v 24 Overlay¦37511 it with pure gold and¦37510 have a gold moulding¦37516 fitted all¦37518 around¦37518 it.
\v 25 Make¦37520 an 8cm wide rim around¦37524 it and¦37520 fit a gold¦37528 moulding¦37526 onto the rim.
\v 26 Make¦37538 four gold¦37536 rings¦37535 and¦37532 attach them near the legs on¦37541 the four corners¦37543 \add of the table\add*
\v 27 near the rim¦37549. \add @The rings¦37551\add* are so poles¦37553 can be inserted in order to carry¦37554 the table¦37557.
\v 28 Make¦37559 \add two\add* poles¦37562 out of¦37563 acacia¦37564 wood and¦37559 overlaid¦37565 them with¦37570 gold¦37567 for carrying¦37568 the¦37573 table¦37573.
\v 29 \add In addition,\add* make¦37575 the necessary plates¦37576 and pans¦37577, and the pitchers¦37578 and bowls¦37579 to be¦37581 used for pouring¦37581 out¦37581 \add <the wine offerings\add*—all from pure¦37584 gold¦37583.
\v 30 You¦37588 must¦37588 keep sacred bread¦37592 on¦37589 the¦37591 table¦37591 continually¦37595 in front¦37594 of¦37592 me.\x + \xo 25:30: \xt Lev 24:5-8.\x*
\s1 The lampstand
\rem /s1 The Lampstand; Plans for the Lampstand
\r (Exo. 37:17-24)
\p
\v 31 You¦37598 must¦37598 make¦37598 a lampstand¦37599 from pure gold. The¦37604 base¦37605 and shaft¦37606 are to be hammered¦37602 out \add of one piece of gold\add* as well as its cups¦37607 and \add decorative\add* buds and flowers.
\v 32 There must¦37615 be six¦37613 branches¦37614 extending from the¦37622 shaft—three on each side¦37626.
\v 33 Three¦37629 cups shaped like almond flowers must¦37632 be on¦37632 each branch¦37645 along with¦37632 a bud and petals.
\v 34 The¦37651 \add centre\add* shaft must¦37651 have four¦37652 cups¦37653 like¦37654 almond¦37654 flowers along with¦37651 a bud and¦37651 petals¦37656.
\v 35 For each of the three pairs of branches \add (one branch out each side in each pair)\add*, make a bud \add on the centre shaft\add* so each pair of branches appear to be growing out of it.
\v 36 These buds and¦37682 their branches¦37682 must¦37684 all¦37685 be¦37686 hammered¦37686 out from \add that\add* single piece of pure¦37689 gold¦37688.
\v 37 Make¦37691 seven¦37695 lamps¦37694 and¦37691 set them up¦37696 on it to give¦37700 light¦37700 out from the¦37703 front¦37704 of¦37699 it,
\v 38 and make all the utensils and dishes for the lamps from pure¦37709 gold¦37708.
\v 39 This can all¦37717 be made¦37714 from 35kg of¦37717 pure¦37713 gold¦37712
\v 40 and¦37723 be sure to make¦37723 it all like the pattern¦37724 that¦37725 you're¦37727 being¦37728 shown¦37728 on¦37724 the mountain¦37729.\x + \xo 25:40: \xt Acts 7:44; Heb 8:5.\x*
\c 26
\s1 Plans for the sacred residence
\rem /s1 Plans for the Tabernacle; The Tabernacle; The Tent of the Lord's Presence
\r (Exo. 36:8-38)
\p
\v 1 You must make \add &my\add* residence with ten¦37736 fabric panels formed by a skilled¦37746 craftsman. They must be made from twisted¦37739 finely-spun linen¦37738, with winged-creatures \add embroidered on them using\add* blue, purple¦37741, and scarlet¦37743 thread.
\v 2 The hanging panels must¦37754 all be the same size¦37762: 14.5m long and two metres¦37756 wide.
\v 3 Five¦37776 of¦37769 the panels will be coupled together \add as one set\add* and¦37776 the other five¦37776 as another.
\v 4 For each set¦37792, make¦37784 loops¦37785 of blue cloth and¦37793 fasten them along the outer¦37797 edge¦37795 of each of the end¦37791 panels—
\v 5 place fifty¦37806 loops on¦37804 the end¦37809 of¦37809 each set¦37812 so that¦37811 the loops are opposite¦37814 each other.
\v 6 You'll¦37827 also need to make fifty¦37822 gold¦37824 clips to join¦37825 the two sets together to make a single walled structure.
\p
\v 7 You¦37852 must¦37840 make¦37840 eleven panels with goats' hair¦37842 to form a tent¦37846 over¦37844 the¦37846 residence.
\v 8 Each of¦37854 the eleven panels must¦37858 be the same size¦37865: 15m long and¦37860 two metres¦37859 wide.
\v 9 You must¦37871 join¦37871 five¦37874 panels into a set, and¦37871 six¦37879 panels into the other set so that the sixth¦37886 panel can be doubled¦37882 over at the front¦37889 of¦37891 the tent¦37891.
\v 10 You¦37893 must¦37893 make¦37893 50 loops¦37895 to go onto the end of one¦37899 set, and¦37902 another 50 for the edge¦37905 of the other set.
\v 11 You'll also need to make fifty¦37913 bronze¦37912 clips to join¦37919 the two sets together¦37919 to make the tent¦37922 cover.
\v 12 Let the extra half¦37930 panel hang over¦37934 the back of¦37936 the residence.
\v 13 The half metre of extra length¦37943 on¦37942 each side that¦37939 extends beyond the linen sets will¦37946 hang \add evenly\add* over¦37948 the sides¦37950.
\p
\v 14 You¦37956 must¦37956 make¦37956 a covering of¦37959 rams¦37960 hides dyed¦37961 red¦37961 to go over the tent¦37958, then¦37956 another covering sea-cow hides to go over that.
\s2 The wooden frames
\rem /s1 The Framework
\p
\v 15 You¦37968 must¦37968 make¦37968 frames¦37971 for¦37972 \add &my\add* residence from acacia¦37974 wood. They'll stand¦37975 upright¦37975
\v 16 \add and\add* each one must¦37977 be¦37981 five metres¦37978 high and 0.75m wide.
\v 17 Each frame must¦37990 have two protrusions so it can be connected to the¦37990 frame next to it.
\v 18 Assemble¦38004 twenty¦38008 frames¦38006 for¦38007 the south side¦38010 of the residence
\v 19 along with forty¦38014 silver¦38017 bases to go underneath them—two bases supporting each frame, and¦38014 each frame having two protrusions at the bottom to go into the bases.
\v 20 For¦38039 the north¦38043 side, assemble twenty¦38044 frames¦38045
\v 21 along with forty¦38047 silver¦38049 bases—two under each frame.
\v 22 For¦38061 the rear of¦38061 the residence facing west, assemble six¦38065 frames¦38066
\v 23 plus two extra frames¦38069 for¦38071 \add bracing\add* the two back corners¦38071—
\v 24 they must¦38075 be joined \add on\add* at the bottom¦38077 and¦38075 then¦38075 both¦38091 of them have their¦38090 tops attached to a ring¦38086.
\v 25 Then¦38095 assemble eight¦38096 frames¦38097 with their¦38098 sixteen silver¦38099 bases—two bases supporting each frame.
\p
\v 26 Make¦38114 crossbars from acacia¦38117 wood—five¦38118 of¦38116 them to¦38119 go across the frames¦38119 on the northern side¦38120,
\v 27 five for the south side, and¦38125 five for the frames¦38134 at the rear of¦38135 the residence on¦38138 the western side.
\v 28 The middle¦38141 crossbar in¦38142 the centre of¦38143 the frames¦38143 will¦38144 go right through¦38144 from¦38145 one end¦38147 to¦38148 the other.
\v 29 Overlay¦38155 the frames¦38154 and the crossbars with gold, and make¦38160 the rings¦38159 for holding the crossbars with gold.
\v 30 Then¦38171 you must¦38170 assemble \add &my\add* residence exactly as per the plan¦38174 that¦38175 you were shown¦38176 on¦38177 the mountain¦38177.
\s2 The curtain
\rem /s1 The Curtain
\p
\v 31 You¦38191 must¦38180 have a skillful¦38189 workman make¦38180 a curtain¦38181 from twisted¦38187 finely-spun linen¦38186, with winged-creatures \add embroidered on them using\add* blue, purple¦38183, and scarlet¦38185 thread.
\v 32 Hang¦38195 it¦38195 from gold clips on four pillars¦38199 made from acacia¦38200 wood overlaid¦38201 with gold on four silver¦38210 bases¦38208.
\v 33 Hang the curtain under¦38216 the clips, then¦38212 bring¦38218 the box¦38223 that contains the agreement inside¦38220 the curtain. That curtain will separate¦38225 for¦38227 you¦38227 between¦38228 the sacred section and¦38212 the \em very\em* sacred interior.\x + \xo 26:33: \xt Heb 6:19; 9:3-5.\x*
\v 34 Install¦38235 the atonement¦38237 platform as the lid on¦38241 the box¦38239 there in¦38241 the very sacred¦38241 interior.
\v 35 Set the table up outside¦38248 the curtain¦38249 on the north¦38264 side, with the lampstand¦38252 on the south¦38258 side opposite¦38253 the table.
\p
\v 36 Then¦38266 make¦38266 a curtain out of twisted¦38275 finely-spun linen¦38274 for the entrance¦38268 of the tent¦38269. It should be embroidered with blue, purple¦38271 and¦38266 scarlet¦38273 yarn.
\v 37 Make¦38279 five pillars¦38282 for the curtain from acacia¦38283 wood overlaid¦38284 with gold. The hooks¦38287 on them must be made¦38279 of gold, and¦38279 their¦38287 five bases¦38292 must be cast with bronze¦38293.
\c 27
\s1 The altar for burnt offerings
\rem /s1 Plans for the Altar of Burnt Offering; The Altar of Burnt Offering; The Altar
\r (Exo. 38:1-7)
\p
\v 1 Make¦38297 an altar from acacia¦38301 wood that's 2.5m square¦38308 and 1.5m high¦38313
\v 2 and¦38323 as part of that same piece of wood, include \add a carved projection like\add* a horn on¦38317 each of the top corners¦38319 of the altar. Overlay¦38324 \add @the altar\add* with bronze¦38325.
\v 3 Make¦38327 pots¦38328 for the ashes¦38329 out of bronze¦38338, along with the necessary shovels¦38330, sprinkling bowls¦38331, meat forks¦38332, and¦38327 fire-trays¦38333.
\v 4 Then¦38340 make¦38340 a bronze grating¦38342 for¦38341 it¦38341 with four rings¦38351 of¦38351 bronze on¦38353 the four corners¦38355.
\v 5 Fit¦38358 it down onto a ledge¦38360 inside the altar so that it sits inside the altar about halfway up.
\v 6 \add Also,\add* make¦38369 poles¦38370 from acacia¦38374 wood for¦38371 the altar¦38371 and¦38375 overlay¦38375 them¦38376 with bronze¦38377.
\v 7 They'll be able to be put¦38379 into rings¦38383 on¦38383 two¦38388 sides¦38389 of the altar¦38390 for carrying¦38391 it¦38392.
\v 8 The altar should be hollow in¦38401 the middle and made¦38396 from \add wooden\add* planks just¦38398 like you¦38400 were shown on¦38401 the mountain¦38401.
\s1 The courtyard around the sacred tent
\rem /s1 Plans for the Courtyard; The Enclosure for the Tent of the Lord's Presence; The Courtyard; The Court and Its Hangings
\r (Exo. 38:9-20)
\p
\v 9 Make¦38406 curtains¦38414 of twisted¦38417 finely-spun linen¦38416 \add to form\add* a courtyard¦38408 \add around\add* the¦38409 residence. The¦38410 south¦38411 side¦38410 will require fifty metres¦38419 of curtain¦38414
\v 10 with twenty \add bronze\add* pillars and their¦38426 twenty bases¦38426. The¦38429 hooks¦38429 on the pillars and the clips must¦38425 be made of silver¦38432.
\v 11 Similarly on¦38437 the north¦38436 side¦38435, \add another\add* fifty metres of curtain¦38438 with¦38437 twenty¦38443 pillars¦38448 and their bases¦38444 and their hooks¦38447 and clips.
\v 12 The west¦38456 side¦38454 will¦38461 require twenty-five metres of¦38462 curtain¦38457 with ten¦38463 pillars¦38460 and¦38452 bases¦38462.
\v 13 The¦38465 east side¦38467 of¦38465 the courtyard¦38466 must¦38470 also be¦38470 twenty-five metres wide.
\v 14 Only side side of the gate, the curtain¦38476 will be 7.5m long with three pillars¦38478 and¦38473 bases¦38480,
\v 15 and¦38491 the same on the other side¦38483.
\v 16 The entrance curtain must be made of twisted¦38505 finely-spun linen¦38504. It'll be ten metres wide and hang on four pillars¦38508 with their bases¦38510, and must be embroidered with blue, purple¦38501, and scarlet¦38503 yarn.
\v 17 All the pillars¦38515 for the courtyard¦38516 must¦38518 have silver decorations around¦38517 them, as well as their¦38520 silver hooks¦38520 and¦38522 bronze¦38523 bases¦38522.
\v 18 \add So\add* the courtyard¦38526 will be fifty metres by¦38528 twenty-five metres and the curtain enclosure will be 2.5m high. All the curtains will be made of finely-spun linen¦38536, and the bases¦38538 under all the pillars will be bronze¦38539.
\v 19 All¦38541 the equipment for the operation of the residence and all¦38546 the tent¦38543 pegs and pegs for the courtyard¦38552 must¦38553 be¦38553 made of bronze¦38553.
\s1 The oil for the lamps
\rem /s1 Oil for the Lampstand; The Oil for the Lamp; Taking Care of the Lamp
\r (Lev. 24:1-4)
\p
\v 20 Order¦38559 the Israelis¦38562 to¦38564 bring¦38563 you¦38564 pure¦38567, pressed¦38568 olive¦38566 oil¦38565 for¦38569 the lamps so they¦38563 can be kept burning continually¦38572.
\v 21 In the outer portion of the sacred tent¦38574 (outside¦38576 the curtain¦38577 where the sacred box is¦38579 kept), Aharon¦38584 and¦38585 his¦38585 sons¦38585 must¦38582 attend to the lampstand in¦38590 front¦38590 of Yahweh¦38591 from¦38586 evening¦38586 to morning¦38589. This regulation¦38592 will¦38594 apply continually to all future generations¦38594 of Israelis¦38597.
\c 28
\s1 The priests' uniform
\rem /s1 Vestments for the Priesthood; The Priestly Garments; Garments for the Priests; Clothing for the Priests
\r (Exo. 39:1-7)
\p
\v 1 \add Now,\add* have your brother¦38606 Aharon and his sons brought¦38601 to you from¦38611 among¦38611 the¦38612 Israelis¦38613. Aharon, Nadab¦38618, Abihu¦38619, Eleazar¦38620, and Ithamar¦38621 will become my priests¦38614.
\v 2 Make¦38625 sacred¦38628 clothes¦38626 for Aharon¦38629—they must¦38625 be \add uniquely\add* beautiful¦38632
\v 3 \add so\add* speak¦38635 to all¦38638 the \add ≈skilled¦38640 craftsmen who've been given¦38644\add* the spirit¦38645 of¦38650 wisdom¦38646, and¦38634 they¦38647 can make¦38647 Aharon's¦38648 clothes¦38650 to \add ≈dedicate him¦38652 as¦38653\add* my priest¦38653.
\v 4 These¦38657 are¦38658 the clothes that¦38659 they'll need to make: a small pouch to hang around the neck and an¦38662 apron, a robe¦38663 and a tunic¦38664 of¦38664 woven work¦38665, and a turban¦38666 and a waistband. They¦38668 must¦38660 make \add those\add* sacred¦38671 clothes for your brother¦38673 Aharon¦38672 and for his¦38674 sons¦38674 to serve¦38675 as¦38675 my priests¦38675.
\v 5 \add @The skilled craftsmen\add* must¦38680 \add ≈be given\add* gold¦38683 \add wire\add* and blue, purple¦38689, and scarlet¦38692 yarn, and finely-spun linen¦38696.
\rem /s1 The Ephod; Design of the Ephod
\p
\v 6 They¦38700 must¦38699 make¦38699 the¦38702 sacred apron from twisted¦38709 finely-spun linen¦38708 with gold¦38703 \add wire\add* and blue, purple¦38705, scarlet¦38707 yarn embroidered onto it by a skilled¦38711 craftsman.
\v 7 It should have two shoulder¦38714 pieces attached to¦38719 \add the top\add* two corners so it¦38723 can be¦38723 fastened.
\v 8 Then¦38725 the waistband for the apron shall be¦38729 made with similar workmanship¦38729 from¦38730 twisted¦38738 finely-spun linen¦38737 with gold¦38732 \add wire\add* and blue, purple¦38734, and scarlet¦38736 yarn skillfully¦38726 embroidered into it.
\v 9 Then¦38740 get two¦38743 onyx¦38746 stones¦38744 and¦38747 engrave¦38747 the names¦38749 of Yisra'el's¦38751 \add twelve\add* sons¦38750 onto them—
\v 10 six names¦38754 on one¦38757 stone¦38756 and¦38758 six on the other, in their¦38767 birth¦38767 order¦38767.
\v 11 A¦38783 stone¦38771 craftsman with experience at making¦38787 signet rings should do the engravings¦38770, and then mount the two¦38777 stones¦38778 in decorative gold¦38786 settings¦38785.
\v 12 Then¦38790 attach the two stones¦38794 to the shoulder¦38796 pieces of the apron as a reminder about Yisra'el's¦38801 sons¦38800. That way Aharon¦38803 will¦38802 carry their¦38806 names¦38806 on¦38809 his two shoulders¦38812 as a reminder \add every time\add* he goes to face¦38807 Yahweh¦38808.
\v 13 Make¦38816 the decorative settings¦38817 for the \add two\add* stones from gold¦38818,
\v 14 and¦38829 then¦38820 make¦38825 two¦38820 twisted¦38824 chains¦38821 of pure¦38823 gold¦38822 like a cord that you¦38825 can attach to the decorative settings¦38836.
\s1 The chestpiece
\rem /s1 Design of the Chestpiece; The Breastpiece; The Breastplate
\r (Exo. 39:8-21)
\p
\v 15 Make a \bd sacred chest pouch\bd* for \add ≈important decision making\add*. A skilled¦38843 craftsman should make it from twisted¦38853 finely-spun linen¦38852 like the apron, embroidered with gold¦38847 \add wire\add* and blue, purple¦38849, and scarlet¦38851 yarn.
\v 16 It¦38858 needs to be 23cm square when folded¦38859 over double¦38859,
\v 17 and¦38865 it will¦38872 have four¦38869 rows¦38870 of¦38872 \add three\add* gemstones on it: a \add red\add* ruby¦38873, a \add yellow\add* topaz¦38874, a \add green\add* emerald¦38875,
\v 18 then¦38879 a turquoise¦38881, a \add blue\add* sapphire¦38882, a diamond¦38883,
\v 19 then¦38885 a \add red\add* jacinth¦38887, a \add white\add* agate¦38888, a \add purple\add* amethyst¦38889,
\v 20 and in the last row¦38891, a \add yellow\add* beryl, an¦38894 onyx¦38894, and a \add green\add* jasper¦38895. All those stones must¦38893 be¦38898 mounted in decorative gold¦38897 settings¦38899,
\v 21 and¦38901 \add so\add* they'll represent the twelve tribes¦38923 of Yisrael¦38908. The names of the twelve sons¦38906 must¦38902 be engraved¦38914 on the stones¦38901 similar to how signet rings \add are made\add*.
\v 22 Make¦38925 two twisted¦38930 chains¦38929 of pure¦38934 gold¦38933 like a cord that you¦38925 can attach to the pouch,
\v 23 then¦38936 make¦38936 two gold¦38942 rings and¦38936 attach them to the two \add top\add* corners of the pouch.
\v 24 Attach¦38955 the two gold¦38959 chains to the rings¦38963 on the corners
\v 25 with the other ends¦38971 fastened to the two decorative settings¦38978 \add around the stones\add* and¦38969 then¦38969 attached to the front¦38986 side of the shoulder¦38982 straps.
\v 26 Make¦38994 two more gold¦38992 rings¦38991 and¦38989 attach them¦38994 on¦38995 the inside¦39008 to¦39004 the two \add bottom\add* corners of¦39006 the pouch,
\v 27 and¦39010 another two gold¦39013 rings¦39012 to be attached to the bottom of the two shoulder pieces near where the shoulder straps join the apron just above¦39026 the crafted waistband¦39027.
\v 28 Then¦39031 tie the rings on the sacred pouch to¦39036 the rings on the apron with¦39040 a blue cord¦39040 so that the pouch sits above the waistband and¦39047 can't¦39047 come loose from¦39051 the apron.
\p
\v 29 Then¦39054 \add whenever\add* Aharon¦39055 enters the sacred inner room¦39070 \add to stand\add* in¦39062 front¦39072 of \add *me\add*, he'll be carrying the names¦39058 of Yisra'el's¦39061 sons¦39059 over¦39064 his¦39066 heart¦39066 on¦39062 the sacred pouch that'll be used for making decisions.
\v 30 Then¦39077 place the ‘Urim’ and¦39077 the ‘Tummim’\f + \fr 28:30 \ft These two objects appear abruptly in the text—they haven't been described previously—in fact, they're not described anywhere, so we don't really know much about them except that it seems they were used somehow to indicate Yahweh's decision on important matters. Although we presume that Mosheh must have already been familiar with them, sadly we're unable to translate these two Hebrew words, because we're not.\f* in the sacred pouch so they'll be¦39088 over Aharon's¦39092 heart when¦39093 he¦39093 goes¦39093 in before Yahweh, thus Aharon will¦39096 continually¦39109 bear¦39096 the decisions of¦39100 the Israelis¦39103 over his¦39106 heart before Yahweh.\x + \xo 28:30: \xt Num 27:21; Deu 33:8; Ezr 2:63; Neh 7:65.\x*
\s1 The other parts of the uniform
\rem /s1 Additional Clothing for the Priests; Other Priestly Vestments; Other Priestly Garments; The Other Priestly Garments
\r (Exo. 39:22-31)
\p
\v 31 Make¦39113 the \bd robe¦39115\bd* \add to be worn under\add* the sacred apron entirely¦39117 from blue material
\v 32 with¦39124 an opening for his¦39123 head¦39123 in the middle¦39124 of it. A border¦39125 will¦39126 be needed around¦39128 the opening so it can't¦39136 tear¦39137—this should be woven like openings¦39131 in regular clothes.
\v 33 Then¦39139 on¦39154 the bottom hem, make¦39139 \add decorations that look like\add* pomegranates—they must¦39139 be¦39154 made from blue, purple¦39145, and¦39139 scarlet¦39147 yarn. Gold¦39153 bells¦39152 should hang in¦39154 between¦39154 the pomegranates
\v 34 so as you go around¦39167 the hem of¦39157 the robe¦39166 there'll be a gold bell, then a pomegranate, then a gold bell, then a pomegranate, etc.
\v 35 Aharon¦39172 must¦39174 wear this robe when he's serving¦39173, so when he goes¦39176 into¦39177 \add *my\add* presence in¦39176 the sacred¦39179 inner room¦39179, I'll¦39181 hear¦39174 the bells when he enters and¦39169 when he leaves, so he won't¦39183 die¦39184.
\p
\v 36 Make¦39187 a \add ≈plate¦39188\add* from pure¦39190 gold¦39189 and engrave¦39191 ‘Dedicated to Yahweh¦39196’ on it like you would on a signet ring.
\v 37 Attach¦39199 it to¦39208 the front¦39210 of¦39213 the turban¦39213 using a blue cord¦39202
\v 38 \add so\add* it'll be on Aharon's¦39220 forehead. \add In this way,\add* it'll bear¦39221 the \add deficiencies of human\add* sinfulness when Israelis¦39230 \add attempt\add* to make sacred objects and \add attempt\add* to make sacred offerings. Thus it'll be on his forehead continually¦39239 so that¦39227 \add *I\add* will \add see it and\add* accept¦39240 those things¦39226.
\p
\v 39 The¦39246 \bd tunic¦39246\bd* must be woven¦39245 from finely-spun linen, and¦39245 similarly for the \bd turban¦39249\bd*. An embroiderer must make the \bd waistband\bd*.
\p
\v 40 You must¦39258 \add also\add* make tunics¦39259, waistbands and headbands for Aharon's¦39257 sons¦39256 so they'll \add look\add* splendid and beautiful¦39267.
\v 41 Put these clothes¦39269 on your brother¦39274 Aharon¦39273 and his¦39277 sons¦39277, then¦39269 anoint¦39279 them \add with oil\add* and ordain¦39281 and consecrate¦39285 them as¦39287 priests¦39287 to¦39288 me¦39288.
\v 42 Make¦39290 \bd underwear¦39292\bd* out of¦39292 linen¦39294 to cover¦39295 their bare flesh¦39296 from¦39298 the waist¦39298 to the thighs¦39301—
\v 43 they must wear it whenever they enter the sacred¦39323 tent¦39323 and whenever they approach¦39318 the altar¦39321 to¦39322 offer sacrifices so they won't¦39324 be disobeying and die¦39328. Aharon¦39307 and his¦39332 descendants¦39332 must obey that rule forever¦39330.
\c 29
\s1 Consecrating the priests
\rem /s1 Instructions for Ordaining Aaron and His Sons as Priests; The Ordination of the Priests; Dedication of the Priests; Consecration of the Priests
\r (Lev. 8:1-36)
\p
\v 1 Now this¦39336 is¦39337 how you¦39340 should consecrate¦39342 them to be priests¦39344 to me¦39345: Take¦39346 a young bull¦39347 from the¦39351 herd¦39351 and¦39352 two¦39353 rams¦39352 without¦39354 defects¦39354.
\v 2 \add Also,\add* using finely-ground wheat¦39367 flour¦39366, \add bake\add* flat bread¦39356, flat cakes¦39358 with \add olive\add* oil in¦39361 them, and¦39356 flat wafers¦39362 sprinkled with \add olive\add* oil.
\v 3 Put¦39371 \add @the baking\add* into a basket, and bring¦39377 the basket \add to me\add* along with¦39379 the bull¦39382 and two¦39384 rams¦39385.
\p
\v 4 Then¦39387 take Aharon¦39389 and his¦39392 sons¦39392 near¦39393 to¦39394 the entrance¦39396 of¦39396 the sacred¦39397 tent¦39397 and wash¦39399 them¦39400 with¦39401 water¦39401.
\v 5 Take¦39403 the clothes¦39407 and dress Aharon¦39410 in¦39425 the tunic¦39413 and the robe¦39415 that goes with¦39425 the apron, then¦39403 the sacred apron and the sacred pouch, and tie the waistband around the apron.
\v 6 Put¦39434 the turban¦39429 on¦39430 his¦39432 head¦39432 and¦39433 attach the sacred¦39437 \add gold\add* ornament to it,
\v 7 then¦39442 take¦39442 the anointing¦39446 oil¦39445 and¦39442 pour¦39447 it¦39452 over his¦39450 head¦39450 to dedicate him¦39452.
\v 8 \add Next,\add* bring¦39457 his¦39456 sons¦39456 and¦39458 put tunics¦39459 on them¦39458
\v 9 and wrap waistbands around them and tie on¦39466 their headbands. They¦39462 \add and their descendants\add* will¦39469 be priests¦39471 forever¦39473, and you¦39474 must¦39461 ordain¦39474 \add both\add* Aharon¦39464 and his sons¦39480.
\p
\v 10 Then¦39482 bring¦39482 the bull¦39485 to the front¦39486 of the sacred¦39487 tent¦39487 and get Aharon¦39490 and his¦39491 sons¦39491 to put their¦39494 hands¦39494 on¦39495 its head¦39497,
\v 11 then¦39500 slaughter¦39500\f + \fr 29:11 \ft The throat will be cut with a sharp knife, and the blood will be caught in a bowl.\f* the bull¦39503 before¦39504 \add *me\add* at the entrance¦39506 of¦39506 the sacred¦39507 tent¦39507.
\v 12 Take¦39519 some¦39511 of that blood¦39511 and¦39510 smear it on the top projections of the altar with¦39518 your¦39518 finger¦39518, then¦39510 pour¦39524 the rest of it \add on the ground\add* at¦39518 the base¦39527 of the altar.
\v 13 Take¦39530 all the fat covering¦39536 the innards including¦39540 the lobe¦39541 above the liver¦39544, as well as both¦39546 kidneys¦39547 and the fat on them¦39552, and burn¦39553 it all on the altar¦39554,
\v 14 but¦39556 the bull's meat¦39558 and hide¦39562 and intestines must¦39566 be burnt¦39566 outside¦39568 the camp¦39569. Sacrificing \add the bull's fat like that\add* is an act of purification.
\p
\v 15 Then¦39573 take¦39577 one of the rams and have Aharon¦39579 and his¦39580 sons¦39580 place their¦39583 hands¦39583 on¦39584 \add *its\add* head¦39586,
\v 16 then¦39589 slaughter¦39589 it. \add Catch\add* the blood¦39596 and¦39589 sprinkle¦39597 it all over \add the sides of\add* the altar¦39600.
\v 17 Then¦39603 cut¦39606 the ram¦39605 into¦39607 pieces and wash¦39608 its innards and its legs¦39610, and put¦39611 \add @those bits\add* \add together\add* with¦39612 the head¦39617 and the \add other\add* pieces.
\v 18 Then¦39619 burn¦39619 \add *it\add* all¦39622 on the altar¦39625. That will be a burnt¦39626 offering to \add *me\add*—a pleasing¦39630 aroma¦39629 \add from\add* the offering by fire¦39631.\x + \xo 29:18: \xt Eph 5:2; Php 4:18.\x*
\p
\v 19 Then¦39635 take¦39635 the¦39637 second¦39638 ram¦39637, and have Aharon¦39640 and his¦39641 sons¦39641 place their¦39644 hands¦39644 on¦39645 \add *its\add* head¦39647,
\v 20 then¦39650 slaughter¦39650 it. \add Catch\add* the blood¦39655 and smear some¦39655 of it on the tip of Aharon¦39661 and his sons' right ears and on their right thumbs¦39670 and big¦39675 toes. Then¦39650 sprinkle¦39678 the rest all over \add the sides of\add* the altar¦39684.
\v 21 Take¦39687 some of the blood¦39690 that is¦39692 on the altar¦39694 and some of the anointing¦39696 oil¦39695, and sprinkle¦39697 that on Aharon and his clothes, and on his sons and their clothes. That will¦39712 consecrate¦39712 Aharon and his sons and their \add priestly\add* clothes.
\p
\v 22 Take¦39720 the fat of \add that second\add* ram, and its fat tail¦39725, the fat covering¦39730 its innards and the lobe¦39735 above the liver¦39736, the two¦39739 kidneys¦39740 and that fat that's on¦39745 them¦39745, and the right¦39748 thigh¦39747, \add because\add* this ram is for consecration.
\v 23 \add Also\add* take one \add round\add* bread loaf¦39754, one cake¦39757 of bread made with oil¦39759 in it, and¦39754 one wafer¦39761 from¦39763 that basket¦39763 of flat breads¦39764 that's \add offered to\add* \add *me\add*.
\v 24 Put¦39781 all those into the hands of¦39772 Aharon¦39773 and his sons¦39776, and \add they\add* must¦39769 wave them¦39778 before¦39780 \add *me\add* as a wave offering.
\v 25 Then¦39783 take¦39783 them from¦39785 their¦39785 hands¦39785 and burn¦39786 them completely on the altar¦39787 on top of the burnt¦39790 offering. That also will be a fire¦39795 offering to¦39797 \add *me\add*, and its aroma¦39791 will please \add *me\add*.
\p
\v 26 Take¦39811 the breast¦39802 meat of the ram¦39803 (\add the second one\add* for the consecration of Aharon¦39806) and wave it as a wave offering¦39804 before¦39810 \add *me\add*. Then¦39799 after that¦39805 it will be your share \add to cook and eat\add*.
\p
\v 27 \add So\add* you¦39816 should consecrate¦39816 the breast¦39819 of the wave offering¦39820 and the thigh¦39822 of the contribution¦39823 from¦39828 the ram¦39828 of consecration¦39816 for Aharon¦39831 and his¦39833 sons¦39833.
\v 28 Those will belong to Aharon¦39836 and¦39835 his¦39837 sons¦39837 as their regular portion¦39838 from the Israelis—it will be¦39848 a contribution from the Israelis from their peace¦39854 offerings¦39854 to \add *me\add*.
\p
\v 29 The¦39870 sacred¦39859 clothes¦39858 that¦39860 belong¦39862 to Aharon¦39861 will¦39862 be used for his male descendants to wear \add in turn\add* when they're anointed¦39865 and¦39858 consecrated.
\v 30 The¦39877 priest¦39877 who succeeds¦39878 him¦39878 from among his¦39878 sons¦39879, who comes¦39881 into¦39882 the sacred tent¦39887 to¦39886 serve¦39886 in¦39887 the sacred inner room¦39878 must¦39876 wear¦39876 them for seven days¦39875.
\p
\v 31 Take¦39892 the¦39890 ram¦39890 of consecration and¦39893 boil¦39893 its¦39896 meat¦39896 in¦39897 a sacred¦39898 place¦39897,
\v 32 then¦39900 Aharon¦39901 and¦39900 his¦39902 sons¦39902 should eat¦39900 the meat¦39905 of¦39905 the ram¦39906 as well as the breads¦39909 that¦39910 are in¦39911 the basket¦39911 at¦39911 the entrance¦39912 to the tent¦39913.
\v 33 They¦39916 must¦39916 eat those things¦39933 used to make atonement¦39919 for them and¦39916 to consecrate¦39925 them. No one else can eat them, because¦39931 they've been made sacred¦39933.
\v 34 If¦39948 any meat¦39939 of the consecration or any of the bread¦39943 remains until¦39944 the morning¦39946, then¦39936 you¦39947 must¦39938 burn¦39947 the remainder—it can't¦39952 be¦39953 eaten¦39953 because¦39954 it's¦39957 been made sacred¦39956.
\p
\v 35 \add So\add* that's what you must¦39959 do¦39959 for Aharon¦39960 and¦39959 his sons¦39961, just like I've¦39966 commanded¦39966 you. You'll consecrate them for seven¦39968 days¦39969,
\v 36 and each day you¦39975 must sacrifice a bull¦39973 to purify¦39980 the altar and compensate for the altar's¦39983 imperfections. You¦39987 must also anoint¦39986 it \add each day with olive oil\add* to make¦39984 it sacred.
\v 37 After¦39997 making atonement¦39992 for the¦39995 altar¦39995 and making it sacred for seven days¦39991, it will then be so sacred that anything that touches¦40004 it will also become sacred.
\s1 The daily sacrifices
\rem /s1 The Daily Offerings
\r (Num. 28:1-8)
\p
\v 38 \add After all that,\add* you¦40011 must¦40011 continually¦40021 offer¦40011 two¦40019, year-old lambs¦40015 every day¦40020—
\v 39 one¦40026 in¦40028 the morning¦40028 and¦40029 the other around twilight¦40034.
\v 40 With¦40039 the¦40041 \add ≈morning\add* lamb¦40047, \add also\add* offer¦40043 2kg of finely-ground wheat flour¦40037 mixed¦40038 with¦40039 a litre of pressed¦40040 olive oil¦40039 and¦40043 a litre of wine¦40046.
\v 41 With the twilight¦40055 lamb¦40051, offer¦40053 the same grain¦40056 offering as in the morning¦40057, and the same drink¦40058 offering. That will be a fire¦40064 offering to¦40065 \add *me\add* and the aroma¦40062 will please me.
\v 42 This¦40075 must¦40077 be a continual¦40068 offering¦40067 to \add *me\add* at the entrance¦40070 of the sacred¦40071 tent¦40071 \add even\add* in¦40074 following generations¦40069, and I'll¦40077 meet¦40077 with you all there to speak¦40080 to you there
\v 43 and I'll¦40084 meet¦40084 with¦40089 the¦40086 Israelis¦40087 there¦40085, and my splendour¦40089 will sanctify it¦40088.
\v 44 I'll consecrate¦40091 the sacred¦40094 tent¦40094 and the altar¦40098, and I'll sanctify Aharon¦40101 and his¦40104 sons¦40104 to be my priests¦40106.
\v 45 Then¦40109 I'll¦40113 live \add there\add* among the Israelis¦40112 and¦40113 I will¦40113 be¦40113 their god¦40115,
\v 46 and¦40117 they¦40117 will¦40117 know¦40117 that¦40118 I'm their god Yahweh, who¦40122 brought¦40123 them out of¦40125 Egypt¦40126 so I could live among¦40128 them. I am¦40130 Yahweh their god.
\c 30
\s1 The incense altar
\rem /s1 The Altar for Burning Incense; The Altar of Incense; Plans for the Incense Altar
\r (Exo. 37:25-28)
\p
\v 1 You¦40141 must¦40134 make¦40134 an¦40135 altar¦40135 out of¦40138 acacia¦40139 word for burning incense¦40137 on.
\v 2 It¦40148 should be square¦40147—half a metre on each side—and a metre high¦40150. Make projections that look like horns¦40152 on top out of that same block of wood.
\v 3 It¦40155 must¦40154 be overlaid¦40154 with pure gold—its top¦40160 and all around its sides¦40163 and its projections—plus¦40161 you¦40168 shall make¦40168 a gold moulding¦40170 all around it.
\v 4 Make¦40195 two¦40174 gold¦40176 rings¦40175 and¦40174 attach them to the altar below the moulding¦40182—one on¦40196 each side to hold the poles¦40193 for carrying¦40194 it¦40195 with¦40196.
\v 5 Make¦40198 the¦40201 two poles¦40201 from acacia¦40203 wood and¦40204 overlay¦40204 them¦40205 with gold¦40206.
\v 6 Put this incense altar outside the curtain¦40211 that¦40212 hangs in¦40210 front¦40210 of the sacred chest¦40215—the box¦40215 containing the stone slabs and¦40208 with a platform on¦40220 it where¦40223 I will¦40224 meet¦40224 with you.
\p
\v 7 Aharon¦40236 must¦40228 burn fragrant incense¦40231 on¦40233 this altar every morning when¦40235 he¦40235 looks after the¦40238 lamps¦40238
\v 8 and¦40241 again every evening¦40247 when¦40241 he¦40248 \add ≈lights\add* the¦40251 lamps¦40245, \add so\add* incense¦40249 must¦40248 be continually¦40250 burning before¦40251 \add *me\add* throughout¦40253 your generations¦40253.
\v 9 Don't¦40264 offer¦40257 up¦40257 any incense¦40259 or burnt¦40261 offering or grain¦40262 offering on it that's different from these instructions, and don't¦40264 pour¦40265 a drink¦40263 offering on it.
\v 10 Once a year¦40274, Aharon¦40269 must¦40268 take the purifying blood¦40275 from¦40275 the annual atonement¦40277 sacrifice and¦40268 put it on¦40274 the projections of the altar to compensate for the altar's imperfection. Each high priest will cover the altar's flaws this way throughout¦40282 all future generations¦40282. \add @The altar\add* will be very sacred¦40283—dedicated to \add *me\add*.
\s1 The tax for making the tent
\rem /s1 The Tax for the Tent of the Lord's Presence; Money for the Tabernacle; The Half Shekel for the Sanctuary; Atonement Money
\p
\v 11 Then Yahweh¦40291 told Mosheh¦40294:
\v 12 Whenever¦40304 you¦40298 \add ≈take a census of¦40301 the Israeli¦40302 men\add*, each man must¦40298 pay a ransom¦40309 to¦40306 Yahweh¦40311 for¦40311 his life¦40310 when¦40312 they're counted, and¦40314 then the counting won't¦40314 cause \add ≈deadly diseases\add* among¦40317 them.
\v 13 As each man crosses¦40327 over¦40327 to stand with¦40333 those¦40330 who've been counted, he must¦40324 pay¦40324 give a half-shekel coin as an offering to \add *me\add*. (That's half of the official shekel weight of 12 grams.)\x + \xo 30:13: \xt Exo 38:25-26; Mat 17:24.\x*
\v 14 Every¦40354 man who's¦40344 twenty¦40349 or older must¦40352 pay¦40352 this contribution¦40353 to¦40345 \add *me\add* when he's¦40352 counted—
\v 15 the wealthy¦40356 aren't¦40357 expected to pay more, and¦40360 the poor¦40360 must¦40359 not pass less than this amount when they pay this ransom to \add *me\add* to make¦40370 atonement¦40370 for your¦40373 lives¦40373.
\v 16 Then¦40375 you must¦40375 take¦40375 that money¦40378 from¦40380 the Israelis¦40382 and¦40375 use it for work¦40387 on¦40385 the sacred¦40388 tent¦40388—it will be a reminder to \add *me\add* that you all have made¦40396 atonement¦40379 for your lives¦40399.
\s1 The washbasin
\rem /s1 Basin for Washing; Plans for the Washbasin; The Bronze Basin
\p
\v 17 The Yahweh¦40403 told Mosheh¦40406:
\v 18 Make¦40409 a bronze washbasin with a bronze base. Place it between¦40417 the sacred¦40419 tent¦40419 and the altar¦40422, and fill it with water¦40425\x + \xo 30:18: \xt Exo 38:8.\x*
\v 19 and Aharon¦40428 and his¦40429 sons¦40429 must¦40427 wash¦40427 their¦40433 hands¦40433 and feet¦40436 in it.
\v 20 Before¦40456 they go¦40438 into¦40439 the sacred¦40441 tent¦40441, they must¦40443 wash¦40443 with¦40449 water¦40445 so that won't¦40446 die¦40447, and¦40446 \add also\add* when¦40449 they're going to the altar¦40452 to burn offerings¦40455 to \add *me\add*—
\v 21 they must¦40458 wash¦40458 their hands¦40459 and feet¦40460 so they won't¦40461 die¦40462. This will be¦40463 a rule for them¦40464 and for all their descendants¦40469 throughout¦40470 the generations¦40470.
\s1 The anointing oil
\rem /s1 The Anointing Oil; The Anointing Oil and Incense; Anointing Oil
\p
\v 22 Then Yahweh¦40474 told Mosheh¦40477:\x + \xo 30:22-38: \xt Exo 37:29.\x*
\v 23 Then¦40480 you yourself¦40483 must collect these spices¦40484: 6kg of \add fragrant sap\add* named myrrh¦40486, then¦40480 3kg of fragrant cinnamon¦40491, 3kg of fragrant cane¦40497,
\v 24 and¦40508 6kg of fragrant casia bark. (Use the¦40507 official weight for everything.) Also¦40503, four litres of olive¦40509 oil¦40508.
\v 25 Get¦40513 a professional perfumer¦40521 to make¦40512 this into a sacred¦40517, fragrant, anointing¦40515 oil¦40522.
\v 26 Use it¦40529 to anoint¦40528 the sacred¦40532 tent¦40532 and¦40534 the box¦40535 containing the stone slabs,
\v 27 as well as the table¦40540 and all¦40543 its utensils, the lampstand¦40548 and its utensils, the incense¦40554 altar¦40553,
\v 28 the altar¦40558 for burnt¦40559 offerings¦40559 and all¦40562 its equipment, and the basin¦40567 and its base¦40570.
\v 29 That¦40573 will make them sacred and dedicated to me, and anything that¦40579 touches¦40579 them will also become sacred.
\p
\v 30 Then¦40583 anoint¦40589 Aharon¦40585 and his¦40588 sons¦40588, and dedicate them¦40591 to become my priests¦40592.
\v 31 Approach the¦40597 Israelis¦40598 and¦40595 tell them, “This \add recipe\add* will be¦40605 my sacred¦40604 anointing¦40602 oil¦40601 for all generations¦40608.
\v 32 It must not be poured¦40615 on¦40616 anyone's body¦40612, and you all must not use any similar recipe \add for yourselves\add*—it's sacred¦40620 and \add ≈must be treated as such\add*.
\v 33 Anyone¦40626 who mixes together anything like¦40629 that¦40630, or who puts any of it on¦40633 a stranger¦40635, must¦40628 be¦40636 \add ≈expelled from¦40637 the community\add*.
\s1 The incense
\rem /s1 The Incense; Incense
\p
\v 34 Then Yahweh¦40641 told Mosheh¦40644: Collect equal amounts¦40657 of stacte¦40649, onycha¦40651, and galbanum¦40652 spices, and pure¦40655 frankincense¦40654,
\v 35 and get a professional ointment¦40663 mixer to add salt and make¦40660 it into pure¦40668 incense¦40662 that will be sacred¦40668.
\v 36 Pulverise¦40672 some of it into a fine¦40670 powder and¦40670 put it in¦40677 front¦40675 of the box containing the stone slabs inside the sacred tent¦40677. This¦40680 incense must¦40670 be¦40685 regarded as being very sacred.
\v 37 No¦40698 one else should make any incense¦40688 for¦40694 yourselves¦40697 with¦40691 this same recipe—it¦40696 must¦40690 be¦40696 regarded as sacred¦40695 to \add *me\add*.
\v 38 Anyone¦40700 who¦40701 makes¦40703 anything similar for its fragrance must¦40703 be¦40707 \add ≈expelled from¦40708 the community\add*.
\c 31
\s1 Bezalel and Oholiab
\rem /s1 Craftsmen for the Tent of the Lord's Presence; Bezalel and Oholiab; Craftsmen: Bezalel and Oholiab
\r (Exo. 35:30–36:1)
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh¦40712 told Mosheh¦40715:
\v 2 Listen, I've¦40719 chosen a man named Bezalel¦40721 (son of Uri¦40724, son of Hur¦40727, from Yehudah's¦40729 descendants)
\v 3 and I've¦40732 filled¦40731 him with \add *my\add* spirit¦40733 with wisdom¦40735, ability¦40736, knowledge¦40737, and general craftsmanship.
\v 4 \add He's capable of\add* designing¦40743 and creating gold¦40745, silver¦40746, and bronze¦40747 works,
\v 5 as well as cutting¦40749 and setting¦40751 gemstones, wood-carving, and other crafts.
