\r (Exo. 25:1-9)
\p
\v 4 Then Mosheh¦42822 told¦42836 all¦42825 the assembled Israelis¦42830, “This¦42832 is¦42833 what Yahweh¦42837 instructed¦42836:
\v 5 Take up an offering for Yahweh—everyone¦42844 \add ≈who wants to participate should¦42847\add* bring¦42847 to Yahweh their contribution¦42849 of¦42849 gold¦42851, silver¦42852, bronze¦42853,
\v 6 blue, purple¦42856, or scarlet¦42858 yarn, finely-spun linen¦42859 and¦42855 goats' hair¦42860,
\v 7 reddened ram skins¦42862, sea-cow hides¦42862, and¦42862 acacia¦42868 wood,
\v 8 \add olive\add* oil for¦42872 the lamps and spices¦42873 to make the anointing¦42875 oil and the fragrant¦42877 incense¦42876,
\v 9 and onyx¦42881 and other gemstones for the \add priest's\add* sacred apron and chest pouch.
\s1 The equipment for the holy tent
\rem /s1 Articles for the Tent of the Lord's Presence
\r (Exo. 39:32-43)
\p
\v 10 Everyone¦42895 among¦42892 you¦42892 all who's \add ≈highly skilled¦42889\add* should¦42893 come¦42893 and¦42894 make¦42894 everything that¦42898 Yahweh¦42900 has¦42899 instructed¦42899—
\v 11 the tent¦42904 for the residence and its covering¦42910, its clasps¦42913 and frames¦42916, its bars¦42919, pillars¦42923, and bases¦42926,
\v 12 the box¦42930 with its¦42933 poles¦42933 and the atonement¦42936 platform \add to go on top\add*, the curtain¦42938 for screening¦42939 \add <it\add*,
\v 13 the table¦42943 and its poles¦42946 and all¦42949 its utensils¦42951 and the sacred bread¦42953,
\v 14 the lampstand¦42958 for light with its¦42962 lamps¦42965 and utensils¦42962 and oil¦42967 \add ≈to burn\add*,
\v 15 the incense altar¦42972 and its¦42976 poles¦42976, the anointing¦42979 oil¦42978 and fragrant¦42982 incense, the curtain for¦42987 the entrance¦42987 of¦42987 the residence,
\v 16 the altar¦42992 for the burnt¦42993 offerings¦42993 with its bronze¦42997 grate, its poles¦43003 and¦43004 all¦43006 of its utensils¦43008, the washbasin with its base¦43014,
\v 17 the courtyard curtains with their pillars¦43021 and bases¦43024, and the curtain for the courtyard gate¦43027,
\v 18 the tent¦43033 pegs¦43032 and ropes¦43040 for the residence and the courtyard¦43037,
\v 19 the finely-woven clothing for serving in¦43047 the Holy Place¦43047, the sacred¦43051 clothes for Aharon¦43052 the priest¦43053, and¦43054 the clothes for his sons¦43057 to¦43058 serve¦43058 as¦43058 priests¦43058.”
\s1 The people's offerings
\rem /s1 The People Bring Their Offerings; Offerings for the Tabernacle
\p
\v 20 Then¦43060 all¦43061 the Israelis¦43066 who'd been assembled in front¦43067 of Mosheh¦43068 went¦43060 away,
\v 21 and everyone¦43071 who \add ≈felt to do so\add* came¦43070 \add back\add* bringing¦43083 their contributions¦43086 to Yahweh¦43087 for the work¦43093 on the sacred tent¦43089 and all¦43091 its¦43093 components, and for the sacred clothes¦43094.
\v 22 This included both men¦43098 and women¦43101 who were \add ≈wanting to contribute\add*, and they brought¦43106 necklaces and earrings¦43108 and ornaments¦43110—all made of gold. They held them up high as a raised offering of gold to Yahweh¦43122.
\v 23 \add ≈Many people\add* who had blue or purple¦43132 or scarlet¦43134 yarn, or finely-spun linen¦43135 or goats' hair¦43136, reddened ram skins¦43137 or sea-cow hides¦43137 brought¦43142 \add <them¦43142\add*.
\v 24 \add ≈Others¦43151 who¦43146 could,\add* brought a contribution¦43146 of silver¦43148 or bronze¦43149 to¦43160 Yahweh¦43153, and¦43154 those¦43146 with¦43157 acacia¦43159 wood brought \add <it¦43157\add* for all¦43160 the \add ≈construction of the tent components\add*.
\v 25 All¦43166 the women who were \add ≈skilled¦43169 at¦43172 making cloth\add* brought¦43174 blue, purple¦43181, or scarlet¦43184 yarn and¦43166 fine¦43188, hand-spun linen¦43188 thread,
\v 26 and¦43190 all¦43190 those women¦43192 who \add ≈felt to do so\add* spun¦43198 goats' hair¦43201.
\v 27 The leaders¦43203 brought¦43204 onyx¦43207 stones¦43209 and other gemstones to be mounted on the sacred apron and for¦43212 the sacred pouch,
\v 28 along with the spices¦43216 and \add olive\add* oil to be used for the maps, the anointing¦43222 oil, and the fragrant¦43224 incense¦43223.
\v 29 \add Thus\add* the Israelis¦43248 brought a voluntary offering¦43249 to Yahweh—every man¦43228 and woman¦43229 who \add ≈felt to do so\add* brought something for all the work¦43237 which¦43238 Yahweh had¦43239 instructed¦43239 them through Mosheh¦43244 to do.
\s1 The skills of Bezalel and Oholiab
\rem /s1 Bezalel and Oholiab; Workers to Make the Tent of the Lord's Presence
\r (Exo. 31:1-11)
\p
\v 30 Then Mosheh¦43254 said¦43253 to¦43255 the Israelis¦43258, “Listen, Yahweh¦43261 has¦43260 called¦43260 Bezalel¦43263 by name¦43262 (the son of Hur's¦43269 son Uri¦43266, from the tribe¦43270 of Judah)
\v 31 and he¦43273 has¦43273 filled¦43273 him¦43274 with \add *his\add* spirit¦43275, with skill¦43277 and understanding¦43278, and with knowledge¦43279 and with all¦43280 kinds of craftsmanship.
\v 32 \add He can\add* design¦43284 designs¦43285, work¦43286 in¦43287 gold¦43287, silver¦43288, and¦43284 bronze¦43289,
\v 33 in cutting¦43291 gemstones for setting¦43293, and in wood-carving—to do¦43296 all¦43297 kinds of¦43299 design¦43300 and crafting.
\v 34 \add @Yahweh\add* has¦43303 also \add ≈given him the desire\add* to teach¦43302, along with¦43304 Oholiab¦43306 (Ahisamak's¦43309 son¦43307 from the tribe¦43310 of Dan¦43312).
\v 35 He's¦43315 given them¦43315 \add both\add* skills to do¦43319 all¦43320 the work¦43322 of craftsmen and of skillful¦43325 workmen¦43336 and of embroiderers¦43326 in blue, purple¦43328, and scarlet¦43329 yarn, and in the fine linen¦43331 and of weavers¦43332—\add both\add* as designers¦43338 as well as the various, actual crafts.
\c 36
\nb
\v 1 Bezalel¦43341 and Oholiab¦43368 will do the work¦43362 of constructing all the components of the sacred¦43363 tent¦43363 along with¦43354 any others who are \add ≈like-minded\add* and who have been given the necessary skills¦43346 and understanding¦43353 by Yahweh¦43351.”
\s1 More than enough materials donated
\rem /s1 The People Bring Many Gifts
\p
\v 2 \add ≈So¦43370\add* Mosheh¦43371 summoned Bezalel¦43374 and Oholiab¦43400, as well as anyone else \add ≈who felt to¦43376 help\add* and had¦43387 been given the¦43398 \add appropriate\add* skills¦43383 by Yahweh¦43388,
\v 3 and¦43402 they¦43402 took¦43402 \add away\add* all¦43406 the contributions¦43408 that¦43409 the Israelis¦43412 had¦43410 brought to Mosheh¦43404 to be used for¦43413 the construction of¦43413 the sacred¦43415 tent¦43415. \add ≈However\add* \add @the people¦43411\add* \add still\add* continued to bring voluntary offerings¦43422 every morning.
\v 4 \add ≈So¦43426\add* all¦43427 those skilled¦43429 \add people\add* came \add over\add* to \add start on\add* the work of \add making\add* the sacred¦43435 tent¦43435, Holy¦43435 Place¦43435 came, \add ≈leaving behind\add* the work that¦43440 they¦43442 had been doing¦43443,
\v 5 and¦43445 they¦43445 \add ≈informed\add* Mosheh¦43448, “The¦43461 people¦43451 are¦43450 bringing¦43452 way more than enough to use for the work¦43455 that¦43456 Yahweh¦43459 has¦43458 instructed¦43458 us to do¦43460.”
\p
\v 6 \add ≈So\add* with¦43467 Mosheh's¦43464 \add ≈agreement\add*, they¦43465 spread a message around the camp¦43467 saying¦43468, “\add ≈Please don't¦43471 bring\add* any more contributions¦43477 for the sacred¦43478 tent¦43478.” \add ≈So\add* the people¦43480—men and¦43463 women—stopped bringing¦43481 \add <donations\add*
\v 7 as \add they had already collected\add* more than enough¦43491 materials¦43483 for all the work¦43488.
\s1 The construction of the tent
\rem /s1 Making the Tent of the Lord's Presence; Building the Tabernacle; Construction of the Tabernacle; The Tabernacle
\r (Exo. 26:1-37)
\p
\v 8 Then all the \add ≈most-skilled men\add* out of the workmen made the ten¦43505 \add strips of\add* curtaining¦43506 for the residence from twisted¦43508 finely-spun linen¦43507 and blue, purple¦43510, scarlet¦43512 yarn \add used to embroider\add* winged-creatures on¦43500 them¦43517.
\v 9 Each \add ≈strip\add* was¦43522 14.5m long and two metres¦43524 wide—they were all¦43532 the¦43534 same size¦43530.
\v 10 Five¦43537 \add ≈strips\add* were sewn together as one set, and¦43536 the other five¦43545 as another set.
\v 11 \add #They\add*\f + \fr 36:11 \ft It's singular ‘he’ in the original, which is confusing after v8 mentioned a group of craftsmen. The \+em OET-RV\+em* has chosen to follow v8 (and change the pronoun to ‘they’ in this section), but other translations are divided in how they handle this dilemma.\f* made¦43553 loops¦43554 of blue on¦43561 the edge of the first¦43559 curtain¦43558 at¦43561 the edge of the set, and similarly for the second¦43568 set—
\v 12 fifty¦43575 \add matching\add* loops on¦43573 the edge of¦43578 each set¦43581 so they were¦43583 aligned with¦43573 each other.
\v 13 \add #They\add* \add also\add* made¦43590 fifty¦43591 gold¦43593 clips and¦43590 used them to join the \add two\add* curtain¦43597 \add sets\add* to each other to make \add the wall of\add* the sacred¦43604 tent¦43604.
\p
\v 14 Then¦43620 \add #they\add* made¦43608 eleven goats' hair¦43610 curtain¦43618 sections for the tarpaulin over¦43612 the sacred¦43614 tent¦43614.
\v 15 All eleven \add ≈sections\add* were the same size¦43632 at¦43626 fifteen metres long and¦43627 two metres wide,
\v 16 and they joined¦43638 five¦43641 \add ≈sections\add* to each other \add for one set\add* and six¦43646 for the¦43647 other.
\v 17 Then¦43650 \add #they\add* \add ≈attached\add* fifty¦43652 \add blue\add* loops¦43659 onto one edge¦43663 of each of the two sets
\v 18 and¦43668 made¦43668 fifty¦43671 bronze¦43670 clips for connecting them into one¦43677 \add continuous\add* cover.
\v 19 \add #They\add* then¦43679 made¦43679 a tarpaulin from red rams skins¦43686 to go over the tent¦43681, and¦43685 the top \add waterproof\add* tarpaulin of sea-cow hides¦43682.
\p
\v 20 Then¦43692 \add #they\add* made¦43691 \add forty-eight\add* upright¦43698 frames¦43694 from acacia¦43697 wood for the sacred¦43695 tent¦43695.
\v 21 Each frame¦43703 was¦43704 five metres¦43701 long and¦43705 0.75m wide
\v 22 with two protrusions on each frame¦43713 for connecting them together, so all the frames¦43723 were made in this way.
\v 23 \add #They\add* made¦43726 twenty¦43731 frames for the south¦43734 side¦43733
\v 24 with their forty¦43737 silver¦43740 bases—two bases under each frame¦43744 that two \add more\add* protrusions fitted \add down\add* into,
\v 25 then¦43762 two \add more\add* for the north¦43766 side
\v 26 with their¦43772 forty¦43771 silver¦43773 bases—two bases under each frame.
\v 27 Six¦43789 frames¦43790 were made¦43788 for¦43785 the west side
\v 28 as well as two¦43792 frames¦43793 for¦43795 the back corners¦43795 of¦43795 the sacred¦43796 tent¦43796.
\v 29 At the two¦43815 corners¦43816, \add @the frames\add* were connected together¦43802 at the bottom¦43801, and¦43802 \add similarly\add* at the top before being attached to a single ring¦43810
\v 30 \add ≈so\add* that made eight¦43819 frames¦43820 with their¦43821 sixteen silver¦43822 bases—two bases under¦43830 each frame¦43831.
\p
\v 31 Then¦43834 \add #they\add* made¦43834 crossbars from acacia¦43837 wood—five¦43838 for the frames¦43839 on the \add north\add* side¦43840 of¦43840 the sacred¦43842 tent¦43842,
\v 32 five for the \add ≈south\add* side, and¦43845 five crossbars for the frames¦43854 on the west \add side\add* along the back.
\v 33 The¦43860 middle¦43863 crossbar was made¦43859 to pass¦43864 through¦43864 from \add one\add* end to \add ≈the other\add* in the centre of the frames¦43866,
\v 34 and the frames¦43876 were overlaid with gold. \add #They\add* then¦43874 made¦43882 gold rings¦43881 to be the holders¦43884 for¦43885 the crossbars, and \add *they\add* \add too\add* were overlaid with gold.
\p
\v 35 \add #They\add* made a curtain¦43895 from twisted¦43901 finely-spun linen¦43900, and¦43892 a skillful¦43903 workman embroidered winged creatures on them with blue, purple¦43897, scarlet¦43899 yarn.
\v 36 \add #They\add* made¦43908 four pillars¦43911 of¦43911 acacia¦43912 wood overlaid¦43913 with gold, as well as their¦43915 gold hooks¦43915 and¦43908 four bases¦43920 cast from silver¦43922.
\v 37 \add #They\add* made a curtain for¦43926 the tent¦43927 entrance¦43926—an embroiderer made it from twisted¦43933 finely-spun linen¦43932 and blue, purple¦43929, and scarlet¦43931 yarn,
\v 38 along with it's five pillars¦43939 and their hooks¦43943. The tops¦43945 of the pillars were overlaid¦43944 with gold¦43947, and their five bases¦43948 \add were made of\add* bronze¦43950.
\c 37
\s1 Making the sacred box
\rem /s1 Making the Covenant Box; Making the Ark of the Covenant; The Ark; Building the Ark
\r (Exo. 25:10-22)
\p
\v 1 Then¦43955 Bezalel¦43954 made¦43953 the¦43957 sacred chest¦43957 from acacia¦43959 wood—it was 1.2m long¦43962, 0.75m wide¦43965, and 0.75m high¦43968.
\v 2 He¦43970 overlaid¦43970 \add both\add* the inside¦43973 and the outside¦43974 with pure gold, and made¦43975 a gold moulding¦43977 all¦43979 around¦43979 it,
\v 3 and he¦43981 cast four rings of gold¦43985 \add and attached them\add* to its four feet¦43988—two rings on each one side¦43999.
\v 4 He¦44007 made¦44002 \add two\add* acacia¦44005 wood poles¦44003 and¦44006 overlaid¦44006 them¦44007 with gold¦44008,
\v 5 and¦44010 he¦44010 put the poles¦44013 into the rings¦44014 on¦44014 the sides¦44016 of the box¦44017 for carrying¦44018 it.
\p
\v 6 Then¦44023 he¦44023 made¦44023 the atonement¦44024 platform from pure¦44026 gold¦44025 \add to fit\add* as the lid for the box, \add so\add* it¦44023 was \add also\add* 1.2m long¦44029 and¦44023 0.75m wide¦44032,
\v 7 then¦44034 from¦44041 the ends¦44042 of¦44041 the lid, he hammered¦44038 the gold¦44037 to form two winged creatures.
\v 8 There¦44059 was a winged creature sitting on each end of the atonement¦44057 platform made¦44058 from¦44062 its¦44063 two¦44062 ends¦44063,
\v 9 and¦44066 they¦44066 were¦44066 spreading¦44068 their¦44072 wings¦44069 upwards¦44070 to¦44070 cover¦44071 over¦44073 the platform. They¦44084 faced each¦44077 other, \add ≈looking down towards\add* the atonement¦44083 platform.
\s1 Making the bread table
\rem /s1 Making the Table for the Bread of the Presence; Making the Table for the Bread Offered to God; The Table; Building the Table
\r (Exo. 25:23-30)
\p
\v 10 He¦44090 made¦44089 \add the bread\add* table¦44092 from acacia¦44094 wood—one metre long¦44096, half¦44100 a metre wide¦44098, and three-quarters of a metre high¦44101.
\v 11 It¦44104 was overlaid¦44103 with pure gold and¦44103 had a gold moulding¦44109 all¦44111 around¦44111 it.
\v 12 He¦44113 made¦44113 an 8cm wide rim all¦44117 around it¦44118, and¦44113 a gold¦44121 moulding¦44119 around the rim.
\v 13 He¦44125 moulded four gold rings from gold and¦44125 attached them to the four corners¦44136 of the table. Each ring was close to each table leg
\v 14 near the rim¦44142 to hold the poles¦44146 for¦44146 carrying¦44147 the table¦44150.
\v 15 He made¦44152 \add two\add* acacia¦44157 wood poles¦44155 and¦44158 overlaid¦44158 them¦44159 for gold¦44160—they were for carrying¦44161 the¦44164 table¦44164.
\v 16 He¦44166 \add also\add* made the utensils that¦44171 would be on¦44188 the table¦44174—its plates¦44177 and pans¦44180, and its bowls¦44182 and pitchers¦44185 to be used for pouring¦44187 out \add <the wine offerings\add*—all made from pure¦44190 gold¦44189.
\s1 Making the lampstand
\rem /s1 Building the Lampstand; The Lampstand; Making the Lampstand
\r (Exo. 25:31-40)
\p
\v 17 Then he¦44193 made¦44193 the lampstand from pure¦44198 gold¦44197. The¦44203 base¦44204 and shaft¦44205 of the lampstand were hammered¦44199 \add ≈into shape\add*, along with its cups¦44206, and its buds and blossoms¦44208.
\v 18 There were¦44214 six¦44212 branches¦44213 going¦44214 out¦44214 from the shaft of the lampstand—three on each side.
\v 19 Each branch¦44231 had three¦44235 gold cups¦44236 on¦44231 it that¦44245 looked like almond¦44237 flowers with¦44231 buds and petals. It was the same for each of¦44243 the lampstand's¦44248 six¦44243 branches¦44244.
\v 20 On the lampstand¦44250 \add shaft\add* there were four¦44251 cups¦44252 that also looked like¦44253 almond¦44253 blossoms¦44253, each one with¦44250 flower buds and¦44250 petals¦44255.
\v 21 He made one flower bud beneath every pair of branches \add (that were going out from opposite sides)\add*. The¦44265 bud was¦44268 attached to each pair of branches as if they were growing out of it—all six¦44273 lampstand branches were attached like that.
\v 22 The buds and¦44279 the branches¦44279 coming out of them were¦44281 all¦44282 hammered¦44283 out of one¦44284 piece of pure¦44286 gold¦44285.
\v 23 Then¦44289 he¦44288 made¦44288 the seven¦44292 lamps¦44291 out of pure¦44296 gold¦44295, along with the tongs¦44293 and¦44288 trays¦44294.
\v 24 He¦44301 made¦44301 \add @the lampstand\add* and tongs and trays using 33kg of pure¦44300 gold¦44299.
\s1 Making the incense altar
\rem /s1 Building the Incense Altar; Making the Altar of Incense; Making the Altar for Burning Incense
\r (Exo. 30:1-5)
\p
\v 25 Then he¦44309 made¦44309 the altar¦44312 \add for burning\add* incense¦44313 out of acacia¦44315 wood. It was square¦44320—half a metre on each side—and one metre high¦44322. Its projections \add on the top four corners\add* were part of it.
\v 26 He¦44327 overlaid¦44327 it with pure gold—all four sides¦44336 including¦44334 the projections—and¦44327 put a gold moulding¦44343 all around¦44345 it.
\v 27 Then¦44365 he¦44350 made¦44350 two gold¦44349 rings¦44348 \add and attached them\add* below the moulding¦44355—two on¦44366 each side for holding the poles¦44363 for carrying¦44364 \add @the altar\add*.
\v 28 The¦44371 \add two\add* poles¦44371 were made¦44368 from acacia¦44373 wood and¦44374 then¦44368 overlaid¦44374 with gold¦44376.
\rem /s1 Making the Anointing Oil and the Incense
\rem /r (Exodus 30.22-38)
\v 29 He¦44378 got a \add professional\add* ointment maker to make¦44378 the sacred¦44383 anointing¦44382 oil¦44381 and¦44384 the pure¦44383, fragrant¦44387 incense¦44386.\x + \xo 37:29: \xt Exo 30:22-38.\x*
\c 38
\s1 Making the altar for burning sacrifices
\rem /s1 The Altar of Burnt Offering; Building the Altar of Burnt Offering; Making the Altar for Burning Offerings; Making the Altar of Burnt Offering
\r (Exo. 27:1-8)
\p
\v 1 Then \add @Bezalel\add* made¦44393 the altar¦44396 for burning¦44397 offerings¦44397 from acacia¦44399 wood. It was square¦44407—2.5m on each side—and 1.5m tall.
\v 2 \add ≈From¦44421 the same piece of wood\add*, he made¦44412 horn-like projections for each of the four¦44415 corners¦44416, then¦44412 he overlaid¦44420 it¦44421 all with bronze¦44422.
\v 3 He¦44424 made all the utensils¦44448 for the altar¦44430: the pots¦44433 and shovels¦44436, basins¦44439 and meat-forks, and the firepans¦44445—all made from bronze¦44450.
\v 4 He¦44452 made¦44452 a grating¦44454 for¦44453 the altar—a bronze¦44457 lattice \add that fitted\add* \add ≈down in the middle¦44463 of the altar\add*,
\v 5 and¦44465 he¦44465 cast the grating¦44470 with¦44468 rings¦44467 on¦44468 the four corners¦44469 as holders¦44472 for¦44470 the \add carrying\add* poles¦44473.
\v 6 He¦44475 made¦44475 the¦44478 acacia¦44480 wood poles¦44478 and¦44481 overlaid¦44481 them¦44482 with bronze¦44483
\v 7 and¦44485 he¦44485 put the poles¦44488 into the rings¦44489 on¦44489 the sides¦44491 of¦44491 the altar so they could carry¦44493 it¦44494 with¦44489 them¦44495. He¦44498 made¦44498 \add @the altar\add* hollow¦44496 using boards¦44497.
\s1 Making the washbasin
\rem /s1 Basin for Washing; Building the Washbasin; Making the Bronze Basin
\r (Exo. 30:18)
\p
\v 8 Then he¦44502 made a bronze washbasin and¦44506 base¦44507. \add They were made\add* with¦44509 the bronze from the mirrors¦44509 of the serving¦44510 women¦44510 who¦44511 served¦44512 at¦44509 the entrance¦44513 of the sacred¦44514 tent¦44514.\x + \xo 38:8: \xt Exo 30:18.\x*
\s1 Making the courtyard
\rem /s1 Making the Court of the Tabernacle; The Enclosure for the Tent of the Lord's Presence; The Courtyard; Building the Courtyard
\r (Exo. 27:9-19)
\p
\v 9 Then \add @Bezalel\add* worked on¦44531 the courtyard. For¦44522 the south¦44524 side¦44522, the courtyard curtains¦44526 were¦44528 fifty metres¦44531 of twisted¦44529 finely-spun linen¦44528
\v 10 with their twenty¦44534 \add bronze\add* pillars and their bases¦44535. The¦44538 hooks¦44538 on the¦44539 pillars and their rings were¦44541 made of silver¦44541.
\v 11 On the north¦44544 side¦44543, there were fifty metres¦44546 of curtains with¦44546 their twenty bronze¦44551 pillars¦44553 and bases¦44549, and their silver¦44555 hooks¦44552 and rings.
\v 12 On the west¦44559 side¦44557, there were twenty-five metres¦44562 of curtains¦44560 with¦44562 their ten pillars¦44568 and bases¦44565, and their silver¦44570 hooks¦44567 and rings,
\v 13 and¦44572 \add another\add* twenty-five metres on the¦44572 east side¦44572.
\v 14 The¦44585 curtains for \add one\add* side¦44585 \add of the entrance\add* were 7.5m long with their three pillars¦44586 and¦44588 their bases¦44588.
\v 15 On the \add ≈other\add* side¦44591, opposite the courtyard¦44596 entrance, the curtains¦44597 were¦44598 7.5m long with their three¦44602 pillars¦44601 and¦44591 their bases¦44603.
\v 16 All of the courtyard¦44609 curtains¦44608 were¦44611 made with twisted¦44612 finely-spun linen¦44611.
\v 17 The¦44614 bases¦44614 for¦44615 the pillars were bronze¦44616, \add ≈but¦44614\add* the hooks¦44617 on them and¦44614 their rings were silver. All¦44627 of¦44627 the courtyard¦44629 pillars had decorative silver caps fitted to their tops¦44622.
\v 18 The curtain for the courtyard gate¦44632 was done by an embroiderer. It was made from twisted¦44641 finely-spun linen¦44640 \add embroidered\add* with¦44646 blue, purple¦44637, and scarlet¦44639 yard. The curtain was ten metres¦44648 long and 2.5m high¦44645, just like the other courtyard curtains
\v 19 with its four¦44654 bronze¦44657 pillars¦44653 and their bases¦44655 and their silver hooks¦44658 and rings, and with silver caps.
\v 20 All¦44665 the tent pegs¦44667 for the sacred¦44668 tent and¦44669 for around¦44670 the courtyard¦44669 were¦44671 made with bronze¦44671.
\s1 List of metals used
\rem /s1 The Materials Used; Materials of the Tabernacle; Metals Used in the Tent of the Lord's Presence; Inventory of Materials
\p
\v 21 These¦44674 are the records \add from making\add* the sacred tent¦44676 (where the \add stone\add* transcripts¦44678 were kept). They were recorded¦44680 by the Levites¦44686 according to Mosheh's¦44684 instructions \add and supervised by\add* Ithamar¦44688 (a son¦44689 of Aharon¦44691 the priest¦44692).
\p
\v 22 Bezalel (son of Uri¦44697 son of Hur¦44700, from the¦44701 tribe¦44701 of Judah) made¦44703 everything that¦44707 Yahweh¦44710 had¦44709 instructed¦44709 Mosheh¦44713 \add <to make\add*.
\v 23 Oholiab¦44716 (son¦44717 of Ahisamak¦44719, from the¦44720 tribe¦44720 of Dan¦44722) worked with¦44726 him¦44715—he was an engraver¦44723 and a designer¦44724 and an embroiderer¦44725 in blue, purple¦44727, and scarlet¦44728, and in fine linen¦44730.
\p
\v 24 The¦44735 gold that¦44736 they used¦44736 for¦44737 the construction of¦44739 the sacred¦44740 tent was measured at¦44738 965kg. This was the gold from the wave offering¦44744 and was weighed using the official standard weights.
\v 25 The silver¦44755 contributed by the people was measured at¦44766 3,420kg \add also\add* using the official weights.\x + \xo 38:25-26: \xt Exo 30:11-16.\x*
\v 26 \add During the census,\add* every¦44775 male who¦44776 was twenty¦44781 years old or older paid a 5g silver coin—a total of 603,550 men.\x + \xo 38:26: \xt Mat 17:24.\x*
\v 27 They'd used 3,400kg of silver¦44797 to¦44798 make the bases under the pillars that supported the sacred¦44801 tent's curtains¦44804 (34kg for each of one hundred¦44805 bases)
\v 28 and the other twenty kilograms were used for the hooks¦44820 for the pillars¦44821 and for the caps on the tops¦44823 of¦44823 them¦44825.
\v 29 The¦44827 bronze¦44827 from the¦44828 wave offering¦44828 came to 2,400kg
\v 30 and it was used to make¦44837 the bases¦44841 \add for the pillars\add* at¦44838 the tent¦44843 entrance¦44842 and for the altar¦44846 and its grating¦44850 and all¦44856 the utensils,
\v 31 and for the bases \add for the posts\add* for \add the curtains\add* around the courtyard and at the entrance, and for the tent¦44875 pegs for the \add ≈main structure\add* and around the courtyard.
\c 39
\s1 Making the priests' uniforms
\rem /s1 Making the Vestments for the Priesthood; The Priestly Garments; Clothing for the Priests; Making the Garments for the Priests
\r (Exo. 28:1-14)
\p
\v 1 They used the blue, purple¦44887, and¦44884 scarlet¦44888 \add cloth\add* to make the sacred clothes to be used in¦44895 the sacred tent¦44895. They made Aharon's¦44902 clothes just¦44903 as¦44903 Yahweh¦44905 had¦44904 instructed¦44904 Mosheh¦44908.
\s2 The sacred apron
\rem /s1 The Ephod; Making the Ephod
\p
\v 2 \add @Bezalel\add* made¦44911 the¦44914 sacred apron from twisted¦44921 finely-spun linen¦44920 \add embroidered with\add* gold¦44915 \add wire\add*, and blue, purple¦44917, and scarlet¦44919 yarn.
\v 3 They¦44924 hammered¦44923 thin¦44926 sheets of gold¦44927 and he¦44928 cut¦44928 them¦44928 into thread¦44929 of wire for a skillful¦44941 craftsman to \add ≈embroider\add* into the fine¦44939 linen¦44939 along with¦44933 the blue, purple¦44934, and scarlet¦44936 yarn.
\v 4 They¦44944 made¦44944 joined shoulder pieces for it—joined on two¦44950 corners
\v 5 and the skillfully¦44955 woven¦44955 waistband which¦44957 is attached to the apron was made of the same material of twisted¦44968 finely-spun linen¦44967, and had similar embroidery using gold¦44962 \add wire\add*, and blue, purple¦44964, and scarlet¦44966 yarn, just¦44969 as¦44969 Yahweh¦44971 had instructed¦44970 Mosheh¦44974.
\v 6 They¦44976 cut \add two\add* onyx¦44980 stones¦44979 and¦44976 mounted them in decorative gold¦44983 settings¦44982. The¦44979 names¦44989 of the¦44980 \add twelve\add* sons¦44990 of Yisra'el¦44991 were engraved on¦44987 \add them\add* similar to how a signet ring is engraved,
\v 7 and¦44993 he¦44993 put them¦44994 on¦44995 the shoulder¦44996 pieces of the sacred apron as stones¦44998 of reminder for¦45000 the sons¦45000 of Yisra'el¦45001, as Yahweh¦45004 had¦45003 instructed¦45003 Mosheh¦45007.
\s1 Making the sacred pouch
\rem /s1 Making the Breastpiece; The Breastpiece; Making the Chestpiece
\r (Exo. 28:15-30)
\p
\v 8 Then he¦45010 made the sacred chest pouch which took the work¦45014 of a skilled¦45015 craftsman like¦45016 making the sacred apron. It too was made of twisted¦45024 finely-spun linen¦45023 and embroidered with gold¦45018 \add wire\add*, and blue, purple¦45020, and scarlet¦45022 yarn.
\v 9 When¦45030 the material was folded over double, it¦45027 made¦45029 a square pouch that was 23cm long¦45034 and¦45035 23cm wide¦45036,
\v 10 and they¦45039 attached four¦45042 rows¦45043 of gemstones onto the¦45049 front. The¦45050 first row¦45045 had a \add red\add* ruby¦45046, a \add yellow\add* topaz¦45047, and a \add red\add* garnet.
\v 11 The second¦45053 row¦45052 had a \add green\add* emerald¦45056, a \add blue\add* sapphire¦45055, and¦45056 a diamond¦45056.
\v 12 The third¦45059 row¦45058 had a \add red\add* jacinth¦45060, a \add white\add* agate¦45061, and¦45062 a \add purple\add* amethyst¦45062.
\v 13 The fourth¦45065 row¦45064 had a \add yellow\add* beryl, an onyx¦45067, and¦45068 a \add green\add* jasper¦45068. \add @All the stones\add* were¦45069 mounted in¦45072 decorative gold¦45071 settings.
\v 14 The¦45074 gemstones were¦45075 engraved with the names¦45077 of the twelve sons¦45078 of Yisra'el¦45080 similar to how a signet ring is engraved, and¦45074 they¦45081 represented the twelve tribes¦45095.
\v 15 They¦45097 \add attached two\add* twisted¦45102 chains¦45101 of pure¦45106 gold¦45105 crafted like a cord onto the pouch,
\v 16 and made¦45108 two decorative gold settings¦45110 and two gold rings¦45119, and attached them to the \add ≈upper corners\add* of the sacred pouch.
\v 17 They¦45126 attached one end¦45136 of each gold¦45129 chain to one of the rings¦45133 on¦45134 the \add ≈top corner\add* of the pouch,
\v 18 and¦45139 they attached¦45149 the other end¦45141 of each chain to the two¦45142 decorative settings¦45148 that enclose the stones. Then¦45139 they put¦45144 those on¦45145 the front¦45156 side of the shoulder¦45152 straps of the sacred apron.
\v 19 Then¦45159 they¦45159 made¦45159 two¦45160 \add more\add* gold¦45162 rings¦45161 and¦45159 attached them¦45163 to the \add ≈lower corners\add* of the sacred pouch on¦45169 the inside¦45177 edges next to the sacred apron.
\v 20 They¦45179 made¦45179 two¦45180 more gold¦45182 rings¦45181 and¦45179 attached¦45183 them¦45183 to the lower¦45189 part of the front¦45190 of the shoulder straps near where the shoulder straps join with the sacred apron just above¦45194 the waistband¦45195.
\v 21 They tied¦45198 the rings on the sacred pouch to the rings on the sacred apron with¦45207 a blue cord¦45207, so that the sacred pouch was above the waistband and¦45214 would not come loose from¦45218 the apron. They did these things exactly as¦45220 Yahweh¦45222 had¦45221 instructed¦45221 Mosheh¦45225 \add <to do\add*.
\s1 Making the other clothes
\rem /s1 Additional Clothing for the Priests; Other Priestly Garments; Making the Other Priestly Garments
\r (Exo. 28:31-43)
\p
\v 22 \add @Bezalel\add* had a weaver¦45233 make¦45227 the robe¦45230 \add that would be worn under\add* the sacred apron entirely¦45234 from blue \add material\add*.
\v 23 It opened down the middle¦45240 like other clothes and¦45237 had a border¦45243 around¦45245 this opening so that it¦45247 wouldn't tear¦45247,
\v 24 and the bottom hem had \add decorative\add* pomegranates¦45254 on it that were made¦45249 from blue, purple¦45256, and scarlet¦45258 yarn.
\v 25 They¦45266 \add also\add* made¦45261 \add small\add* bells¦45262 from pure¦45264 gold¦45263 and¦45265 attached them between the pomegranates¦45270 around¦45275 the bottom of¦45273 the robe¦45274,
\v 26 so it alternated between a bell and a pomegranate going around¦45287 the hem, just¦45289 as¦45289 Yahweh¦45291 had¦45290 instructed¦45290 Mosheh¦45294.
\p
\v 27 A weaver¦45303 made¦45297 tunics¦45300 for¦45304 Aaron¦45304 and¦45305 his¦45305 sons¦45305 from fine¦45301 linen¦45301,
\v 28 along with the turban¦45308 and¦45307 caps from fine linen, the underwear¦45317 made from twisted¦45320 finely-spun linen,
\v 29 and the waistband from fine¦45325 linen¦45325 with blue, purple¦45328, and scarlet¦45330 embroidery, just¦45333 as¦45333 Yahweh¦45335 had¦45334 instructed¦45334 Mosheh¦45338.
\p
\v 30 They¦45341 made¦45341 a \add decorative\add* plate¦45344 (to represent a holy crown¦45345) from pure¦45347 gold¦45348 and¦45350 they¦45341 engraved¦45353 ‘Dedicated to Yahweh¦45356’ on¦45351 it¦45351 (like you'd do for¦45356 a signet ring).
\v 31 They¦45370 put¦45358 a blue cord¦45360 on¦45359 it to attach it to the front of the turban¦45365, just¦45367 as¦45367 Yahweh¦45369 had¦45368 instructed¦45368 Mosheh¦45372.
\s1 Mosheh inspects the finished components
\rem /s1 The Completion of the Work; Moses Inspects the Work; The Work Completed; Moses Inspects the Tabernacle
\r (Exo. 35:10-19)
\p
\v 32 And¦45389 \add so\add* all¦45376 the components of¦45378 the residence, the tent¦45379 for meeting¦45381 \add <God\add*, were finished¦45375—the Israelis¦45384 had done just what Yahweh¦45388 had instructed¦45387 Mosheh¦45391.
\v 33 \add @The craftsmen\add* brought¦45396 the \add disassembled\add* residence to¦45400 Mosheh¦45402—the tent¦45399 and all¦45408 its equipment, the clips, the frames¦45412 with their bars¦45413, and the pillars¦45415 with their bases¦45416,
\v 34 the reddened rams¦45422 hide tarpaulin and¦45418 the top sea-cow hide tarpaulin, the curtain¦45430 for covering¦45426 \add <the most sacred place\add*,
\v 35 the box¦45435 for the transcript¦45436 slabs and its¦45439 poles¦45439, the atonement¦45441 platform,
\v 36 the table¦45445 with all¦45448 its¦45450 utensils¦45450 and¦45451 the sacred bread¦45452 to present¦45453 to God,
\v 37 the pure lampstand with its row of lamps and all¦45466 its utensils¦45468 and the lamp oil¦45470,
\v 38 the gold¦45475 \add incense\add* altar¦45474, the anointing¦45478 oil¦45477 and the fragrant¦45481 incense, and the curtain for the tent¦45485 entrance¦45484,
\v 39 the \add large\add* bronze altar¦45489 with its grating¦45493, its poles¦45500, and¦45501 all¦45503 its utensils¦45505, the washbasin with its base¦45511,
\v 40 the courtyard curtains with their pillars¦45518 and bases¦45521, the courtyard gate¦45525 curtain, \add all the\add* ropes¦45529 and tent pegs¦45530 and all the items to service¦45535 the residence as the tent of¦45537 meeting¦45538,
\v 41 and the finely-woven clothes for serving in¦45545 the holy place¦45545, the sacred¦45549 clothes for Aharon¦45550 the priest¦45551, and the clothes for his sons¦45555 to¦45556 \add serve as\add* priests¦45556.
\p
\v 42 The Israelis¦45569 had done¦45567 all¦45571 the¦45573 work¦45573 just as Yahweh¦45562 had instructed¦45561 Mosheh¦45565.
\v 43 Mosheh \add ≈inspected\add* all¦45579 the¦45581 work¦45581, and wow, they¦45583 had done¦45583 it¦45584 just¦45585 as¦45585 Yahweh¦45587 had instructed¦45586 \add <them\add*, and Mosheh blessed¦45590 them.
\c 40
\s1 Erecting the sacred tent
\rem /s1 Setting Up the Tabernacle; The Tabernacle Erected and Its Equipment Installed; The Tabernacle Completed; Setting Up and Dedicating the Tent of the Lord's Presence
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh¦45596 told Mosheh¦45599,
\v 2 “On¦45609 the first day¦45602 of the first month¦45607, you¦45608 should set¦45608 up the tent¦45611 \add ≈where we'll meet¦45613\add*.
\v 3 Put¦45615 the box¦45618 for the transcript¦45619 slabs in its place and¦45620 \add hang\add* the curtain¦45626 to screen¦45620 it \add from sight\add*.
\v 4 Then¦45628 bring in the \add bread\add* table¦45631 and arrange¦45632 its¦45635 utensils, and bring in the lampstand¦45639 and set up¦45640 the lamps¦45643,
\v 5 and put¦45654 the gold¦45649 incense¦45650 altar¦45648 in¦45651 front¦45651 of the sacred box¦45652 and set up the entrance¦45658 curtain for the tent¦45659.
\v 6 Put¦45662 the altar¦45663 for burning¦45664 sacrifices in front¦45665 of the entrance¦45666 to the sacred¦45667 tent¦45667 \add ≈where we'll meet¦45670\add*.
\v 7 Put¦45673 the washbasin between¦45676 the sacred¦45678 tent¦45678 and that altar¦45681, and fill it with water¦45684.
\v 8 \add ≈Set-up the curtains around¦45690 the outside to make the courtyard, and¦45691 also set up the courtyard's¦45696 entrance curtain\add*.
\p
\v 9 Then¦45698 take¦45698 the \add sacred\add* anointing¦45702 oil¦45701 and \add ≈smear\add* it on the sacred tent¦45706 and everything that's¦45711 in¦45713 it, then¦45698 \add ≈none of¦45709 those things¦45722 should be touched by others\add*—it will¦45714 \add all\add* be sacred.
\v 10 \add ≈Also¦45724\add* anoint¦45724 the altar for burnt¦45728 offerings¦45728 and¦45724 all¦45731 its¦45733 utensils¦45733. After that, the altar \add ≈shouldn't be touched by others\add*—it¦45738 will be \em very\em* sacred¦45740.
\v 11 Anoint¦45743 the¦45746 washbasin and its¦45749 base¦45749, and then¦45743 \add ≈prohibit others from using\add* it¦45751.
\p
\v 12 \add ≈Now\add* bring¦45753 Aharon¦45756 and his¦45759 sons¦45759 to¦45760 the entrance¦45762 of¦45762 the sacred¦45763 tent¦45763 and wash¦45765 them¦45766 with¦45767 water¦45767.
\v 13 Then¦45769 put the sacred garments¦45774 on Aharon¦45772 and anoint¦45776 him and make him sacred, so he¦45780 can \add now\add* serve¦45780 as¦45780 a priest¦45780 to¦45781 me¦45781.
\v 14 \add ≈Also¦45783\add* bring¦45786 his¦45785 sons¦45785 and¦45787 dress them¦45788 in tunics¦45789
\v 15 and anoint¦45791 them like you¦45794 anointed¦45794 their father¦45797, and they¦45798 will serve¦45798 as¦45798 priests¦45798 to me¦45799. Their anointing¦45803 will cause them to be a priesthood¦45804 forever¦45805 throughout¦45806 their generations¦45806.”
\p
\v 16 \add ≈So¦45814\add* Mosheh¦45809 did¦45816 \add everything\add* that¦45811 Yahweh¦45813 had¦45812 instructed¦45812 him¦45814 \add <to¦45810 do\add*.
\v 17 It¦45819 was¦45819 the first day¦45824 of the first month¦45825 of \add their\add* second¦45823 year¦45822 when they set¦45826 up¦45826 the sacred¦45827 tent¦45827.
\v 18 Mosheh¦45830 set¦45829 up the residence by setting out its bases¦45837 and standing up the frames¦45841 with their bars¦45845, and the pillars¦45849.
\v 19 He¦45870 spread¦45851 the \add ≈two tarpaulin layers\add* over¦45855 the tent¦45857 just¦45865 as¦45865 Yahweh¦45867 had¦45866 instructed¦45866 \add *him¦45863\add*.
\v 20 Then he took¦45873 the \add two stone slabs with the\add* transcript¦45877 and put \add #them\add* into the box. He¦45881 put the poles¦45884 \add into the rings\add* on the box and fitted the atonement¦45891 platform on the top of the box \add as a lid\add*,
